hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"9076","From Ibrahim, from Malik ibn Abi Hamzah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us on the day of Badr: Whoever sees the polytheists overwhelming you, shoot them with arrows, and do not draw the swords until they press upon you; meaning: They press upon you."
"9077","From Ibn Jurayj, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions one day while facing the enemy: Let none of you fight. Then a man of them rushed and shot the enemy and fought them, so they killed him. Then it was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): So-and-so was martyred. Then he said: After I forbade fighting? They said: Yes. He said: No disobedient one shall enter Paradise."
"9078","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, I heard him saying: Food is not taken in enemy land except with the permission of the commander. Al-Zuhri said: Then if the commander permits him and he takes of it something and sells it for gold or silver, then therein is the fifth."
"9079","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about taking food in enemy land, he said: They used to permit them food and fodder, so long as they did not tie up wealth with it."
"9080","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Awn, from Khalid ibn al-Durayk, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Fudalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, he said: These want to turn me away from my religion; and no, by Allah, I shall die upon my religion. Whatever of it is sold for gold or silver, of food or other than it, then therein is Allah's fifth and the shares of the Muslims."
"9081","From Abu Jafar, from Rabi ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliyah, that Salman was brought a basket in which were bread and cheese - meaning: And wealth. He said: Then he lifted the wealth and ate the bread and cheese. From al-Thawri, from Isa, from al-Rabi, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Salman, the like of it."
"9082","From Sufyan, he said: It used to be said: When they are in enemy land, they eat; then when they bring it to the land of the Muslims, they hand it to the commander, and they do not sell it in enemy land. Then if they sell it for gold or silver, then therein is the fifth."
"9083","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Sulayman ibn Musa: A man carries food to his family? He said: There is no harm in that."
"9084","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from a man, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: Food was not subjected to the fifth on the day of Khaybar."
"9085","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Kahmas, he said: I said to al-Hasan: What did you use to get on the road? He said: Straw and firewood. He said: I said: A man passes by fruits? He said: He eats and does not carry."
"9086","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, he said: Food does not remain in enemy land, nor is the commander asked permission about it; whoever gets to it first takes it, unless the commander forbids it, then it is left by his forbidding. Then if he sells any of the food for silver or gold, it is not lawful for him; it is then of the spoils. He said: This is the sunnah and the truth with us."
"9087","From Ibrahim, he said: Said ibn Ishaq informed me, from Khalid ibn Abi Umar, that he asked Ibn al-Musayyib and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about what the detachment finds in the silos of the Romans. He said: As for gold and silver and clothes and food, it is cast into the spoils. As for whatever is of food, even if abundant - oil, or clarified butter, or honey - then it belongs to that detachment, not the army; they eat and gift, and do not sell."
"9088","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Kahmas, that he said to al-Hasan: Should a man charge the enemy or be in the rank? He said: Rather be in the rank; then when they rise, rise with them. He said: And al-Hasan said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: Be in the rank; then when the Muslims charge, charge with them."
"9089","Salih ibn Muhammad informed us, from Makhul, from Abu al-Darda, that he was asked about what the detachment gets of the food of the Romans. He said to them: They eat and bring it back to their families; then if they sell any of it, therein is the fifth; and they are equal in it."
"9090","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Najih, that Mujahid informed him: That a man in the expedition of Khaybar with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the spoils were before him, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give me this for a ball of yarn with which to bind the bone of my leg. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for my share of it, it is yours."
"9091","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: The commander does not gift anything of the spoils except with the permission of its owner, unless he appoints for a guide or a herdsman."
"9092","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Anas was with Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Bakrah in a raid he raided, and they took captives. Then he wanted to give him of the captives before they were divided. Then Anas said: No, but divide and give me from the fifth. Then Ubayd Allah said: No, except from all the spoils. Then Anas refused to accept from him, and Ubayd Allah refused to give him anything from the fifth."
"9093","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Ibn al-Aqmar, or from his father, and from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from al-Aqmar, he said: The cavalry raided in Syria, and the Arabians reached the same day, and the Kwadin reached by mid-morning the next day. Then al-Mundhir ibn Abi Hamsah al-Hamdani, while in command over the people, said: I shall not make the share of him who reached like him who did not reach. Then he wrote of that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar wrote: May his mother be bereaved of the Wadii; you have understood it - carry it out upon what he said."
"9094","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, that he heard Makhul saying: There is no share except for two horses, even if he has a hundred horses with him."
"9095","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no share except for two horses; and when a man has horses with him, then for two horses there are four shares, and for the man one share. And the shares of horses and hinnies are equal."
"9096","From a shaykh of the people of Syria, that he heard Makhul raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no share of horses except for two, even if he has a thousand horses with him, when he enters with them the land of the enemy. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided on the day of Badr: for the horseman two shares, and for the footman one share."
"9097","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Hani ibn Hani, he said: He was allotted, under the command of Said ibn Uthman, for two horses: for them four shares, and for him one share."
"9098","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, that horses and hinnies are equal; I think he raised it to the Prophet."
"9099","From Mamar, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, I think from Makhul, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made for the Arabian horse two shares, and for its rider one share, on the day of Khaybar. Yazid said: Then I narrated this hadith to Muawiyah ibn Hisham, and he accepted it."
"9100","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made for the horseman two shares, and for the footman one share."
"9101","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: If a man enters enemy land with horses, there are for every horse two shares. I said: Even if he fights the enemy upon them? He said: Yes. Adraba means: He entered with them the land of the enemy."
"9102","From Mamar, he said: It reached me that he made for the Muqrif horse one share, and for the foot soldiers one share."
"9103","From Ibn Jurayj, from Salih ibn Kaysan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided for thirty-six horses on the day of al-Nadir: for every horse two shares. And he divided on the day of Khaybar for two hundred horses: for every horse two shares. I said: Even if he fought."
"9104","From Ibrahim, he said: Salih ibn Muhammad informed me, from Makhul: That al-Zubayr attended Khaybar with two horses, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him five shares."
"9105","From Abd al-Quddus, he said: al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Musa wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab that there was death and hardship among the Arabian horses, then after them came things not reaching the rank of the Arabians - hinnies and their likes. So I would like you to give your view about them. Then Umar wrote to him: That there be allotted for the Arabian horse two shares, and for the Muqrif one share, and for the mule one share."
"9106","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Uthman ibn Yazid informed me, he said: It is acted upon among us, and he raises it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when a child is born to a man after he leaves the land of the Muslims and the land of truce, then that newborn has a share. He said: And they named the man for whose child the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled."
"9107","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Uthman ibn Yazid informed me, he said: It is acted upon among us, and they raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: When a man dies after he enters the land of the enemy, and leaves the land of the Muslims and the land of truce, then his share is for his family."
"9108","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Shihab saying: Jews used to raid with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would allot them like the shares of the Muslims. From al-Thawri, he said: Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir informed me, from al-Zuhri, the like of it."
"9109","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: I asked him about the polytheists raiding with the Muslims: What is for them with the Muslims? He said to them: What they agreed upon - what was said to you: Such-and-such, then it is theirs."
"9110","From Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, that Makhul narrated to him, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah al-Fihri, he said: I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He granted extra of the third."
"9111","From Mamar, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, that Habib ibn Maslamah - and he was ill - used to grant the detachments, at the outset, the third after the fifth."
"9112","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted extra of the third after the fifth."
"9113","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa narrated to me, from Makhul, from Abu Umamah, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to grant extra, at the outset, the fourth, and when returning, the third."
"9114","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: We were in a detachment, and our shares reached eleven camels for every man of us. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted us extra after that, a camel each."
"9115","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment toward Najd, and I was among them. Then we took many camels, and our shares reached eleven camels for every man of us. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted us extra after that, a camel each for every person."
"9116","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, he said: The people used to be granted extra of more than the third, until when Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz came, he wrote that it has not reached us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted extra of more than the third. Then it continued to be acted upon after."
"9117","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: When the detachment goes out with the permission of the commander, whatever they get, the leader takes the fifth of it, and what remains is for that detachment. And when they go out without his permission, the leader takes the fifth of it, and what remains is among the whole army."
"9118","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I said: The leader sends a detachment and they get spoils? He said: If the leader wishes, he takes the fifth of it, and if he wishes, he grants them all of it as extra."
"9119","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: The army shares with the detachments, and the detachments share with the army."
"9120","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Dawud ibn Abi Asim, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: There is no extra grant in the spoils of the Muslims except in the fifth of the fifth."
"9121","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: They used to grant extra only from the fifth."
"9122","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas, that a commander among the commanders wanted to grant him extra before it was fifthed, and he refused to accept it until it was fifthed."
"9123","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Khalid ibn Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to grant extra only from the fifth."
"9124","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said to me: There is no extra grant until the fifth is divided, and no extra grant until the first of the spoils is divided in the Book of Allah, the Exalted, among the believers."
"9125","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, he said: There is no extra grant except in a known specific item of gold or silver."
"9126","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, he said: There is no extra grant in the first thing taken of the spoils. He said: That is known; it was acted upon in what passed until today."
"9127","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whatever the polytheists secured, then the Muslims take it, it is theirs, so long as it is not a free person or a treaty-bound one who is returned to his owner. From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, the like of the saying of al-Zuhri."
"9128","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Goods the enemy takes from the Muslims, then Allah returns them to them? He said: If no precedent has gone in it, returning it to him is dearer, so long as it is not divided; then if it is divided, he has nothing."
"9129","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: We heard that whatever the enemy secured belongs to the Muslims; they divide it."
"9130","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi, the freedman of Ibn Umar, claiming that the enemy took the horse of Abd Allah ibn Umar. Then when the enemy was defeated, Khalid ibn al-Walid found his horse and returned it to Abd Allah ibn Umar."
"9131","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: A slave of mine fled on the day of al-Yarmuk, then the Muslims prevailed over him and returned him to me."
"9132","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whatever is recognized before it is divided, then he returns it to its family; and whatever is not recognized until the arrows go through it, they do not return it."
"9133","From Mamar, he said: It reached me from Qatadah - and I do not know, perhaps I heard it from him - that Ali said: It is the fay of the Muslims; it is not returned."
"9134","From Mamar, he said: I heard some people of Kufah saying: It is returned if recognized before the division or after it."
"9135","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Hakam, he said: The Muslim returns to his brother."
"9136","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Tamim ibn Tarafa: the enemy seized a she-camel belonging to a Muslim man, then a Muslim man bought it from the enemy, and its owner recognized it and established proof over it, so the two disputed before the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled that the price for which he bought it from the enemy be paid to him, otherwise it is left to the buyer."
"9137","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Makhul narrated to us that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: whatever of Muslim property the polytheists take, then the Muslims recover afterwards, if its owner reaches it before the Muslims cast lots over it, he has most right to it, and if the lots of the Muslims have fallen upon it, there is no way to it except by its value."
"9138","He said: I heard Hisham narrating from Muhammad that two men sought judgment from Shurayh over a slave-girl taken captive from the Muslims, then a man bought her from the enemy, so Shurayh said: the most deserving to restore to the Muslim is his brother; the other said: she has become pregnant by me; so Shurayh said: free her; a ruling of the commander, meaning Umar ibn al-Khattab; from Mamar, from a man, from Ibn Sirin, the like of it."
"9139","From Uthman ibn Matar and Ibn Uyayna, from Said, from Qatada: a mukatab was captured by the enemy, then a man bought him, so Bakr ibn Qirwash asked Ali about him; Ali, peace be upon him, said: speak on it, O Bakr ibn Qirwash? He said: Allah knows best; so Ali said: I am the servant of Allah and the cousin of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: if his master ransoms him, he remains on the rest of his contract, and if his master refuses to ransom him, he belongs to the one who bought him."
"9140","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: when the enemy takes anything of Muslim goods, it belongs to its owner as long as it is not divided, but if they divide it, its owner has most right to its price."
"9141","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, he said: Ibrahim was asked about dhimmis whom the enemy captures, then the Muslims recover them; he said: they are not to be enslaved."
"9142","From al-Thawri, about a man who finds his merchandise in the hand of a man who says: I bought it from the enemy? He said: if he bought it with proof, its owner takes it for the price, and if he establishes proof of the purchase but the price is not known, the word is the word of the buyer."
"9143","From al-Thawri, he said: about the polytheist who takes anything of Muslim goods then sells it before securing it to the land of shirk: his sale is void; its owner takes it wherever he finds it."
"9144","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: free women whom the enemy captures, then a man buys them, may he have intercourse with them? He said: he may not enslave them, but he surrenders them to themselves for what he paid for them, with no addition charged to them; he said: Abd al-Karim said about it: if she is of the dhimmis, likewise also; from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, about the freeman whom the enemy captures then the Muslims buy him: the like of his word about the women; and Amr ibn Dinar said the like of that."
"9145","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked about the Muslim whom the enemy captures and he kills a Muslim there, then the Muslims capture him afterwards, or he commits adultery there? He said: I see nothing upon him for what he did there."
"9146","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: one of the people of knowledge told me that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: no commander of an army nor commander of a detachment shall flog a Muslim man until he comes back past the pass, for I fear zeal may carry him to join the polytheists."
"9147","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from his father, he said: Shurahbil ibn al-Simt was over an army, and he said to his army: you have settled a land of many women and drink, meaning wine; whoever of you incurs a hadd, let him come to us so we purify him; so people came to him; that reached Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he wrote to him: it is you, may you have no mother, who orders people to tear the cover of Allah with which He covered them."
"9148","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, he said: the commander of the army, al-Walid ibn Uqba, took drink and became drunk, so the people said to Abu Masud and Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman: carry out the hadd upon him; they said: we shall not do it while facing the enemy, and we dislike that they know, lest it be boldness from them against us and weakness in us."
"9149","From a man, that he heard Abu Bakr al-Hudhali, that he heard al-Hasan say: a Muslim man stole a horse and entered the land of Rome, then returned with the Muslims with it, and they wanted to cut him, so Ali ibn Abi Talib said: do not cut until he leaves the land of Rome."
"9150","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: He said: Whatever you cut of lina or leave standing; and Amr ibn Dinar said it; Ibn Jurayj said: and Mujahid said: of lina means the palm tree; some of the Emigrants forbade others cutting palms, saying: it is only among the spoils of the Muslims; so the Quran came confirming who forbade cutting it and absolving who cut it of sin; its cutting and leaving are only by His permission."
"9151","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said told me that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq sent armies to Sham and sent commanders, then sent Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, and said to him while walking: either you ride or I dismount; Abu Bakr, may the pleasure of Allah be upon him, said: I am not a rider, nor are you one dismounting; I seek reward for my steps in the path of Allah; and Yazid that day was over a fourth of the fourths; he said: you will find people claiming they confined themselves for Allah, so leave them and what they claim they confined themselves for, and you will find people who bared the middle of their heads of hair, leaving of it like headbands, so strike with the sword what they bared; and I counsel you with ten: do not kill a woman, nor a child, nor an old man, nor hamstring a palm, nor burn it, nor be cowardly, nor steal from the spoils; those who bared their heads are the shammasis, and those who confined themselves are those in the monasteries; from al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, that Abu Bakr bade farewell to Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, then mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj."
"9152","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Bakr, when he sent his armies to Sham, said: you will find people who bared their heads with swords, and will find people who confined themselves in monasteries, so leave them with their sins; from Mamar, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, that Abu Bakr sent Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, then mentioned the like of the hadith of Mamar from al-Zuhri."
"9153","From Mamar, from Ayyub, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade killing servants and hired laborers, and al-asif is the hired man; from al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, the like of it, adding: and of it Hassan ibn Thabit says: How light upon the chiefs of Banu Luayy was a spreading blaze at al-Buwayra."
"9154","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade hamstringing trees, for it is a refuge for beasts in drought."
"9155","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: al-Muraqqa ibn Sayfi told us, from Hanzala the scribe, he said: we raided with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and we passed a slain woman of comely form with people crowded over her, so they made way for the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: she was not one to fight; then he said: go and catch up with Khalid and say to him: do not kill offspring nor a hired servant."
"9156","From al-Thawri, from Abu Fazara, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amra, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed on the day of Hunayn by a slain woman and said: did I not forbid this? A man said: I had her riding behind me and she wanted to kill me, so I killed her; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered her burial."
"9157","From Hushaym, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade killing women and children, except whoever of them attacks with the sword."
"9158","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas, he said: al-Saab ibn Juthama narrated to me, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we strike among the offspring of the polytheists in night raids; he said: they are of them."
"9159","He said: and Ibn Kab ibn Malik told me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he sent against Ibn Abi al-Huqayq, forbade at that time killing women and children."
"9160","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a detachment to Khaybar, and the killing reached the offspring, and that reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he said: what carries you to kill offspring? They said: are they not children of the polytheists? He said: are not your best ones children of the polytheists? He said: then he addressed us and said: indeed every newborn is born upon the fitra until his tongue expresses from him."
"9161","From Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, from one who narrated to him, from Habib ibn Maslama that he made a night raid upon an enemy at night."
"9162","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik: Kab ibn al-Ashraf used to lampoon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and harm him, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered Sad ibn Muadh to send five men to him; they came to him while he was in the gathering of his people at al-Awali; when he saw them he was alarmed, saying: what brought you? They said: we came to you for a need; he said: let one of you approach and tell me his need; so one of them approached and said: we came to pledge to you coats of mail with us; he said: by Allah, if you do, you have toiled since this man settled among you; he said: so they promised to come to him after the night quieted; they came to him and he rose to them, and his wife said: these men have not come to you at this hour with anything you love; he said: they told me their need; when he drew near them, Abu Abs embraced him and Muhammad ibn Maslama struck him with the sword, piercing his side with his dagger and killing him; when the Jews awoke they went to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: our companion was killed treacherously; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, reminded them of his lampooning him in his poems and harming him; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called them to write an agreement between him and them; he said: I think he said: that writ is with Ali; and al-Zuhri or another said: one who claimed Islam said to Abu Abs: you killed Kab treacherously; so Abu Abs swore: he will never see him able to be killed but he will kill him; and when he saw him he ran after him until the other eluded him."
"9163","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: he disliked killing polytheists in captivity, reciting: Then bind firmly, thereafter either grace or ransom; he said: and I say: then it was abrogated by: Then seize them and kill them wherever you find them; and it came, they claimed, about the Arabs specifically; and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, killed Uqba ibn Abi Muayt on the day of Badr in captivity."
"9164","From Israil ibn Yunus, he said: Abu al-Haytham told me, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, crucified Uqba ibn Abi Muayt to a tree, and he said: apart from Quraysh? He said: yes; he said: then who for the children? He said: the Fire."
"9165","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, that it reached him from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq that he wrote to him about a commander giving such-and-such for him, and he said: kill him; the killing of one polytheist man is dearer to me than such-and-such."
"9166","From Mamar, he said: a Syrian man who used to guard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz told me, he said: I never saw Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz kill a captive ever, except one of the Turks; he said: captives of the Turks were brought, and he ordered them enslaved; a man of those who brought them said: O Commander of the Faithful, had you seen this one killing among the Muslims, your weeping for them would be much; he said: he is yours, so kill him; so he rose to him and killed him."
"9167","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: captives are not killed except in war, to frighten with them."
"9168","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: and Uthman al-Jazari told me, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ransomed the captives of Badr, and the ransom of each of them was four thousand, and he killed Uqba ibn Abi Muayt before the ransom, so Ali ibn Abi Talib rose to him and killed him in captivity; he said: who for the children, O Muhammad? He said: the Fire."
"9169","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: Banu Amir had captured two men of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, captured a man of Thaqif and took a she-camel upon which pilgrims used to race; he passed by him while bound, saying: O Muhammad, O Muhammad; so he turned to him and said: for what am I held, and the racers camel of the pilgrims taken? He said: for the offense of your allies of Banu Amir, and Banu Amir were allies of Thaqif; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed on, and he called him again: O Muhammad; so he answered him, saying: I am a Muslim; he said: had you said that while you controlled your affair, you would have prospered wholly; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed on, and he called again, so he returned to him, saying: feed me, for I am hungry; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: this is your need; so he ordered food for him; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ransomed the man for the two men captured of his companions."
"9170","He said: some people raided a side of Madina, taking a she-camel and also a woman, and carried them to their dwellings; the woman rose in part of the night to their camels, which they rested at their courtyards, and whenever she neared a camel to mount it, it bellowed, until she came to the she-camel of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a docile camel, which did not bellow, until she sat in its rear then shouted to it; the people noticed her and rode seeking her; she vowed while going, with them after her: if Allah saves her upon it, she will slaughter it; she was saved, and when she reached Madina, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was told: this is your she-camel, so-and-so woman came upon it, Allah saved her upon it; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had the woman brought and asked her: what did you do? She told him: I vowed, while they sought me: if Allah saves me upon it, I will slaughter it; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: evil is what you rewarded her with then; there is no fulfilling a vow in disobedience to Allah, nor in what the son of Adam does not own."
"9171","From al-Thawri and Israil, or one of them, from Abu Ishaq, from Haritha ibn Mudarrrib, from Furat ibn Hayyan: he was taken captive, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered his killing, so he said: I am a Muslim; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was told and left him, saying: among you are men I entrust to their faith, among them Furat ibn Hayyan."
"9172","From Uthman al-Thaqafi, and I heard him narrating to Mamar, he said: I was with Mujahid in a raid, and a captive of one of those with us fled, so a man followed him and killed him, and Mujahid reproached him for that."
"9173","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the captives of Badr: none of you shall be killed except by the blow of a man or by ransom."
"9174","From Ibn Uyayna, from Zakariya, from al-Shabi, from Abd Allah ibn Muti, from Muti ibn al-Aswad, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, named him Muti; he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said on the day of the Conquest: no Qurashi shall be killed after today in captivity."
"9175","From Mamar, from Ibn Shihab, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the captives of Badr: had al-Mutim ibn Adi been alive and spoken to me about these filthy ones, I would have left them."
"9176","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took the captives of Badr, among them was Abu Wadaa ibn Sabara al-Sahmi, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: he has a clever son in Makka, al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaa, and he was the first to come with his fathers ransom."
"9177","From Mamar, meaning from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Ubayda, he said: Jibril, peace be upon him, descended upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Badr and said: your Lord gives you choice: if you wish, kill these captives, and if you wish, ransom them, and an equal number of your companions will be killed; so he consulted his companions, and they said: we ransom them and grow strong with them, and Allah honors with martyrdom whom He wills."
"9178","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi say: two things the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did: his permitting the hypocrites, and his taking from the captives."
"9179","From Abbad ibn Kathir, from Layth, he said: I said to Mujahid: it reached me that Ibn Abbas said: captives are not lawful, because Allah, blessed and exalted, said: Thereafter either grace or ransom, until war lays down its burdens; Mujahid said: no heed to this at all; I met the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, all denying this and saying: this is abrogated; it was only in the truce between the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the polytheists; as for today, by the word of Allah exalted: Then kill the polytheists wherever you find them; if they are polytheists of the Arabs, nothing is accepted from them but Islam, and if they refuse they are killed; as for others besides them, when captured the Muslims have choice over them: if they wish they kill, if they wish they spare, and if they wish they ransom, so long as they do not turn from their religion; but if they profess Islam, they are not ransomed."
"9180","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, and Juwaybir from al-Dahhak, about His word: Thereafter either grace or ransom; they said: it was abrogated by: Then kill the polytheists, the verse; and al-Suddi said it."
"9181","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave on the day of Badr to each of his companions the captive he captured, and he ransomed him himself."
"9182","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Muhammad told me that Ikrima ibn Khalid narrated to him that Suhayl ibn Amr bore the ransom of the captives of Badr, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, charged him to tell him what Quraysh intended in its raid, and he ransomed Abu Wadaa for four thousand."
"9183","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: carrying arms to the land of the enemy was disliked; I said: are horses carried to them? He refused that, saying: what they grow strong with in fighting is not carried to them; other than that, no harm; and Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"9184","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz forbade carrying horses to the land of Hind."
"9185","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade traveling with the Quran to the land of the enemy, fearing the enemy may take it; from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it; he said: and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote of it to the garrison towns."
"9186","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid burned people of the apostates, so Umar said to Abu Bakr: do you leave this one who tortures with the torture of Allah? So Abu Bakr said: I do not sheathe a sword Allah unsheathed against the polytheists."
"9187","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrima: Ali killed people who disbelieved after their Islam and burned them with fire, and that reached Ibn Abbas, so he said: had I been, I would have killed them and not burned them, because the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: whoever changes, or said: whoever turns back from his religion, kill him, and do not torture with the torture of Allah, meaning the Fire; he said: the word of Ibn Abbas reached Ali, so he said: woe to Ibn Abbas."
"9188","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from al-Hasan ibn Sad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, he said: we were with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and we passed a village of ants burned, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: it is not fitting for a mortal to torture with the torture of Allah."
"9189","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ubayd ibn Umayr or Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: all flies are in the Fire except the bee, and he used to forbid killing them and burning food. From Mansur, from Ibrahim: he disliked burning the scorpion with fire, because it is mutilation."
"9190","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I thought from Mujahid, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a detachment and said: if you take Habbar ibn al-Aswad, place him between two branches of firewood, then cast fire in it; then he said: glory be to Allah, it is not fitting for anyone to torture with the torture of Allah; if you find him, cut off his hand, then his leg, then cut off his hand, then his leg; he said: that detachment did not catch him, and a move to Madina caught him; he was a reviling man, so he was brought to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and it was said: this is Habbar ibn al-Aswad being reviled, yet he does not revile; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to him walking until he stood over him, and Habbar was a Muslim, so he said to him: revile who reviles you, revile who reviles you."
"9191","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: Hanzala ibn Abd Allah al-Aslami told me that Hamza ibn Amr al-Aslami, the companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent him and a group with him as a detachment against a man of his enemy, saying to them: if you overpower so-and-so, burn him in fire; they set out until, when hidden from him, he called them and sent to them and returned them, saying to them: if you overpower him, kill him and do not burn him with fire, for none tortures with fire but the Lord of the Fire."
"9192","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: Amir al-Shabi told me: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a force against people, ordering them to kill them all if they overpower them; the bearer of glad tidings came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, telling him they attacked them at dawn and began killing them, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kept rejoicing and smiling at what he told him; while so, the man said: a man passed and ran until he climbed a tall huge tree, and we shot him with arrows while in it, then kindled fire and burned the tree; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, grew angry when burning with fire was mentioned to him; the man said: the man fell, and the arrows had already killed him."
"9193","From al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, from Tawus, he said: I heard him say: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, counseled Muadh ibn Jabal when he sent him to Yemen, saying: you will come upon people of the People of the Book, so call them to tawhid; if they affirm that, say: Allah has enjoined upon you five prayers by night and day; if they affirm that, say: Allah has enjoined upon you fasting a month in twelve months; if they affirm that, say: Allah has enjoined upon you zakat in your wealth, taken from your rich; if they affirm that, take from their wealth, avoiding its choicest, and beware the supplication of the wronged, for there is no veil for it from Me."
"9194","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not fight Banu Qurayza until he called them to Islam, and they refused, so he fought them."
"9195","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from a man of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called Banu al-Nadir to give a covenant they would covenant with him, and they refused, so he fought them."
"9196","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid wrote to Mihran ibn Zadahan and another with him he named: to proceed, I call you to Islam; if you refuse, I call you to give the jizya; if you refuse, I have with me people who love fighting as Persia loves drinking wine."
"9197","He told us, he said: Umar ibn Dharr told us, from Yahya ibn Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talha: when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Ali, he sent a man after him, saying: follow Ali and do not leave him behind you, but follow him and take his hand, and say to him: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: halt until he comes to you; he said: so he halted until the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came and said: do not fight a people until you call them. Abd al-Razzaq said: and I heard it myself from Yahya ibn Ishaq."
"9198","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: we used to call the enemy and call."
"9199","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: they already know what they are called to."
"9200","From al-Thawri, from a companion of his, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, never fought a people but he called them."
"9201","From al-Thawri and Mamar, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Burayda al-Aslami, from his father, he said: when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, appointed a commander over an army or detachment, he counseled him in his own self to fear Allah, and good toward the Muslims with him, then said: raid in the name of Allah in the path of Allah, fight whoever disbelieves in Allah, raid and do not betray, nor mutilate, nor steal from the spoils, nor kill a child; when you meet your enemy of the polytheists, call them to three traits: whichever they answer you with, accept from them and hold back from them; call them to Islam, and if they answer, accept from them and hold back from them, then call them to move from their abode to the abode of the Emigrants, telling them theirs is what the Emigrants have and upon them is what is upon the Emigrants; if they refuse to move from their abode to the abode of the Emigrants, tell them they shall be as the Bedouin Muslims, the ruling of Allah that runs over the believers running over them, with nothing for them in the fay and spoils except to strive with the Muslims; if they refuse to enter Islam, ask them to give the jizya, and if they do, accept from them and hold back from them; if they refuse, seek help from Allah and fight them; and if you besiege people of a fortress and they want you to give them the dhimma of Allah and the dhimma of His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, do not give them the dhimma of Allah nor the dhimma of His Prophet, but give them your dhimma and your fathers dhimma and the dhimmas of your companions, for your breaking your dhimma and your fathers dhimma is lighter upon you than breaking the dhimma of Allah and the dhimma of His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; and if you besiege people of a fortress and they want you to bring them down upon the ruling of Allah, do not bring them down upon the ruling of Allah, but bring them down upon your ruling, for you do not know whether you hit the ruling of Allah concerning them or not."
"9202","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: Umar wrote to us while we were at Khaniqin: when you besiege a palace, do not say: come down upon the ruling of Allah and our ruling, but bring them down upon your ruling, then judge among them as you wish; and when a man meets a man and says to him: matras, he has granted him safety, and when he says: do not be dismayed, he has granted him safety, and when he says: do not fear, he has granted him safety, for Allah knows tongues."
"9203","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Habib al-Walid told me: when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent an army, he said: go in the name of Allah and by Allah and in the path of Allah, fighting whoever disbelieves in Allah; I send you upon not stealing from the spoils, nor being cowardly, nor mutilating, nor killing a child, nor burning a church, nor hamstringing a palm; and he sent a man to a man to lie about him in Yemen, saying: burn him; then he said: do not torture with the torture of Allah."
"9204","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, he said: Umar wrote to us while we were at Khaniqin: the crescents, some are greater than some, so when you see the crescent do not break fast until two men testify they saw it yesterday; and when you besiege people of a fortress, do not bring them down upon the ruling of Allah and His Messenger, but bring them down upon your ruling, then judge among them as you wish; and do not say: do not fear, nor be dismayed, nor matras, for Allah knows tongues."
"9205","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, he said: a Syrian man came to Ibn al-Musayyab and said to him: O Abu Muhammad, shall I tell you what we do in our raids? He said: no; he said: then tell me what the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his companions used to do? He said: when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, settled at a town, he called its people to Islam; if they followed him, he mingled them with himself and his companions, and if they refused, he called them to give the jizya; if they gave it, he accepted it from them, and if they refused, he notified them on equal terms; and the lowest of them, when he gave them covenant, they all fulfilled it for him."
"9206","From Fudayl, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: the people of idols are fought for Islam, and the People of the Book are fought for giving the jizya."
"9207","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Khalid ibn al-Walid to Judhayma, and he called them to Islam, but they could not say: we submitted, instead saying: we changed religion, we changed religion; so Khalid set about killing and capturing them, and gave each of us a captive, until one day he ordered each of us to kill his captive; Abd Allah ibn Umar said: I said: by Allah, I shall not kill my captive, nor shall any of my companions kill his captive; so we came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he raised, meaning his hands, saying: O Allah, I disavow to You what Khalid did, twice."
"9208","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Ubayda, from Talha ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Kurayz, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: whichever man calls a polytheist man, pointing to the sky, Allah has granted him safety, for he but settled by the covenant of Allah and His pact."
"9209","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Fudayl al-Raqqashi, he said: a village of the villages of Persia called Shahirta witnessed; we besieged it a month, until one day we hoped to attack them at dawn, and turned from them at midday rest, when a slave of ours stayed behind and they sought safety from him, so he wrote safety to them on an arrow then shot it to them; when we returned to them, they came out in their clothes and laid down their arms; we said: what is your affair? They said: you granted us safety, bringing out to us the arrow with the writ of their safety; we said: he has no power over anything; they said: we do not know your slave from your free, and they had come out with safety; we said: return with safety; they said: we shall never return to it; so we wrote to Umar part of their story, and Umar wrote: the Muslim slave is of the Muslims; his safety is their safety; he said: so we missed what we had been on the verge of of their spoils."
"9210","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha, she said: a woman would indeed grant protection upon the Muslims, saying: he is granted safety."
"9211","From Abu Mashar, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi: Umm Hani came with two men and Ali wanted to kill them, so she came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mentioning that to him, and he said: we have granted protection to what Umm Hani granted."
"9212","From Malik, from Maymun ibn Maysara, from Abu Murra freedman of Aqil, from Umm Hani: she entered upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of the Conquest and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mothers son claims he will kill so-and-so, a man I granted protection; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: we have granted protection to what Umm Hani granted."
"9213","From al-Thawri, from Wail ibn Dawud, from Abd Allah al-Bahi: Zaynab said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi, if near, is a cousin, and if far, is the father of a child, and I have granted him protection; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allowed it."
"9214","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali told him that Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, husband of the daughter of Khadija, was brought to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bound, and Zaynab daughter of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, freed him."
"9215","From Mamar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam freedman of Ibn Abbas: Zaynab daughter of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted protection to her husband Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, carried out her protection."
"9216","From Mamar, he said: I heard al-Amash say: two horsemen met, horses of Daylam and horses of the Arabs, and the horses uncovered, and there was a fallen man between them; so the Arabs came thinking him of them, saying: no harm; but when they crowded over him, he was a man of Daylam; so some of them said: by Allah we granted him safety, and some said: by Allah we did not grant him safety, we only thought him of us; so their view agreed that they had granted him safety."
"9217","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said al-Maqburi, he said: when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed Fajr, Zaynab rose and said: he mentioned my husband who was brought, and I have granted him protection; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: this matter I have no knowledge of, and the lowest of the people grants protection over them."
"9218","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the Muslims are one hand against others, their blood equal, their covenant contracted by their lowest; no Muslim is killed for a disbeliever, nor a covenanted one in his covenant, and their lowest over their farthest, and the traveler over the sitter, and the strong over the weak; he says: about the spoils."
"9219","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab and others: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allowed the protection of Zaynab his daughter."
"9220","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said to us: no share for a slave with the Muslims; he said: and Amr ibn Shuayb told us then: a slave found a buried treasure in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, so Umar took it from him, bought him from it, freed him, gave him wealth from it, and put the rest in the wealth of the Muslims."
"9221","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: it reached us it is said: no slave is entered in a register, nor is zakat taken from him."
"9222","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that Hasan ibn Muhammad told him that one of the Ghifaris, Khalid ibn al-Ghifari, told him: slaves of theirs witnessed Badr, and Umar ibn al-Khattab used to give them three thousand, three thousand each year."
"9223","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Fadala ibn Ubayd: they were with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in a raid, and among us were slaves; so he did not allot to them."
"9224","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said, he said: Najda wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the slave and the woman, are they given of the fifth? He said: they have nothing of the fifth."
"9225","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr told me, from one who told him, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: he used to allot the slave and the woman from the spoils of the people; he said: and I say the word of Ibn Abbas about the slave and the woman present at battle: they have no known share, except to be gifted from the spoils of the people."
"9226","From Ibrahim, from al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Umar, he said: the slave has no portion of the spoils; al-Hajjaj said: and Ata told me, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"9227","From Ibrahim, he said: Muhammad ibn Zayd told me, from Umayr freedman of Abi al-Lahm, he said: I attended Khaybar with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he did not allot to me, but gave me of the mean goods."
"9228","From Mamar, from Qatada and Ismail ibn Umayya: Najda wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the share of kin, and about killing children, and about slaves, were they given anything of the spoils? So Ibn Abbas wrote to him: you wrote to me about the share of kin, for it was ours until our people deprived us; and you wrote about killing children, if you know of them what the companion of Musa knew, else killing them is not lawful to you; and you wrote about slaves whether they were given anything of the spoils, and they were gifted a thing without a share struck for them."
"9229","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, they said: no share for the hired man."
"9230","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, he said: Abu Salama al-Himsi told me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to a poor Emigrant man: will you go out with me, O so-and-so, to raid? He said: yes, promising him; but when departure came he called him and he refused to go out with him; so Abd al-Rahman said to him: did you not promise me? Do you lie to me and break with me? He said: I cannot go out; he said: what prevents you? He said: my dependents and family; he said: what pleases you to go out? He said: three dinars, upon going out with him; so he went out with him; and when they routed the enemy and took spoils, he said to Abd al-Rahman: give me my portion of the spoils; so Abd al-Rahman said: I shall mention your affair to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; so he mentioned it, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: these three dinars are his lot and portion of his raid, of his affair of his world and his hereafter."
"9231","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the rewards, he said: when a man takes it for his debt gaining strength with it, no harm."
"9232","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Umar, he said: the sitter used to grant the raider, but that a man sells his raid, I know not what it is."
"9233","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Shaqiq ibn al-Izar al-Asadi, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about the rewards, and he said: I would not take a bribe except what Allah bribed me; he said: and I asked Ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: leaving it is better, but if you take it spend it in the path of Allah."
"9234","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Ubayd Allah ibn al-Ajam, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about the rewards, and one of every four came out to us, and of every three one, saying: if you put it in hoof or weapon, no harm, but if you put it in a slave or slave-girl or sheep, it is not lasting."
"9235","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: they used to give, dearer to them than to take; this about the reward."
"9236","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, he said: Masruq used to give on behalf of himself when the expedition went out."
"9237","From Kathir ibn Ata al-Janadi, he said: Abd Allah ibn Zubayb al-Junaydi narrated to me, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Abu al-Walid, O Ubada ibn al-Samit, when you see charity concealed, lessened, hiring in raiding, ruins inhabited, inhabited ruined, and a man rubbing with his trust as the camel rubs with the tree, then you and the Hour are as these two; and he pointed with his two fingers, the index and the next."
"9238","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: the battle cry of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Musaylima was: O companions of Surat al-Baqara; from al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, the like of it."
"9239","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard al-Muhallab ibn Abi Sufra say: one who heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, told me, he says: if you are night-attacked tonight, say: Ha Mim, they shall not be aided."
"9240","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: al-Bara ibn Malik brother of Anas dueled the marzuban of al-Zara and killed him, taking his spoils, and his spoils reached thirty thousand; so that reached Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said to Abu Talha: we used not to fifth the spoils, but the spoils of al-Bara reached much wealth, and I see but fifthing it."
"9241","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: al-Bara ibn Malik lay on his back and hummed, so Anas said to him: remember Allah, O my brother; so he sat up and said: O Anas, do you see me dying on my bed, having killed a hundred of the polytheists in duels besides what I shared in killing."
"9242","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Ikrima, he said: a man of Banu Qurayza stood and said: who duels? So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: rise, O Zubayr; so Safiyya said: my only one, O Messenger of Allah? So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: whichever overcomes his companion kills him? So al-Zubayr overcame him and killed him, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave him his spoils."
"9243","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi freedman of Abd Allah ibn Umar say: we never ceased hearing ever, when the Muslims and disbelievers met and a Muslim man killed a disbeliever man, his spoils are his, except in the thick of battle."
"9244","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Layla told me: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not fifth the spoils."
"9245","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Sihr ibn Alqama al-Abdi; Abu Said said: I found in another's book Shibr, and it is correct; he said: we were at al-Qadisiyya, and a man of them came out with arms and bearing, saying: mard, and mard means: man for man; so I offered my companions to duel him, but they refused, and I was a short man; he said: so I went to him, he shouted a cry, I said takbir, he roared, I said takbir, he carried me and struck, and his horse swerved with him; he said: so I took his dagger, leapt upon his chest and slaughtered him; he said: and I took his belt, sword, two banners, shield and two bracelets, valued at twelve thousand; so I brought it to Sad ibn Malik, and he said: come to me in the evening, and come with the spoils; he said: so I went to him in the evening, he stood on the pulpit and said: these are the spoils of Shibr ibn Alqama, take it wholesome and pleasant; so he gave all of it to me."
"9246","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: on the day of Banu Musaylima, al-Bara ibn Malik met a man called the donkey of Yamama, a tall man with a white sword in his hand, while al-Bara was a short man; so al-Bara struck his legs with the sword as if missing him, and he fell on his back; he said: so I took his sword and sheathed my sword."
"9247","From al-Thawri, he said: when you have no weapon but the weapons of the enemy, fight with it, then return it to the spoils."
"9248","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, from Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Abu Muhammad freedman of the Ansar, he said: I heard Abu Qatada say: I dueled a man on the day of Hunayn and killed him, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave me his spoils."
"9249","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrima freedman of Ibn Abbas: a polytheist man reviled the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he said: who suffices me my enemy? So al-Zubayr said: I; so al-Zubayr dueled him and killed him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave him his spoils."
"9250","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: al-Ahwaz was opened with their commander Abu Musa or another, and he called Majzaa or Shaqiq ibn Thawr, Abu Bakr doubted, saying: look for me a man of your people I may send in an expedition; he said: if this matter you want is good, I love none of my people to precede me to it, and if otherwise, I love not to cast any of my people into it, so send me; he said: we were guided to a tunnel entered to the city; he said: so he sent him with people, and I know but he said: over them was al-Bara ibn Malik; he said: so Majzaa or Shaqiq entered the tunnel, and when he came out they threw a rock at him and killed him, and the people entered until many, and Allah opened it for them; he said: we heard he was a youth of twenty."
"9251","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Jafar, he said: Ali followed their way with the fifth."
"9252","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: Ibn Abbas was asked about the share of kin, and said: it was ours, but our people deprived us; so Umar called us, saying: your days are married in it, and your debtor is given in it; so we refused, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, refused."
"9253","From Mamar, from Qatada, about His word: Then to Allah belongs its fifth: five fifths, to the Messenger, kin, orphans, the needy and the wayfarer."
"9254","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim al-Jadali, he said: I asked al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Hanafiyya about the word of Allah exalted: And know that whatever you gain, to Allah belongs its fifth; he said: this is a key of speech, to Allah belong the world and the hereafter, and to the Messenger, and to kin; so they differed after the death of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, over these two shares; one said: the share of kin is for the kin of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and one said: the share of kin is for the kin of the caliph; and the view of the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, agreed to put these two shares in horses and equipment in the path of Allah, and that was in the caliphate of Abu Bakr and Umar; I said to him, he said: he disliked being attributed opposition to them."
"9255","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Saib, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, he said: on the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: whoever kills a slain one has such-and-such, so they killed seventy and captured seventy, and Abu al-Yusr ibn Amr came with two captives, saying: O Messenger of Allah, you promised us whoever kills a slain one has such, and whoever captures a captive has such, and I brought two captives; so Sad ibn Ubada rose, saying: O Messenger of Allah, neither asceticism in the hereafter nor cowardice from the enemy kept us, but we held this stand fearing the polytheists might cut you off, and if you give these, nothing remains for your companions; so these kept saying and these kept saying, and there came down: They ask you about the spoils; say: the spoils are for Allah and the Messenger, so fear Allah and amend what is between you; he said: so they surrendered the spoils to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; he said: then came down: And know that whatever you gain, to Allah belongs its fifth; from Mamar, from Muhammad ibn al-Saib, the like of it."
"9256","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, he said: the share of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was called al-safi; if he wished a slave, if he wished a horse, choosing it before the fifth, striking for him his share, whether present or absent; and Safiyya daughter of Huyayy was of the safi."
"9257","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi Aisha, he said: I heard Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, and I asked: how much was the share of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? He said: it was a fifth of the fifth."
"9258","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr told me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: selling the fifth was disliked until divided; from Mamar, from al-Amash, from Said ibn Jubayr, raising it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like, except he said: the day of Khaybar."
"9259","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade on the day of Khaybar the flesh of domestic donkeys, pregnant women to be approached, selling spoils until divided, and eating every fanged beast of prey; from al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, from Makhul, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"9260","He told us, he said: Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: Abu Uthman ibn Yazid told me: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called for al-faq. [Source text truncated/garbled here; only these words legible.]"
"9261","From Mamar, from Hammam, from Abu Hurayra, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: a prophet of the prophets raided, saying: let none raid with me who married a woman he has not consummated with, nor a man with sheep awaiting their birth, nor a man who built a building not finished; and when he came to the place he wanted at Asr, he said to the sun: you are commanded and I am commanded; O Allah, hold it for me an hour; so Allah held it for him an hour, then Allah opened for him; then the spoils were laid and fire came but did not eat it; so he said: among you is theft from spoils, so let a man of each tribe pledge me; his hand stuck to the hand of two or three men, so he said: among you is the theft; so they brought out like a cow head of gold and cast it into the spoils, then fire came and ate it; he said: so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: it was not lawful to any before us, and that because Allah saw our weakness and made it good for us; and they claimed the sun was never held for any before him nor after him."
"9262","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gained spoils, he sent a crier: let no man steal a thread or less; indeed let no man steal a camel, coming with it on his back on the Day of Resurrection with bellowing; indeed let no man steal a horse, coming with it on the Day of Resurrection on his back with neighing."
"9263","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: Aqil ibn Abi Talib came, and his wife said to him: we know you fought, did you bring us anything? He said: this is a needle, sew your clothes with it; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a crier: indeed let no man steal a needle or less; so Aqil said to his wife: I see your needle is lost to you."
"9264","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, about His word: And know that whatever you gain: he said: the thread of the thing."
"9265","From Mamar, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, he said: one who heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, told me, while at Wadi al-Qura standing upon his horse, and a man of Balqin came to him, saying: your boy was martyred, or said: your freedman so-and-so; he said: rather he is now dragged to the Fire in a cloak he stole from Allah and His Messenger."
"9266","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim: his father told him that while with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on his return from Hunayn, the Bedouins clung asking him, forced to a samura tree, and his cloak was snatched while upon his mount, so he halted, saying: return my cloak to me, do you fear miserliness from me? Had these thorn-trees in number been livestock, I would divide it among you, then you would find neither miserly, nor cowardly, nor lying."
"9267","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Ajlan, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: at the division of the fifth, a man came seeking to make lawful a thread or needle, and he said: return the thread and needle, for theft from spoils is shame, fire and disgrace; he said: then he raised hairs or wool from his camel, saying: I have of what Allah bestowed upon you not even the like of this, except the fifth, and it is returned to you."
"9268","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah accepts no prayer without purification, nor charity from theft of spoils."
"9269","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Muslim al-Khawlani, he said: four in four are not accepted in hajj, nor umra, nor jihad, nor charity: treachery, theft, theft from spoils, and the orphans wealth."
"9270","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said told me that Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban al-Ansari told him that Abu Amra freedman of the Ansar told him that he heard Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani say: we were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Khaybar, and a man of Ashja died, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not pray over him, so they went looking in his goods and found in them beads of Jewish beads not worth two dirhams; he said: and Ibn Uyayna told us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, that Abu Amra told him that he heard Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani narrate the like of it."
"9271","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said told me that Abd Allah ibn al-Mughira ibn Abi Burda said: on the day of Hunayn, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to the tribes in their dwellings, supplicating for them and greeting them, leaving one tribe of those tribes he did not come to, and they searched among them, finding in a mans saddle-cloth a necklace of onyx he had stolen, then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to them and prayed over them as he prays over the dead."
"9272","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: a man over the baggage of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called Karkara died, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: he is in the Fire; so they went looking at him and found upon him a cloak he had stolen."
"9273","From Ibn Jurayj, from Zayd ibn Aslam: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was told about a man who held his beasts head in fighting: so-and-so was martyred; so he said: he is now turning in the Fire; it was said: why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: he stole a wrap on the day of Khaybar; so a man of the people said: O Messenger of Allah, I took two straps on such-and-such day; he said: two straps of Fire."
"9274","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: a man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud, saying: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so stole such-and-such; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: O so-and-so, did you do it? He said: no; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, looked at the man who told him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, dig here; so they dug and brought out a velvet cloth, so they said: O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for him; so he said: leave us from Abu Khar, meaning dung."
"9275","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mutarrif, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, about His word: Is one who follows Allahs pleasure as one who returns with wrath from Allah; he said: whoever steals from spoils."
"9276","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: it was ordered for a man who stole from spoils that his baggage be burned and his portion of the spoils forfeited."
"9277","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, he said: it was ordered for a man who stole that his baggage be ordered out, brought forth and burned; he said: and Amr said, from al-Hasan: and his portion of the spoils is forfeited."
"9278","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, he said: Salih ibn Muhammad told me that he witnessed a man called Ziyad following spoil-theft in the path of Allah in the land of Rome, and Salim ibn Abd Allah, Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and Raja ibn Haywa were asked about him, and all counseled: he be lashed a painful lashing, his goods gathered except animals and burned, then freed in his trousers, giving him his sword only; from Ibn Uyayna, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, he said: his baggage is gathered and burned; from Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, the like of it."
"9279","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: do not wish to meet the enemy, for you know not, perhaps you will be tried with them, and ask Allah well-being; and when they come to you glittering, chanting and shouting, then the ground, the ground, sitting; then say: O Allah our Lord and their Lord, our forelocks and their forelocks are in Your hand, and only You kill them; and when they draw near you, rise to them; and know Paradise is beneath the flashing."
"9280","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqba told me, from Abu al-Nadr, a scribe, from a man of Aslam of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa: he wrote to Umar ibn Ubayd Allah when he marched to the Haruriyya, telling him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in his days when he met the enemy, waited until, when the sun declined, he stood among them, saying: O people, do not wish to meet the enemy, and ask Allah well-being; and if you meet them, be patient, and know Paradise is beneath the shades of swords; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, rose, saying: O Allah, Sender of the Book, Mover of clouds, Defeater of the confederates, defeat them and aid us over them; and he mentioned also that it reached him that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, supplicated in the like of that, saying: O Allah our Lord and their Lord, we are Your servants and they are Your servants, our forelocks and their forelocks are in Your hand, and aid us over them; from al-Thawri, from Abu Hayyan, from an elder of Madina, he said: the scribe of Ubayd Allah ibn Mamar narrated to me, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa wrote to Ubayd Allah ibn Mamar, then mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Abi Awfa, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Abu al-Nadr."
"9281","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Awfa saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: O Allah, Sender down of the Book, Swift in reckoning, Mover of the clouds, Defeater of the confederates, O Allah, defeat them and shake them."
"9282","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met the enemy, he would say: O Allah, You are my support and my helper, and by You I move, and by You I charge, and by You I fight."
"9283","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wish to meet the enemy, and ask Allah for well-being, but when you meet them, stand firm and remember Allah, and if they raise a clamor and shout, then keep to silence."
"9284","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What about fleeing from the battle line? He said: The one who flees, other than one turning aside for fighting or joining a company, is subject to the word of Allah. I said: If a man flees when there is no pitched battle? He said: There is no harm in that; that only applies in the pitched battle."
"9285","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning His word: When you meet those who disbelieve advancing, up to: and an evil destination, he said: They held that this was about the day of Badr; do you not see that He says: And whoever turns his back to them on that day."
"9286","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who said: This was only on the day of Badr, and the Muslims had no company to which they could withdraw."
"9287","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Umar put Abu Ubayd al-Thaqafi in command of an army, and he was killed in the land of Persia along with his army, so Umar said: If they had withdrawn toward me, I would have been a company for them."
"9288","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from more than one person, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to the Muslims: I am your company, so whoever of you withdraws, it is to the armies."
"9289","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Umar said: I am the company of every Muslim."
"9290","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, it having reached him, that Ibn Abbas said: In His word: If there are among you a hundred steadfast, they will overcome two hundred, and if there are among you a thousand, they will overcome two thousand by Allah's permission, ten disbelievers were imposed upon one Muslim; so if one man meets two men and flees, or meets one man and flees, that is a grave sin, but if he meets three and flees from them, there is no harm."
"9291","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, concerning His word: If there are among you twenty steadfast, he said: This was obligatory upon them, that one man should not flee from ten, then Allah lightened it for them. From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Ata, the same."
"9292","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every wound a Muslim is wounded with in the way of Allah will be in its state as when it was inflicted, gushing blood; the color is the color of blood, and the scent is the scent of musk."
"9293","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would burden my ummah, I would not stay behind any expedition fighting in the way of Allah, but I do not find means to carry them, nor do they find means to follow me, nor is it pleasing to their hearts to stay behind after me."
"9294","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of the one who strives in the way of Allah - and Allah knows best who strives in His way - is like one constantly standing and fasting, and Allah has guaranteed for the one who strives in His way that He will cause him to die and admit him to Paradise, or return him safe with whatever reward or booty he obtained."
"9295","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Thalabah, from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is wounded in the way of Allah will come on the Day of Resurrection bleeding, his scent the scent of musk and his color the color of blood."
"9296","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that men among the believers do not like to stay behind me, and I do not find what to carry them on, no expedition fighting in the way of Allah would go out except that I would be with them; by Allah, I would love to be killed in the way of Allah, then brought to life, then killed, then brought to life, then killed."
"9297","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris, who said: Abu al-Darda said: Killing washes away filth, and killing is of two kinds: expiation and rank."
"9298","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, who said: Malik ibn Yukhamir narrated to us that Muadh ibn Jabal narrated to them that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No Muslim man fights in the way of Allah for the time between two milkings of a camel except that Paradise becomes due for him; and whoever sincerely asks Allah for death in battle from his own heart, then dies or is killed, he has the reward of a martyr; and whoever is wounded in the way of Allah or suffers a mishap will come on the Day of Resurrection in his fullest state, its color like saffron and its scent like musk; and whoever goes out in the way of Allah has upon him the seal of the martyrs."
"9299","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, who said: Kathir ibn Murrah narrated to us that Ubadah ibn al-Samit narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no living soul on earth that dies having good with Allah, the Exalted, that would love to return to you, even if it had the world, except the one killed in the way of Allah, for he loves to return and be killed once more."
"9300","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ishaq ibn Rafi, who said: It reached me from a trustworthy person that when the warrior leaves his house, there is counted for him whatever people of the qiblah, people of the dhimmah, and animals he leaves behind, and for each one of them a qirat runs for him every night, like a mountain, or he said, like Uhud."
"9301","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard al-Numan ibn Bashir saying: The likeness of the warrior is like one who fasts perpetually and stands in prayer at night."
"9302","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yazid ibn Shajarah, who said: His words were confirmed by his deeds, and he used to address us saying: Remember Allah's blessing upon you, how fine is the mark of Allah's blessing upon you; if you saw what I see of green and yellow, and what is in the saddlebags; he said: It was said: When people are lined up for fighting, or lined up in prayer, the gates of heaven are opened, and the gates of Paradise, and the gates of Hell, and the houris are adorned and look out; if he advances, they say: O Allah, aid him, and if he turns back, they veil themselves from him and say: O Allah, forgive him; so strike hard at the faces of the people, may my father and mother be ransom for you, and do not disgrace the houris; he said: With the first drop that spills of his blood, Allah expiates for him everything he did; he said: And two houris descend to him, wiping the dust from his face, saying: The time has come for you, and he says: The time has come for you both; then he is clothed in a hundred robes not woven by the children of Adam, but from the plants of Paradise, such that if placed between two fingers they would contain him; he said: And he used to say: I have been told that swords are the keys of Paradise, and when the Day of Resurrection comes it will be said: O so-and-so, this is your light, and O so-and-so son of so-and-so, you have no light."
"9303","From Abd al-Quddus, that he heard Makhul saying: One of the Companions narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fights in the way of Allah for the time between two milkings of a camel, whether killed or dying, enters Paradise; and whoever shoots an arrow that reaches the enemy or falls short, it is equal to freeing a slave; and whoever grows a grey hair in the way of Allah, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever is wounded will come on the Day of Resurrection with its scent like musk and its color like saffron."
"9304","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: When the two lines meet, the houris are sent down to the lowest heaven, and when they see a man and are pleased with his advance, they say: O Allah, make him firm, but if he retreats they veil themselves from him; and if he is killed, they descend to him and wipe the dust from his face, saying: O Allah, soil the one who soiled him, and dust the one who dusted him."
"9305","From Abu Mashar, that he heard Said ibn Abi Said narrating from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The mukatab is aided, and the one who marries is aided, and the warrior is aided, with a guarantee upon Allah for whatever reward or booty he obtains, until he returns to his family, and if he dies he enters Paradise."
"9306","From Mamar, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three are those whose help is a right upon Allah: the warrior in the way of Allah, the one who marries seeking chastity, and the mukatab who intends payment."
"9307","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A morning outing or an evening outing in the way of Allah is better than the world and what is in it, and the standing of one of you in the battle line is better than the worship of a man for sixty years."
"9308","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Amr ibn Abasah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grows a grey hair in the way of Allah, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the way of Allah, it is equal to freeing a slave."
"9309","From Jafar, from Hisham, from Jabalah ibn Atiyyah, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: We were with a reciter who was reciting, and he passed this verse: Allah has favored the mujahidin, up to: and forgiveness and mercy; so he said to the reciter: Stop; it has reached me that there are seventy degrees, between every two degrees seventy years for a swift fine horse."
"9310","From al-Thawri, from Adam ibn Ali al-Shaybani, who said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: A journey in the way of Allah is better than fifty pilgrimages."
"9311","He said: And I heard Ibn Umar saying: People will be called on the Day of Resurrection as the diminished; it was said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, who are the diminished? He said: One of them diminishes his prayer in his ablution and his glances."
"9312","From Mamar, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from the expedition of Tabuk and came in sight of Madinah, he said: There are people in Madinah who did not travel a road nor cross a valley except that they were with you; excuse detained them."
"9313","From Jafar, from Aban, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, who said: Abu Umamah informed me that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grows a grey hair in the way of Allah, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the way of Allah, whether it misses or hits, it is equal to freeing a slave from the children of Ismail."
"9314","From Jafar, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An evening outing or a morning outing in the way of Allah is better than the world and what is in it."
"9315","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: O Allah, I ask You for martyrdom in Your way in the city of Your Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"9316","From al-Thawri, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from Marur ibn Suwayd, who said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: That I die on my bed, patient and seeking reward, is dearer to me than that I advance upon a people I do not want to kill me; or is it not that Allah brings martyrdom while a man is greatly burdened on behalf of his companions, confined to his place."
"9317","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Judan, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Abdullah ibn Jahsh said on the day of Uhud: O Allah, I swear upon You that I meet the enemy, and when I meet the enemy they kill me, then slit my belly, then mutilate me; and when I meet You, You will ask me, and I will say: For what is this? He said: So he met the enemy and was killed, and that was done to him; so Ibn al-Musayyib said: I hope Allah will fulfill the latter part of his oath as He fulfilled the first."
"9318","From Mamar, from Qatadah, who said: It reached us that the souls of the martyrs are in the forms of white birds, eating from the fruits of Paradise; and al-Kalbi said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): In the form of white birds sheltering to lamps hung beneath the Throne."
"9319","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, who said: We asked Abdullah about this verse: And do not think those killed in the way of Allah, up to: are provided for; he said: The souls of the martyrs are with Allah like birds having lamps hung from the Throne, roaming in Paradise wherever they wish; he said: Then your Lord looked upon them a look and said: Do you desire anything so that I increase you? They said: Our Lord, do we not roam in Paradise wherever we wish? Then He looked upon them a third time and said: Do you desire anything so that I increase you? They said: Return our souls to our bodies so that we fight in Your way and are killed another time; he said: So He said nothing further to them."
"9320","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, that he said concerning the third time, when He said: Do you desire anything so that I increase you? He said: Convey peace to our Prophet, and tell him that we are pleased and He is pleased with us."
"9321","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The souls of the martyrs are in the forms of green birds suspended in the lamps of Paradise, which Allah will return on the Day of Resurrection. Mamar said, and al-Kalbi: The souls of the martyrs are in the forms of green birds roaming in Paradise, sheltering to lamps hung beneath the Throne, he mentioned it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"9322","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The souls of the martyrs are transformed into green birds clinging to the fruits of Paradise."
"9323","From Mamar, from Qatadah, who said: It reached us that the souls of the martyrs are in white birds eating from the fruits of Paradise."
"9324","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Bahir ibn Sad, from Khalid ibn Madan, from al-Miqdam ibn Madi Karib al-Kindi, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The martyr has nine traits with Allah - I doubt - Allah forgives his sin at the first shedding of his blood, and he is shown his seat in Paradise, and he is adorned with the ornament of faith, and he is protected from the punishment of the grave, and he is married from the houris, and he is secured from the greatest terror, and a crown of dignity is placed on his head, every ruby of which is better than the world and what is in it, and he is married to seventy-two wives from the houris, and he intercedes for seventy persons among his relatives."
"9325","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: In Paradise there is a house which none shall enter except a prophet, or a truthful one, or a martyr, or a just ruler, or one given choice between killing and disbelief who chooses killing over disbelief."
"9326","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ibn Awn, from Hilal ibn Abi Zaynab, from a man he named, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The martyr was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: The earth does not dry of his blood until his two wives hasten to him, as though they are two roots that lost their young in an open land, each one appearing in a robe better than the world and what is in it."
"9327","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Judan, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They were shown to me in Paradise in a tent of pearl, each of them upon a couch; I saw Zayd and Ibn Rawahah with aversion in their necks, while Jafar was upright with no aversion in him; he said: So I asked, or it was said: When death came upon the two of them, it was as though they turned away, or turned their faces aside, while Jafar did not do so. Ibn Uyaynah said: That is when Ibn Rawahah says: I swear, O soul, you shall descend, with obedience from you, you shall be honored, for long have you been at rest; how sweet is the scent of Paradise, Jafar."
"9328","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by people mentioning a lost expedition, and some of them said: They are their martyrs in Paradise, and some said: They have what they intended; so Umar ibn al-Khattab said: What are you discussing? They said: We are discussing these men; some of us say: They were killed in the way of Allah, and some say: What they intended; so Umar said: Among people are those who fight for show, and among people are those who fight seeking the world, and among people are those who fight when fighting presses them and they find nothing else, and among people are those who fight out of zeal, and among people are those who fight seeking the face of Allah; those are the martyrs; and every soul is raised upon what it dies upon; no soul knows, this man who was killed, it has become clear to him that he has been forgiven what preceded of his sin and what followed."
"9329","From Mamar, who said: Thumamah ibn Abdullah ibn Anas ibn Abdullah ibn Malik informed us that Haram ibn Milhan, the uncle of Anas ibn Malik, when he was stabbed on the day of Bir Maunah, took some of his blood in his hand and sprinkled it over his face and head, saying: I have won, by the Lord of the Kabah; I have won, by the Lord of the Kabah."
"9330","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, who said: A man came to Abu Musa al-Ashari, with Hudhayfah present, and said: Do you see a man who takes his sword and fights with it until he is killed - is Paradise his? Al-Ashari said: Yes. Hudhayfah said: Question the man and make him understand. He said: What did you say? So he repeated to him the same as his first saying, and Abu Musa said to him the same as his first saying. Hudhayfah also said: Question the man and make him understand. He said: What did you say? So he repeated to him the same, and he said: I have nothing but this. Hudhayfah said: Whoever does this such-and-such shall surely enter Hell, but whoever strikes with his sword in the way of Allah hitting the truth, Paradise is his. Abu Musa said: He spoke the truth."
"9331","From Ibrahim, from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salih, from Abdullah ibn Nawfal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: One who dies in the way of Allah is a martyr."
"9332","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abu Musa, who said: They said: O Messenger of Allah, a man fights out of zeal, and a man fights out of bravery; which of that is in the way of Allah? He said: Whoever fights so that the word of Allah is supreme, he is in the way of Allah."
"9333","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The martyr is only the one who, if he died on his bed, would enter Paradise, meaning the one who dies on his bed with no sin upon him."
"9334","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, who said: It is specific to the martyr."
"9335","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, who said: Every believer is a martyr; then he recited: And those who believe in Allah and His messengers, those are the truthful and the martyrs."
"9336","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: That I swear nine times that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was killed is dearer to me than that I swear once that he was not; for Allah made him a prophet and took him as a martyr. Al-Amash said: So I mentioned it to Ibrahim, and he said: They held that the Jews poisoned him and Abu Bakr."
"9337","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Ibn Masud, who said: Whoever falls from mountain tops, and wild beasts eat him, and drowns in the sea, is a martyr with Allah."
"9338","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: We were present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar, or he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at Khaybar, he said of a man among those with him who professed Islam: This one is among the people of Hell. When fighting came, he fought and was wounded, and it was said: He has died; he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was said: The man of whom you said he is among the people of Hell fought hard today and has died. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: To Hell. It was as though some people doubted. While they were so, it was said: He has not died, but he has severe wounds; then in the night he could not bear the wounds and killed himself. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told of that and said: Allah is Most Great, I bear witness that I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger. Then he commanded Bilal, who called out: None shall enter Paradise except a believing soul, and Allah indeed supports this religion with the wicked man."
"9339","Mamar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): This religion is supported by one who has no share in it."
"9340","From Mamar, from Suhayl, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whom do you count as a martyr among you? They said: One killed in the way of Allah. He said: The martyrs of my ummah are few then; being killed in the way of Allah is martyrdom, the belly is martyrdom, drowning is martyrdom, plague is martyrdom, and the woman dying in childbirth is martyrdom."
"9341","From Mamar, perhaps, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from the wife of Masruq ibn al-Ajda, who said: Four are the martyrdom of Muslims: plague, childbirth, drowning, and the belly."
"9342","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn Hafs, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whom do you count as a martyr among you? They said: Whoever is killed in the way of Allah is a martyr. He said: The martyrs of my ummah are few then; whoever is killed in the way of Allah is a martyr, the plague-stricken is a martyr, the belly-ill is a martyr, the drowned is a martyr, and the woman who dies with child is a martyr."
"9343","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: And they say along with him, meaning Ata, and add to it: The martyr is the plague-stricken, the belly-ill, the drowned, the woman in childbirth, and one upon whom a building collapses."
"9344","From Mamar, from Ayyub, who said: A man of Quraysh looked down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions from a hilltop, and they said: How sturdy is this man, if only his sturdiness were in the way of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is none in the way of Allah except one who is killed? Then he said: Whoever goes out in the land seeking lawful provision to shield his family is in the way of Allah, and whoever goes out seeking lawful provision to shield himself is in the way of Allah, and whoever goes out seeking increase is in the way of Satan."
"9345","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Awf, who said: I heard al-Hasan saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The killing of a believer defending his property is martyrdom."
"9346","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Abi al-Suayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: On the day of Uhud, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked over the martyrs killed that day and said: I have borne witness over these, so wrap them in their blood. Two or three men would be buried in one grave, and he would ask which of them knew most of the Quran, and they would put him forward. Jabir said: So my father and my uncle were buried in one grave that day."
"9347","From Mamar, who said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of the martyrs of Uhud: These have passed and I have borne witness over them, and they did not consume any of their rewards, while you consume of your rewards, and I do not know what you will newly do after me."
"9348","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: No prayer was offered over the martyrs of Uhud."
"9349","From al-Thawri, from al-Shaybani, from Abu Malik, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over the slain of Uhud."
"9350","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I have not seen them washing the martyr, nor embalming him, nor shrouding him. I said: What do you think, how is prayer offered over them? He said: As prayer is offered over others who are not martyrs."
"9351","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Muawiyah ordered the killing of Hujr ibn Adi al-Kindi, so Hujr said: Do not loose a chain, or he said: iron, from me, and shroud me with my blood and my clothes."
"9352","From al-Thawri, from Mikhwal, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, from Zayd ibn Sawhan, who said: Do not wash blood from me, nor remove a garment from me except the two leather socks, and bury me in the ground a burial, for I am a disputing man who will dispute on the Day of Resurrection."
"9353","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Musab, a man from the descendants of Zayd, who said: Zayd said: Bury us with whatever dust has touched of our blood. He said: And Ammar al-Duhni informed me, who said: Zayd said: Bind my clothes upon me, and bury me and my maternal brother in one grave, meaning his brother Sarhan, for we are a disputing people."
"9354","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Sad ibn Ubayd, who was called in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the Reciter, and he had met an enemy and fled from them, so Umar said to him: Would you go to Syria? Perhaps Allah will bless you. He said: No, except the enemy from whom I fled. He said: So he addressed them at al-Qadisiyyah, saying: Tomorrow, Allah willing, we shall meet the enemy, and we shall be martyred; do not wash our blood from us, and do not shroud us except in the garment that was upon us."
"9355","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: We asked Sulayman ibn Musa: How is prayer over the martyr? He said: Like it is over others. And we asked him about burying the martyr; he said: If he is on the battlefield, we bury him as he is, neither washing him, nor shrouding him, nor embalming him; but if we bring him back while life remains in him, we wash him, shroud him, and embalm him; we found people upon that, and those before us among people held it."
"9356","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari informed me, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd, who said: When the martyr dies on the battlefield, he is buried as he is; but if he dies after being carried back, he is treated as others are treated."
"9357","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, who said: Umar was among the best of martyrs, yet he was washed, shrouded, and prayed over, because he lived after his stabbing. He said: And Abdullah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the same."
"9358","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, who said: Ali was washed, shrouded, and prayed over."
"9359","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from al-Shabi, who said: He was asked about a man killed by bandits; he said: He is not washed."
"9360","From Mamar, who said: One who heard Ikrimah informed me, saying: Prayer is offered over the martyr, but he is not washed, for Allah has purified him."
"9361","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyib, who both said: The martyr is washed, for every dead person is in a state of impurity."
"9362","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me, from Ibn Abi Ammar, from Shaddad ibn al-Hadi, that a Bedouin man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and believed in him and followed him, saying: I shall emigrate with you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted him to one of his Companions. When the expedition of Khaybar or Hunayn came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gained booty and divided it, assigning him a share, and his Companions gave him what was assigned to him, while he was herding their riding animals. When he came, they gave it to him. He said: What is this? They said: A share Allah and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned to you. He took it and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: What is this, O Muhammad? He said: A share I assigned to you. He said: It is not for this that I followed you, but I followed you that I be shot here - pointing to his throat - with an arrow, and enter Paradise. He said: If you are true to Allah, He will be true to you. He said: They stayed a little, then rose to fight the enemy, and he was brought carried, an arrow having struck him where he pointed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is it he? He was true to Allah, so He was true to him. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded him in a cloak of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put him forward and prayed over him, and among what appeared of his prayer over him was: O Allah, this is Your servant who went out emigrating in Your way, then was killed a martyr, and I am witness over him."
"9363","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man asked Ata: Is prayer offered over the martyr? He said: Yes. He said: Why, while he is in Paradise? He said: Prayer was offered over the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). From Ibn Jurayj: And it reached me that the martyrs of Badr were buried as they were."
"9364","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from al-Shabi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Hamzah on the day of Uhud seventy prayers; each time a man was brought, he prayed over him, with Hamzah laid down and prayed over along with him."
"9365","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, who said: Every skin is removed from the martyr, meaning when he is killed."
"9366","From Israil or another, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, who said: The slain man's leather socks, trousers, and cap - or he said: turban - are removed, and a garment is added or a garment reduced, until it is an odd number."
"9367","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Zubayr, who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: When Muawiyah wanted to run the canal, he said: Whoever has a slain one, let him come to his slain one, meaning the slain of Uhud. He said: So they brought them out supple, bending. He said: The pick struck the foot of one of them and it burst with blood. He said: Abu Said said: No denier shall ever deny after this."
"9368","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: One of the family of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah saw in a dream that he saw him saying: You buried me in a place where water has harmed me, so move me from it. He said: So they moved him, bringing him out like a preserved body, nothing changed of him except some hairs of his beard."
"9369","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Nubayh, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: We had carried the slain on the day of Uhud to bury them, when the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came saying: Bury the slain where they fell. So we returned them."
"9370","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla informed me, who said: The martyr is not buried in two shoes, nor two sandals, nor weapons, nor a ring. He said: He is buried in his waist-wrap and clothes. He said: And it reached me from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, who said: His veil is not buried."
"9371","From al-Thawri, from Said al-Yashkuri, from al-Shabi, who said: If the body of a slain man is found in a house or place, prayer is offered over him and blood-money is due; but if a head or a leg is found, no prayer is offered over him and no blood-money is due."
"9372","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi: That Abu Ayyub al-Ansari raided with Yazid ibn Muawiyah in the expedition in which he died."
"9373","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Abu Ayyub al-Ansari used to raid with Yazid ibn Muawiyah, and he fell ill while with him, so Yazid visited him and said to him: Your need? He said: When I die, carry me through the enemy land as far as you can, then bury me. When he died, he carried him until he penetrated a day or part of a day into Roman land, then alighted and buried him."
"9374","From Jafar, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, who said: I asked Jundab ibn Abdullah: Did you employ non-Arabs? He said: We used to employ them from village to village, to guide us on the road, then we let them go."
"9375","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Jamrah al-Dubai, who said: I said to Ibn Abbas: We raid with these rulers, but they fight seeking the world. He said: So fight for your own share of the Hereafter."
"9376","From Abd al-Quddus, who said: I heard al-Hasan saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not bear witness against your ummah of polytheism, nor declare them disbelievers for a sin; jihad is not harmed by the tyranny of a tyrant nor the justice of a just one; jihad continues until the last of this ummah is raised. And faith in the decree, its good and its evil. He said: And I heard Ibn Sirin mention the like and add: Until this ummah fights the Dajjal."
"9377","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, who said: I asked al-Shabi about raiding, and whether the men of the register are better or the volunteer? He said: Rather the men of the register; the volunteer returns whenever he wishes."
"9378","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Kahmas, who said: I said to al-Hasan: We raid with the rulers, but they do not inform us of their affairs, except that we make peace when they make peace, and we fight when they fight. He said: Fight the enemy of the Muslims along with the Muslims."
"9379","From Dawud ibn Qays, who said: Amr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Qays informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: Whoever stands guard forty nights has completed the guarding."
"9380","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Mukmil informed me that he heard Yazid ibn Abi Habib saying: A man of the Ansar came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said: Where were you? He said: On guard duty. He said: How long did you guard? He said: Thirty. He said: Why did you not complete forty?"
"9381","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ishaq ibn Rafi al-Madini informed me, from Yahya ibn Abi Sufyan al-Akhnasi, who said: Abu Hurayrah used to say: Guarding one night beside the sea behind the exposed frontier of the Muslims is dearer to me than witnessing the Night of Decree in one of the two mosques, the mosque of the Kabah or the mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah; guarding three days equals a year, and the completion of guarding is forty nights. And Salim Abu al-Nadr, freedman of Umar ibn Ubaydullah ibn Mamar, was standing, not sitting, when Yahya conveyed this hadith, and Yahya said to him: Do you know this hadith, O Abu al-Nadr? Salim said: Yes, I bear witness to knowing this hadith."
"9382","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: Makhul narrated to us, who said: Salman al-Farisi passed by Shurahbil ibn al-Simt while he was guarding a fortress in Persian land, and Salman said to him: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith that may help you in what you are in? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Guarding one day in the way of Allah is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer; and whoever dies guarding in the way of Allah is protected from the punishment of the grave, and his righteous deeds are increased for him until the Day of Resurrection."
"9383","From Abd al-Wahhab, who heard it from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, who said: Makhul narrated to me, from Salman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Guarding one day in the way of Allah is better than standing a month and fasting it, standing without sitting and fasting without breaking; and whoever dies guarding in the way of Allah is saved from the punishment of the grave, and his righteous deeds continue for him until the Day of Resurrection."
"9384","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Jabir, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, who said: We were in Persian land, and hardship and severity befell us, so Salman al-Farisi came to us saying: Rejoice, then rejoice; no Muslim guards in the way of Allah except that he is like one fasting a month and standing it; and whoever dies guarding in the way of Allah, his deeds continue for him until the Day of Resurrection, and he is protected from the trial of the grave. From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Musab ibn Muhammad informed me that Salman al-Farisi passed by al-Simt ibn Thabit while he was in a guard post, burdened by it and intending to leave it, and said: Shall I not tell you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Muhammad ibn Rashid."
"9385","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Musa ibn Abi Alqamah, from Isa, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Hold to jihad while it is sweet and green, before it becomes dry grass, or crumbled, or debris; and when expeditions are cut off, spoils consumed, and sanctities violated, then hold to guarding, for it is best in your warfare."
"9386","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Musa ibn Wardan, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever dies guarding dies a martyr, and is protected from the trial of the grave, and is served morning and evening with his provision from Paradise, and his deeds continue for him."
"9387","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib or another, who said: Umar used to dislike carrying Muslims on sea expeditions."
"9388","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I asked Ata about sea expeditions, and he disliked it, saying: I fear."
"9389","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Yunus ibn Yusuf, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab sent Alqamah ibn Mujazziz with men to Abyssinia, and they were stricken in the sea, so Umar swore by Allah never to carry anyone in it again."
"9390","And from Ibn al-Musayyib: It was disliked for warriors to ride in the sea."
"9391","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, and whoever believes in Allah and His Messenger, let him not expose his offspring to the polytheists."
"9392","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, that he used to dislike riding the sea except for three: a warrior, a pilgrim, or one performing umrah."
"9393","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that the wife of Hudhayfah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept, then woke laughing. I said: Are you laughing at me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No, but at people of my ummah going out as warriors in the sea, their likeness like kings upon couches. Then he slept, then woke again laughing. I said: Are you laughing at me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No, but at people of my ummah going out as warriors in the sea, returning with little booty, forgiven. She said: Supplicate Allah to make me among them. He said: So he supplicated for her. Ata ibn Yasar said: So I saw her in an expedition led by al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr to Roman land, she being with us, and she died in Roman land."
"9394","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, who said: An informant informed me, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: An expedition in the sea is better than ten expeditions on land, and whoever crosses the sea, it is as though he crossed the valleys; and one seasick in the ship is like one writhing in his blood."
"9395","From Abd al-Quddus, who said: Alqamah ibn Shihab al-Qurashi narrated to us, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does not attain expedition with me, let him raid in the sea, for the reward of a day in the sea is like the reward of a month on land; and being killed in the sea is like two killings on land; and one seasick in the ship is like one writhing in his blood; and the best martyrs of my ummah are the companions of the cave. They said: Who are the companions of the cave, O Messenger of Allah? He said: People you find in their ships in the way of Allah."
"9396","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Mujahid said: An expedition in the sea equals ten on land, and one seasick in the sea is like one writhing in his blood in the way of Allah."
"9397","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told that Maslamah ibn Makhlad said to people who embarked on a sea expedition: They left behind them none of their sins."
"9398","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Wasil, from Laqit, from Abu Burdah: That Abu Musa al-Ashari used to raid in the sea."
"9399","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ishaq ibn Rafi, who said: It reached us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on the people of the graveyard. Aishah said: The people of al-Baqi? He said: May Allah have mercy on the people of the graveyard. Aishah said: The people of al-Baqi? Until he said it three times. He said: The graveyard of Asqalan."
"9400","Ibn Jurayj said: I heard the cousin of Muhammad ibn Kab narrating, that he used to mention: That eating, drinking, food, and marriage there are better in Asqalan."
"9401","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Khaybar: I shall surely give the banner to a man whom Allah and His Messenger love, and who loves Allah and His Messenger. He said: So he called Ali, who had sore eyes, and spat in his eyes, then gave it to him, and Allah granted victory through him."
"9402","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, that Sad ibn Ubadah carried the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Badr and others."
"9403","From Ibn Jurayj, from one who narrated to him, from Amir, that the banner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be with Ali ibn Abi Talib, and among the Ansar wherever they turned."
"9404","From Mamar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam, that the banner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with Ali ibn Abi Talib, and the banner of the Ansar with Sad ibn Ubadah; and when fighting grew fierce, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be under the banner of the Ansar."
"9405","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from a man who saw a banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he tied for Amr ibn al-As, black."
"9406","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Sad ibn Said, brother of Yahya ibn Said, narrated to me, who said: They narrated that the banner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with Sad ibn Ubadah on the day of the Conquest, and Sad gave it to his son Qays."
"9407","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man of Madinah informed me that the banner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be white, and his standard black."
"9408","He said: I was told from Ibn Sirin, who said: When a Muslim man in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared, he took off his weapons and gave to this one, and to this one, and to this one of his weapons; and the slightest of them in the wind, meaning until they are denied and not recognized."
"9409","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Wahid informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz forbade hamstringing a beast that lagged in enemy land; he said: As for weapons, let him bury them."
"9410","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: Al-Zubayr was the first to draw a sword in the way of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in lower Makkah, and al-Zubayr in Makkah, when he was told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was killed; so he went out with his sword drawn, splitting people with it, until he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him unharmed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him about that, and he told him; so he supplicated for him and for his sword."
"9411","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me that the first man to draw a sword in the way of Allah was al-Zubayr. A blast from Satan was blown: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has been seized, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in upper Makkah; so al-Zubayr went out splitting people with his sword, and met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said to him: What is with you, O Zubayr? He said: I was told you were seized. He said: So he supplicated for him and for his sword."
"9412","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he heard Yaqub ibn Musa saying: The one who bloodied the face of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud was a man of Hudhayl called Ibn al-Qamiah; and his end was that Allah set a billy-goat upon him, which butted him and killed him. Ibrahim said: His name is Abdullah ibn al-Qamiah."
"9413","From Mamar, from al-Jazari, from Miqsam, who said, Mamar said: And I heard al-Zubayr narrating part of it, that Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas broke the front tooth of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud and bloodied his face; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against him, saying: O Allah, let not the year pass upon him until he dies a disbeliever. And the year did not pass upon him until he died a disbeliever, to Hell."
"9414","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to relieve expedition forces."
"9415","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Umar sent an army, and he used to relieve armies; they stayed a while without relief coming to them, so they returned; the commander of the detachment wrote to Umar that they returned and left their frontier post, setting an evil precedent for people. Umar sent to them, none other witnessing it, and was harsh upon them, threatening them with a threat that loomed over them. They said: O Umar, for what do you separate us? You have left among us the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of relieving expedition forces with one another. He said: I do not separate you myself, but for matters that were not among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the Ansar."
"9416","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata was asked about a man of the polytheists who comes to a Muslim without a covenant; he said: Give him choice, either you settle him or convey him to his place of safety. He said: And one of the people of Syria, Abdullah ibn Qays, claimed in the gathering of Ata, saying: A Roman comes, and when he comes to the Muslims without weapons and without covenant, he is not harmed."
"9417","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, and from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, and from Abu al-Nadr, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that they said concerning a man of the people at war who enters under safety and one of his heirs in lineage whom he would inherit dies: If he had shown residence among the Arabs before he died, he has his inheritance, otherwise not. And they said concerning a woman of the People of the Book from the people at war who enters Arab land under safety: If she shows residence in Arab land, there is no harm in Muslims marrying her; but if she shows that only at the time of proposal, she is not to be married."
"9418","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata was asked about a dhimmi man seized among polytheists, having stipulated upon them that he not come to them, saying: I did not intend to aid them; so he disliked killing him except with proof. One of the people of knowledge said to him: If he breaks a single thing of what is upon him, he has broken the treaty."
"9419","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said to me concerning a man who made treaty for himself and his children, young and old, then these betrayed him: There is no disagreement about it; they say: They become lawful through the betrayal of these, if they were treated for themselves."
"9420","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata al-Khurasani informed me that Tustar was under treaty, then its people disbelieved, so the Emigrants raided them, killing them and routing them, taking them captive; the Muslims took their women, and children were born to them from them. He said: I saw their children, they were of that birth. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered whoever took captives among them to return them to their tribute, separating their masters from them."
"9421","From Mamar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam, that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made treaty with the people of Khaybar, he treated with them that their properties were his, and that they were safe in their blood, offspring, and women. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the two sons of Abu al-Huqayq, saying: Where is the wealth with which you left al-Nadir? They said: We spent it and it perished. He said: What if you are lying, then your blood, wealth, and women have become lawful to me? They said: Yes, and he took witness over them. He said: You hid it in such-and-such place. He sent men with them, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found the wealth as he described. Then he struck their necks, took their wealth, and enslaved their women; and Safiyyah was under one of them."
"9422","From Mamar, from Qatadah: It was disliked to marry women of the People of the Book except under covenant."
"9423","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided eighteen expeditions. He said: And I heard him another time saying: Twenty-four expeditions, and I do not know whether it was a lapse from him or something he heard after that. Al-Zuhri said: And what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought in was everything mentioned in the Quran."
"9424","From Mamar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam, who said: The detachments were twenty-four, and the expeditions eighteen or nineteen."
"9425","From al-Thawri, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: The name of the slave-girl of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Khadrah, and his donkey Ufayr, and his she-camel al-Qaswa, and his mule al-Shahba, and his sword Dhu al-Fiqar."
"9426","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Maysarah informed me, he said: The name of the sword of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Dhu al-Faqar, and the name of his coat of mail was Dhat al-Fudul."
"9427","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, that the name of the sword of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Dhu al-Faqar. Ja'far said: I saw the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); its hilt was of silver, its shoe-tip was of silver, and between that were rings of silver. He said: It is with these people, meaning the Banu al-Abbas. From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Ja'far, from his father, similar to this, he said: Its caps were of silver, meaning its top, he said: And in his coat of mail were two rings of silver."
"9428","From Ma'mar, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the sword of Umar ibn al-Khattab was ornamented with silver."
"9429","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me: If a man gives something in the path of Allah and he completes his campaign and brings it back, or part of it, he should not consume it, but let him spend it onward in that same cause. He said: And if he dedicates a she-camel in the path of Allah and it gives birth, its offspring takes its same status."
"9430","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, he said: If anything is left over, he should put it into something like that."
"9431","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar gave a camel in the path of Allah, and said to the one he gave it to: Do not introduce any change in it until, when you pass Wadi al-Qura or its vicinity on the Egypt road, then do with it as you wish. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', similar to it. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about that, and he said: It is his, unless he made it a dedicated endowment."
"9432","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyab being asked about a man given something in the path of Allah, so Sa'id said: When he reaches the head of his campaign destination, it is like his own property. From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyab, similar to it."
"9433","From Ma'mar, from Ibrahim, and he was asked about the jihad of women, and he said: They used to be present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), treating the wounded and giving drink to the fighters, and I did not hear of a woman being killed alongside him. And the women of Quraysh fought on the day of Yarmuk when the hosts of the Romans pressed them, until they mingled with the Muslim camp, so the women struck with swords that day, in the caliphate of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, similar to it."
"9434","From Abd al-Quddus, he said: I heard al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon women is what is upon men, except Friday prayer, funerals, and jihad."
"9435","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Muslim informed me, he said: I think it was from al-Hasan, that a man came to al-Zubayr and said: Shall I kill Ali? He said: Yes. He said: And how would you do it? He said: I would show him that I am with him, then I would be killed with him and so kill him. Al-Zubayr said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Faith restrains treacherous killing; a believer does not kill treacherously. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, similar to it, he said: Faith is a restraint against treachery; a believer does not kill treacherously."
"9436","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah accompanied a people in the Jahiliyyah, then killed them and took their property, then came and embraced Islam. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for Islam, I accept it, but as for the property, I have nothing to do with it. Ma'mar said: And I heard that they had taken a covenant from al-Mughirah that he would not betray them until he gave them notice. They halted at a stop, and he began digging with the tip of his sword. They said: What are you doing? He said: I am digging your graves. By that he made them feel safe, so they drank then slept, then he killed them, and none of them escaped except al-Sharid, and for that he was called al-Sharid."
"9437","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man carrying his sword wrapped about him entered upon al-Mukhtar ibn Abi Ubayd. He said: Al-Mukhtar began to lie against Allah and against His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So I intended to strike him with my sword, then I remembered a hadith narrated to me by Amr ibn al-Hamiq, or Amr ibn so-and-so, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Any man who grants a man security for his blood and property then kills him, the protection of Allah is absolved from the killer, even if the one killed was a disbeliever."
"9438","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked Hammad about people of the land of war with whom the people of Islam made peace for a thousand heads every year, and some of them would capture others and deliver them. He said: There is no harm in that; they may deliver them from wherever they wish."
"9439","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: If a man comes out of enemy land with a slave with him, if he (the master) accepts Islam, he belongs to him; but if the slave precedes him and accepts Islam, he is free."
"9440","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, he said: Abu Uthman al-Nahdi narrated to us, from Abu Bakrah, that he went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was besieging the people of Taif, with twenty-three slaves, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed them; they are those called the Utaqa (the freed ones)."
"9441","From al-Hasan ibn Mihran, from al-Mutarrah, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah will distance his face from the Fire by a journey of a hundred years at the gallop of a swift thoroughbred racehorse."
"9442","From Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Makhul, from Amr ibn Abasah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah will distance his face from the Fire by a journey of a hundred years."
"9443","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id and Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, that they heard al-Nu'man ibn Abi Ayyash narrating, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah will distance his face from the Fire by seventy autumns. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id and Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-Nu'man, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"9444","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to people besieging the enemy in Ramadan: Do not fast."
"9445","From Abdullah, from Shu'bah, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of the conquest of Makkah: This is a day of fighting, so break your fast."
"9446","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Sa'id ibn Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that Umar wrote to Ammar: The spoils belong to whoever witnessed the battle."
"9447","From Hammad ibn Usamah, from al-Mujalid, from Amir, he said: Umar wrote: Distribute to whoever comes, so long as the slain have not burst, meaning so long as the bellies of the slain have not swollen."
"9448","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think of a man who is the rear-guard of the people and defends his companions, will his share be like the share of others? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May your mother be bereaved of you, O son of Umm Sa'd; are you provided and aided except through your weak ones?"
"9449","From Hushaym, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from Amir al-Sha'bi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: Distribute to whatever Muslims join you, so long as the Persian slain have not burst."
"9450","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the first one to race horses against each other. He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, I think."
"9451","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raced horses, sending the trained ones from al-Hufayya, their finish at Thaniyyat al-Wada', and he raced the untrained horses with their finish from the pass to the mosque of Banu Zurayq. He said: And Abdullah ibn Umar was among those who rode the horses. He said: I was upon a horse, he said: It passed by a wall and carried me off until I was behind it. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, similar to it."
"9452","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab raced horses and ran the race."
"9453","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, from Abdullah ibn Husayn, he said: Hudhayfah raced the people on a grey horse of his, and it outran them. He said: So we entered upon him in his house, and there it was at its manger, standing on sand, sweat dripping onto a cloak of his, with Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman sitting beside it on his feet, his buttocks not touching the ground. He said: People began entering upon him congratulating him, saying: May the victory delight you. He said: Then a man entered and said nothing. A man said: Will you not congratulate him? He said: For what? He said: His horse won. He said: I fear that will reach the governor. He said: Over the people that day was Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas. So Hudhayfah said: By Allah, the Hour will not come until there rules over you one who will not weigh a tenth of a gnat on the Day of Resurrection."
"9454","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning two men who stake wagers on a horse, then another joins them with a horse. He said: If they are not sure he can outrun them both, there is no harm in it; but if they are sure of him, it is gambling."
"9455","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of companions are four, the best of detachments are four hundred, the best of armies are four thousand, and twelve thousand will never be defeated from fewness."
"9456","From Ibn Jurayj, from Zuhayr, he said: A man of the Ansar informed me, from al-Hasan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The shawls of the warriors are swords."
"9457","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim, he said: Abu Bakr was brought a head, and he said: You have transgressed."
"9458","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never brought a head, but Abu Bakr was brought a head, and he said: Carrion shall not be brought to the city of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And the first one to be brought a head was Ibn al-Zubayr."
"9459","From Zam'ah ibn Salih, he said: Ziyad ibn Sa'd informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never brought a head, not even on the day of Badr. And Abu Bakr was brought a great head, and he said: What have I to do with their carrion being carried to the land of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Then none was carried after him in the time of the fitnah, neither to Marwan nor to others, until the time of Ibn al-Zubayr, and he was the first to institute that; the head of Ziyad and his companions was carried to him, and they cooked their heads in pots."
"9460","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrimah the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that a man reviled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Who will suffice me against my enemy? Al-Zubayr said: I will. So he met him in single combat and al-Zubayr killed him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his spoils."
"9461","From Ma'mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: Urwah ibn Muhammad informed me, from a man, from ... or he said: two thousand, that a woman used to revile the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will suffice me against my enemy? So Khalid ibn al-Walid went out to her and killed her."
"9462","And my father informed me that Ayyub ibn Yahya went out to Aden, and a Christian man who had reviled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought before him, so he sought counsel concerning him, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-San'ani advised him to kill him, so he killed him. And he narrated to him a hadith concerning that, he said: And he had met Umar and heard much knowledge from him. He said: So Ayyub wrote concerning that to Abd al-Malik, or to al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik, and he wrote approving of that."
"9463","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that a man belied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent Ali and al-Zubayr and said: Go, and if you catch him, kill him."
"9464","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that Ali said concerning one who lies against the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): His neck is struck."
"9465","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The jihad of the old, the jihad of the weak, and the jihad of women is Hajj and Umrah. From Ibrahim, that he heard Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"9466","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Migration has ceased after the conquest, but there is jihad and intention; and when you are called to march forth, then march forth."
"9467","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Anas ibn Malik say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no migration after the conquest."
"9468","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of the conquest of Makkah: There is no migration, but there is jihad and intention; and when you are called to march forth, then march forth."
"9469","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah narrated to us, he said: I heard a man, Ata said, he said: A man was captured by the Daylam, and they said: We will release you if you give us a covenant and pledge that you will send us such-and-such, and if he does not do it he will come back to them of himself, though he has nothing. So how do you command him? He said: He should go back to them. He said: But they are people of idolatry. He said: He should fulfill the covenant. He said: But they are people of idolatry. He said: He should fulfill the covenant to them: indeed the covenant is ever questioned about."
"9470","From al-Thawri, he said: Fay' and ghanimah are different. As for ghanimah, it is what the Muslims take and it comes into their hands from the disbelievers; the fifth of that goes to the commander to place wherever Allah commanded, and the remaining four-fifths belong to those who won the spoils. And fay' is what results from a peace settlement between the imam and the disbelievers concerning their persons, lands, crops, and what they settled upon of what the Muslims did not take by force, nor possess, nor overpower, until a settlement occurred between them concerning it. He said: That settlement is for the imam to place wherever Allah commanded."
"9471","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: My father brought me on the day of Uhud to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I was fourteen years old, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit me. Then he brought me on the day of the Trench when I was fifteen, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned a stipend for me. Nafi' said: So I narrated it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he ordered that a stipend be assigned only to one of fifteen years. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubaydullah informed me, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar said: I was presented to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud when I was fourteen years old, then he mentioned similar to the hadith of Abdullah ibn Umar. He said: And Umar would not assign a stipend to anyone until he reached puberty except a hundred dirhams, and he would not assign for a newborn until weaned. While he was patrolling one night at the prayer place, a child cried, so he said to his mother: Nurse him. She said: The Commander of the Believers does not assign for a newborn until weaned, and I have weaned him. Umar said: I almost killed him. Nurse him, for the Commander of the Believers will assign for him. Then after that he assigned for the newborn from birth."
"9472","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The first that is mentioned of Abd al-Muttalib, the grandfather of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is that Quraysh left the Sanctuary fleeing from the companions of the Elephant, while he was a young lad. He said: By Allah, I shall not leave the Sanctuary of Allah seeking honor elsewhere. So he sat by the House, and Quraysh departed from him. He said: O Allah, a man defends his baggage, so defend Your baggage; let not their cross and their might overcome Your might tomorrow. He remained firm until Allah, blessed and exalted, destroyed the Elephant and its companions. Quraysh returned, and he had grown great among them for his endurance and his honoring of the sacred things of Allah. While he was in that state, his eldest son was born to him and grew up, and he is al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib. Abd al-Muttalib was visited in a dream and told: Dig Zamzam, the hidden treasure of the greatest elder. He woke and said: O Allah, make it clear to me. Then he was shown again in a dream: Dig Zamzam, concealed between dung and blood, at the raven's pecking place, in the ant village, facing the red idols. So Abd al-Muttalib arose and walked until he sat in the Sacred Mosque watching for the signs hidden for him. A cow was slaughtered at al-Hazurah, and it escaped its slaughterer with its last breath until death overcame it in the Mosque at the site of Zamzam. That cow was butchered in its place until its meat was carried away. Then a raven came swooping down until it landed in the dung and pecked in the ant village. So Abd al-Muttalib stood digging there. Quraysh came and said to Abd al-Muttalib: What is this deed? We never thought you ignorant; why do you dig in our mosque? Abd al-Muttalib said: I shall surely dig this well and strive against whoever bars me from it. He and his son al-Harith began digging, and he had no child that day besides him. Men of Quraysh pressed upon them, disputing and fighting them, while others of Quraysh held people back from them for what they knew of his noble lineage, his truthfulness, and his striving in his religion that day. When digging became possible for him and harm intensified against him, he vowed that if he were granted ten sons he would sacrifice one of them. Then he dug until he reached swords buried in Zamzam. When Quraysh saw he had reached the swords, they said to Abd al-Muttalib: Give us a share of what you found. Abd al-Muttalib said: Rather these swords belong to the House of Allah. Then he dug until he reached water, digging it deep, then widened it so it would not run dry. Then he built a basin over it, and he and his son would draw and fill that basin, and the pilgrims would drink from it. Men of envious Quraysh would break it at night, and Abd al-Muttalib would repair it in the morning. When they broke it often, Abd al-Muttalib prayed to his Lord, and he was shown in a dream and told: Say: O Allah, I do not permit it for one who washes, but it is lawful and permitted drink for the drinker. Then he was sufficed against them. Abd al-Muttalib stood when Quraysh had gathered at the Mosque and proclaimed what he had been shown, then departed. No one of Quraysh spoiled his basin thereafter without being struck with an ailment in his body, until they left him his basin and his watering. Then Abd al-Muttalib married women and ten men were born to him. He said: O Allah, I had vowed to You the sacrifice of one of them, and I shall cast lots among them, so strike with it whomever You wish. He cast lots among them, and the lot fell upon Abdullah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, who was the dearest of his sons to him. He said: O Allah, is he dearer to You, or a hundred camels? Then he cast lots between him and a hundred camels, and the lot fell upon the hundred camels. So Abd al-Muttalib slaughtered them in place of Abdullah. Abdullah was the handsomest man ever seen in Quraysh. One day he went out before women of Quraysh gathered together, and a woman among them said: O women of Quraysh, which of you will this youth marry ... the light between his eyes. And there was a light between his eyes. So Aminah bint Wahb ibn Abd Manaf ibn Zuhrah married him, and he consummated with her, and she conceived the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Abd al-Muttalib sent Abdullah ibn Abd al-Muttalib to fetch dates for him from Yathrib, and Abdullah died there. Aminah gave birth to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was in the care of Abd al-Muttalib. He sought a wet-nurse for him, a woman of Banu Sa'd ibn Bakr. The one nursing him took him down to the market of Ukaz, where a soothsayer saw him and said: O people of Ukaz, kill this boy, for he shall have a kingdom. His nursing mother took fright with him, and Allah saved him. Then he grew up with her, until he could walk, with his foster sister watching over him. His sister through his nursing mother came and said: O my mother, I saw a company seize my brother just now and split open his belly. His nursing mother arose in alarm until she reached him, and there he was sitting, his color changed, with no one near him. So she departed with him until she brought him to his mother, and said to her: Take your son from me, for I have feared for him. His mother said: No, by Allah, there is nothing wrong with my son that you fear. I saw while he was in my belly that a light came out of me by which the palaces of Syria were lit. And I bore him, and when I bore him he fell leaning upon his hands, raising his head to the sky. His mother and his grandfather Abd al-Muttalib took charge of him after weaning. Then his mother died, and he remained in the care of his grandfather. As a boy he would come to his grandfather's cushion and sit on it; his grandfather would come out, having grown old, and the maid leading him would say: Get down from your grandfather's cushion. Abd al-Muttalib would say: Leave my son, for he is destined for good. Then his grandfather died while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a boy, and Abu Talib took him in, the full brother of Abdullah. When he neared puberty, Abu Talib journeyed with him trading toward Syria. When they halted at Tayma, a Jewish rabbi of Tayma saw him and said to Abu Talib: What is this boy to you? He said: He is my brother's son. He said: Are you tender toward him? He said: Yes. He said: By Allah, if you go forth with him to Syria you will never return with him to your family; they will surely kill him, for he is their enemy. So Abu Talib returned from Tayma to Makkah. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached puberty, a woman perfumed the Ka'bah with incense, and a spark flew from her censer into the Ka'bah's garments and burned them, weakening it. Quraysh consulted about demolishing it, fearing to demolish it. Al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah said to them: What do you intend by demolishing it, repair or harm? They said: Rather repair. He said: Allah does not destroy the reformers. They said: Who will climb it and demolish it? Al-Walid said: I shall climb it and demolish it. Al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah climbed onto the back of the House with an axe and said: O Allah, we intend only repair. Then he demolished. When Quraysh saw that he had demolished part of it and no punishment they feared befell them, they demolished with him. When they rebuilt it and reached the place of the Corner, Quraysh disputed over the Corner, which tribe would lift it, until they nearly came to blows. They said: Come, let us appoint as judge the first who comes upon us from this lane. They agreed on that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon them, a youth wearing a striped اليمني cloak. They appointed him judge. He ordered the Corner placed in a cloth, then ordered the chief of each tribe and gave him a side of the cloth, then he climbed and they raised the Corner to him, and he himself placed it. He only grew in their pleasure with the passing years, until they called him the Trustworthy before revelation came down to him. They would hardly slaughter a she-camel for sale without bringing it to him to pray for blessing in it for them. When he matured and reached his prime, having little wealth, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid hired him to the market of Hubashah, a market in Tihamah, hiring another man of Quraysh with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), speaking of her, said: I never saw a better employer than Khadijah; whenever my companion and I returned we found with her a delicacy of food she had hidden for us. He said: When we returned from the market of Hubashah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I said to my companion: Come, let us visit Khadijah. So we came to her, and while we were with her there entered upon us an adolescent girl of Quraysh's household slaves, and al-muntashiyah is the nubile one who desires men. She said: Is this Muhammad? By Him by Whom oaths are sworn, if he has come as a suitor. I said: No. When my companion and I went out, he said: Are you shy of Khadijah's proposal? By Allah, there is no Qurayshi woman but considers you her equal. So I returned to her another time, and that adolescent girl entered upon us and said: Is this Muhammad? By Him by Whom oaths are sworn, if he has come as a suitor. I said shyly: Yes. Khadijah and her sister did not refuse us. She went to her father Khuwaylid ibn Asad while he was drunk with drink, and said: This is your brother's son Muhammad ibn Abdullah proposing to Khadijah, and Khadijah is pleased. He called him and asked him about that, and he proposed to him and married him (to her). He said: Khadijah perfumed (the groom) and dressed him in a robe. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consummated with her. In the morning the old man sobered from his drunkenness and said: What is this perfume and this robe? Khadijah's sister said: This is a robe your brother's son Muhammad ibn Abdullah has dressed you in; he married Khadijah to him and has consummated with her. The old man objected, then accepted until that settled, feeling shy. Poets of Quraysh declaimed: Do not turn away, Khadij, from Muhammad, a skin shining like the light of al-Farqad. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained with Khadijah until she bore him some of his daughters, and he had by her al-Qasim. Some scholars claimed she bore him another boy called al-Tahir. He said: And some said: We know she bore him only al-Qasim, and she bore him his four daughters: Zaynab, Fatimah, Ruqayyah, and Umm Kulthum. After she bore him some of his daughters, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began to devote himself, and solitude was made beloved to him."
"9473","He said: Ma'mar informed us, he said: Al-Zuhri informed us, he said: Urwah informed me, from Aishah, she said: The first of revelation with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began was the true dream; he would not see a dream but it came like the break of dawn. Then solitude was made beloved to him, and he would come to Hira and devote himself in it - and it is worship for numbered nights - and take provision for that, then return to Khadijah and take provision for the like of it. Then the Truth came to him while he was in the cave of Hira, and the angel came to him in it and said to him: Read. He says to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Read. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I said: I am not a reader. So he seized me and pressed me until exertion overcame me, then released me and said: Read. I said: I am not a reader. So he seized me and pressed me a third time until exertion overcame me, then released me and said: Read in the name of your Lord who created, until he reached: taught man what he knew not. So he returned with it, his sides trembling, until he entered upon Khadijah and said: Wrap me, wrap me. So they wrapped him until the fear left him. He said to Khadijah: What is wrong with me, and told her the news, and said: I feared for myself? She said: No, by Allah, Allah will never disgrace you; you uphold kinship, speak truthfully, honor the guest, and aid in the calamities of truth. Then Khadijah set out with him until she brought him to Waraqah ibn Nawfal ibn Rashid ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy, the cousin of Khadijah, her father's brother, who had become Christian in the Jahiliyyah and used to write the Arabic script, writing in Arabic of the Gospel what Allah willed him to write. He was a great old man who had gone blind. Khadijah said: O cousin, listen to your brother's son. Waraqah said: O brother's son, what do you see? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told him what he saw. Waraqah said: This is the Namus sent down upon Musa, peace be upon him. Would that I were young when your people drive you out. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will they drive me out? Waraqah said: Yes, no one ever came with what you came with but was opposed and harmed; if your day reaches me I shall aid you mightily. Then Waraqah soon died, and revelation paused for a time, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grieved, in what reached us, a grief from which he repeatedly went forth to throw himself from mountain peaks. Whenever he climbed a mountain top, Jibril, peace be upon him, appeared to him and said: O Muhammad, O Messenger of Allah in truth. So his heart calmed and his soul settled, and he returned. When the pause of revelation lengthened for him he returned to the like of that, and when he climbed a mountain top Jibril, peace be upon him, appeared to him and said to him the like of that."
"9474","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) speaking about the pause of revelation, and he said in his hadith: While I was walking I heard a voice from the sky, so I raised my head, and there was the one who had come to me at Hira sitting upon a chair between heaven and earth. I was struck with terror of him, then I returned and said: Wrap me, wrap me, and cover me. So Allah the Exalted sent down: O you enwrapped, up to: and shun idols, before prayer was ordained, and they are the idols."
"9475","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: And he informed me that Khadijah died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was shown in Paradise a house for Khadijah of hollow pearl, with no clamor in it and no toil, and it is pearl reed. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Waraqah ibn Nawfal, as reached us, and said: I saw him in a dream wearing white garments, and I think that had he been of the people of the Fire I would not have seen the whiteness upon him. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called to Islam secretly and openly, and to abandoning idols."
"9476","Ma'mar said: And Qatadah informed us, from al-Hasan and others, and he said: The first to believe in him was Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, when he was fifteen or sixteen years old."
"9477","He said: And Uthman al-Jazari informed me, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali is the first who embraced Islam. He said: So I asked al-Zuhri, and he said: We know of no one who embraced Islam before Zayd ibn Harithah."
"9478","Ma'mar said: So I asked al-Zuhri, he said: There responded to him whomsoever Allah willed of young men and weak people, until those who believed in him grew many, while the disbelievers of Quraysh denied what he said, saying when he passed them in their gatherings, pointing to him: This youth of Banu Abd al-Muttalib claims, they allege, to be spoken to from heaven. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: None of the nobles of his people followed him except two men, Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah have mercy on them. Umar was harsh toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the believers, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, strengthen Your religion with Ibn al-Khattab. The first of Umar's Islam, after many people had embraced Islam before him, was that he was told his sister Umm Jamil bint al-Khattab had embraced Islam, and that she had a shoulder-blade on which she had written part of the Qur'an, reading it secretly. He was told she would not eat of the carrion from which Umar ate. He entered upon her and said: What is the shoulder-blade I was told you have, in which you read what the son of Abu Kabshah says, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: I have no shoulder-blade. So he struck her, or he said: Umar beat her. Then he arose seeking the shoulder-blade in the house until he found it. When he found it he said: I have been told you do not eat of my food which I eat from. Then he struck her with the shoulder-blade, wounding her twice. Then he went out with the shoulder-blade until he called a reader, who read to him, for Umar could not write. When it was read to him, his heart stirred when he heard the Qur'an, and Islam entered his soul. In the evening he set out until he drew near the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying aloud in recitation. He heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting: You did not recite before it any scripture, nor inscribe it with your right hand, until he reached: the wrongdoers, and heard him reciting: And those who disbelieve say: You are not sent, until he reached: knowledge of the Book. Umar waited for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he finished his prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out to his family, and Umar hastened walking after him when he saw him, and said: Wait for me, O Muhammad. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. Umar said: Wait for me, O Muhammad, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) waited for him, and Umar believed in him and affirmed him. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, embraced Islam, he went until he entered upon Khalid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah and said: O uncle, bear witness that I believe in Allah and His Messenger, and I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); inform your people of that. Al-Walid said: O sister's son, be deliberate in your affair, for a man wakes in a state known to people and evenings in another. Umar said: By Allah, the matter has become clear to me; inform your people of my Islam. Al-Walid said: I shall not be the first to mention that of you. So Umar entered and ... and when Umar knew al-Walid had mentioned nothing of his affair, he entered upon Jamil ibn Ma'mar al-Jumahi and said: Announce that I bear witness there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. Jamil ibn Ma'mar arose dragging his cloak in haste until he went round the gatherings of Quraysh saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab has apostatized. Quraysh returned nothing to him, for Umar was the chief of his people and they feared to reproach him. When he saw they did not reproach him for that, he walked until he came to their fullest gathering, entered al-Hijr, leaned his back against the Ka'bah and said: O assembly of Quraysh, do you know that I bear witness there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger? Men among them rose and fought him fiercely, and he fought them most of his day until they left him. He proclaimed his Islam openly, going to them morning and evening bearing witness there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, and they left him; though after their first outburst they did not leave him. That weighed heavily upon the disbelievers of Quraysh against every man who embraced Islam, and they tortured a number of the Muslims. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: And Hilal mentioned their fathers who died disbelievers, and they vexed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and opposed him. When he was taken by night to al-Masjid al-Aqsa, people woke to him telling that he had been taken by night, and some who had affirmed and believed him apostatized, were tried, and belied him in it. A man of the idolaters hastened to Abu Bakr and said: This companion of yours claims he was taken by night to Bayt al-Maqdis then returned in his night. Abu Bakr said: Did he say that? They said: Yes. Abu Bakr said: I bear witness, if he said that, he has spoken truly. They said: Do you affirm him that he came to Syria in one night and returned before morning? Abu Bakr said: Yes, I affirm him in what is farther than that; I affirm him in the tidings of heaven morning and evening. For that Abu Bakr was called al-Siddiq."
"9479","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: And Anas ibn Malik informed me that prayer was ordained upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night he was taken by night as fifty, then reduced to five. Then it was called: O Muhammad, the word is not changed with Me, and for you with the five is fifty."
"9480","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: And Abu Salamah informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I stood in al-Hijr when my people belied me, and Bayt al-Maqdis was raised for me until I began describing it to them."
"9481","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When I was taken by night I met Musa. He said: So he described him: a man, I think he said, lean, lank-haired as though of the men of Shanuah. He said: And I met Isa, peace be upon him, and described him, saying: Of medium stature, ruddy, as though just out of a bath. He said: And I saw Ibrahim, and I am the most like of his children to him. He said: And two vessels were brought, in one milk and in the other wine. It was said: Take whichever you wish. So I took the milk and drank it, and it was said to me: You were guided to the fitrah, or you attained the fitrah; had you taken the wine your nation would have gone astray."
"9482","From Ma'mar, he said: Al-Zuhri informed me, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, each confirming the other, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the time of al-Hudaybiyyah with over a thousand of his companions, until when they were at Dhu al-Hulayfah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) garlanded the sacrificial animals and marked them, entered ihram for Umrah, and sent ahead a scout of his from Khuza'ah to inform him about Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marched until when they were at Ghadir al-Ashtat near Usfan, his Khuza'i scout came to him and said: I left Ka'b ibn Luayy and Amir ibn Luayy having gathered the Ahabish against you and gathered hosts against you; they will fight you and bar you from the House. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Advise me; do you think we should incline toward the offspring of those who aided them and strike them, so if they sit back they sit bereaved and despoiled, and if they come it will be a neck Allah has cut off; or do you think we should head for the House, and whoever bars us we shall fight? They said: The Messenger of Allah knows best; O Prophet of Allah, we came only as pilgrims, not to fight anyone, but whoever stands between us and the House we shall fight. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then proceed."
"9483","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: And Abu Hurayrah used to say: I never saw anyone who consulted his companions more than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"9484","From Ikrimah ibn Ammar, he said: Abu Zumayl Simak al-Hanafi informed us that he heard Ibn Abbas say: The scribe of the document on the day of al-Hudaybiyyah was Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"9485","He said: Ma'mar informed us, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about it and he laughed and said: He is Ali ibn Abi Talib; but if you asked these, they would say Uthman, meaning the Banu Umayyah."
"9486","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Heraclius was a soothsayer who looked at the stars. One morning his courtiers disliked his appearance and said: What is wrong with you? He said: I looked at the stars last night and saw the king of circumcision has appeared. They said: Let that not grieve you, for only the Jews circumcise; send to your cities and kill every Jew. Al-Zuhri said: And he wrote to a peer of his, also a soothsayer looking at the stars, writing to him the like of his words. He said: And the king of Busra brought up to him an Arab man informing him about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: See, is he circumcised? They said: They looked, and he was circumcised. They said: This is the king of circumcision who has appeared."
"9487","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud informed me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Sufyan narrated to me mouth to mouth, he said: I set out in the truce between us and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). While I was in Syria, a letter came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Heraclius; Dihyah al-Kalbi had brought it and handed it to the chief of Busra, who handed it to Heraclius. Heraclius said: Is there here anyone of the people of this man who claims to be a prophet? They said: Yes. He said: So I was called among a party of Quraysh, and we entered upon Heraclius and sat with him. He said: Which of you is nearest in kin to this man who claims to be a prophet? Abu Sufyan said: I said: I am. So they seated me before him and my companions behind me. Then he called his interpreter and said: Tell them I am asking this one about this man who claims to be a prophet; if he lies, belie him. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, were it not that lying would be attributed to me, I would have lied. Then he said to his interpreter: Ask him, how is his lineage among you? He said: I said: He is of noble lineage among us. He said: Was any of his fathers a king? He said: I said: No. He said: Did you accuse him of lying before he said it? He said: I said: No. He said: Who follows him, your nobles or your weak? I said: Rather our weak. He said: Do they increase or decrease? He said: I said: No, rather they increase. He said: Does anyone apostatize from his religion after entering it, in displeasure of it? I said: No. He said: Did you fight him? I said: Yes. He said: How is your fighting him? He said: I said: War between us and him is alternate; he gains from us and we gain from him. He said: Does he betray? I said: No, and we are in a truce with him, not knowing what he will do in it. He said: By Allah, he gave me no opening for a word to insert besides this. He said: Did anyone say this saying before him? I said: No. He said to his interpreter: Tell him: I asked you about his lineage, and you said he is of noble lineage among you; thus are messengers sent among the nobility of their people. I asked you whether any of his fathers was a king, and you claimed not; I said: Had one of his fathers been a king, I would say a man seeking his fathers' kingdom. I asked you about his followers, whether the weak or the mighty; you said: Rather the weak, and they are the followers of messengers. I asked you whether you accused him of lying before he said what he said, and you claimed not; so I knew he would not abandon lying against people then go and lie against Allah. I asked you whether anyone turns back from them from his religion after entering it in displeasure of it, and you claimed not; thus is faith when it mingles with the delight of hearts. I asked you whether they increase or decrease, and you claimed they increase; thus is faith, ever growing until complete. I asked you whether you fought him, and you claimed you fought him, with war between you and him alternate, he gaining from you and you gaining from him; thus are messengers tried, then theirs is the outcome. I asked you whether he betrays, and you claimed he does not betray; thus are messengers, they do not betray. I asked you whether anyone said this saying before him, and you claimed not; I said: Had anyone said this saying before him, I would say a man imitating a saying said before him. He said: What does he command you? I said: He commands us prayer, charity, chastity, and kinship. He said: If what you say is true, he is a prophet. I knew he would come forth, but I did not think him of you. Had I known I could reach him, I would have loved to meet him; were I with him I would wash his feet. His kingdom shall surely reach beneath my feet. He said: Then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and read it, and in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, chief of Rome. Peace upon whoever follows guidance. I call you with the call of Islam: submit and be safe; submit and Allah will give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin. And: O People of the Book, come to a word common between us and you, that we worship none but Allah, up to His saying: Bear witness that we are Muslims. When he finished reading the letter, voices rose near him and clamor increased, and he ordered us out. He said: So I said to my companions when we went out: The affair of Ibn Abi Kabshah has become great, until Allah brought Islam upon me. Al-Zuhri said: Then Heraclius called the nobles of Rome and gathered them in a house of his, and said: O Romans, is there for you safety and guidance to the end of time, and that your kingdom endure? He said: They fled like wild asses toward the doors and found them locked. He said: He called them and said: I only tested your firmness upon your religion, and I have seen of you what I loved. So they prostrated to him and were pleased with him."
"9488","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning His saying: If you seek judgment, judgment has come to you. He said: Abu Jahl ibn Hisham sought judgment, saying: O Allah, whichever of us was more wicked to You and more severing of kinship, destroy him today, meaning Muhammad and himself. So Allah killed him on the day of Badr as a disbeliever to the Fire."
"9489","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, in his hadith, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Afterward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fighting in verses of the Qur'an. The first scene the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) witnessed was Badr. The chief of the idolaters that day was Utbah ibn Rabi'ah ibn Abd Shams. They met at Badr on Friday, sixteen or seventeen nights past of Ramadan. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were three hundred and a dozen men, and the idolaters between a thousand and nine hundred. That was the Day of Criterion, and Allah routed the idolaters that day; over seventy chiefs of them were killed, and a like number taken captive. Al-Zuhri said: None witnessed Badr except a Qurayshi, an Ansari, or an ally of one of the two parties."
"9490","From Ma'mar, he said: Ayyub informed me, from Ikrimah, that Abu Sufyan came from Syria with a caravan of Quraysh, and the idolaters went out to rescue their caravan, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out seeking Abu Sufyan and his companions. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent two men of his companions as scouts to see at which water he was. They went until they learned his news, then came swiftly and informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Sufyan came until he halted at the water where the two men had been, and said to the people of the water: Did you sense anyone of Yathrib's people? Have any passed by you? They said: We saw only two men of such-and-such folk. Abu Sufyan said: Where was their halting place? They showed him, and he went until he came to their camels' dung and crumbled it, and in it were date-stones. He said: Whence would the sons of so-and-so have these stones? These are the water-camels' fodder of Yathrib's people. So he left the road and took the seashore path. The two men came and informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his news. He said: Which of you took this road? Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, said: I; he is at such-and-such water, we at such-and-such water; he will move and halt at such-and-such water, we at such-and-such water; then he will halt at such-and-such water, we at such-and-such water; then we shall meet at such-and-such water, as though two racehorses. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marched until he halted at Badr, finding at Badr's water some slaves of Quraysh who had gone out to aid Abu Sufyan. His companions seized them and questioned them; when they told them truth they beat them, and when they lied they left them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed them doing that and said: If they tell you truth you beat them, and when they lie you leave them. Then he called one of them and said: Who feeds the people? He said: So-and-so and so-and-so, naming men, each of whom feeds them a day. He said: How many camels are slaughtered for them? He said: Ten camels. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A camel for a hundred; they are between a thousand and nine hundred. He said: When the idolaters came and drew up against them - and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had consulted before that about fighting them; Abu Bakr arose advising him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated him; then he consulted, and Umar arose advising him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated him; then he consulted them, and Sa'd ibn Ubadah arose and said: O Prophet of Allah, it is as though you hint at us today to learn what is in our hearts. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, if you struck their livers until al-Ghimad sank from Dhu Yumn, we would be with you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heartened his companions to patience and fighting, and was gladdened by that from them. When they met, Utbah ibn Rabi'ah went among Quraysh and said: O my people, obey me and do not fight Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions, for if you fight them there will ever remain hatred between you so long as you live, and unending corruption; a man among you will ever look upon his brother's killer and his cousin's killer. If it be kingship, you will eat in your brother's kingship; if he be a prophet, you are the happiest of people with him; if he be a liar, the roaming Arabs will suffice you against him. They refused to hear his word or obey him. He said: I adjure you by Allah concerning these faces like lamps, that you pit them against these faces like serpents' eyes. Abu Jahl said: You have filled your sorcery with fear. Then he went among Quraysh, then said: Utbah ibn Rabi'ah only advises you thus because his son is with Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his cousin, disliking that his son and cousin be killed. Utbah ibn Rabi'ah grew angry and said: O blackener of his backside, you shall know which of us is more cowardly, baser, and more failing to his people today. Then he dismounted, with his brother Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah and his son al-Walid ibn Utbah, and said: Send forth our peers to us. Men of Banu al-Khazraj sprang up, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated them. Ali, Hamzah, and Ubaydah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf arose. Each man exchanged two blows with his opponent; each killed his man, Hamzah aiding Ali against his man and killing him, while Ubaydah's leg was cut and he died thereafter. The first slain killed of the Muslims was Mihja', the freedman of Umar. Then Allah sent down His aid and routed His enemy. Abu Jahl ibn Hisham was killed, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told and said: Did you do it? They said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. He was gladdened by that and said: My memory of him is a whiteness in his knees; go and see, do you see that? He said: They looked and saw it. He said: And men of Quraysh were taken captive that day. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the slain dragged until thrown into a well. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked over them and said: O Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, O Umayyah ibn Khalaf, naming them by their names one by one: Have you found what your Lord promised true? They said: O Prophet of Allah, do they hear what you say? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You know not what I say better than they; they have seen their deeds. Ma'mar said: And I heard Hisham ibn Urwah narrating that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent that day Zayd ibn Harithah as a bearer of glad tidings to Madinah's people. Men would not believe him, saying: By Allah, he returned only fleeing. He kept telling them of the captives and who was killed, but they disbelieved him until the captives were brought bound in leather straps. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed them."
"9491","He informed us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah and Uthman al-Jazari, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed the captives of Badr, the ransom of each man being four thousand. Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt was killed before the ransom; Ali ibn Abi Talib stood over him and killed him. He said: O Muhammad, who for the children? He said: The Fire."
"9492","From Ma'mar, he said: Uthman al-Jazari informed me, from Miqsam, he said: When al-Abbas was taken captive among the captives on the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard his moaning in bonds. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could not sleep that night, no sleep overtook him. A man of the Ansar perceived it and said: O Messenger of Allah, you have been kept awake since tonight. He said: Al-Abbas, the bonds pained him, and that kept me awake. He said: Shall I not go and loosen something from him? He said: If you wish, do that of yourself. So the Ansari went and loosened his bonds, and he settled and calmed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept."
"9493","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Amr ibn Abi Sufyan al-Thaqafi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a scouting detachment and appointed over them Asim ibn Thabit, the grandfather of Asim ibn Umar. They went until at part of the road between Usfan and Makkah they halted. They were mentioned to a clan of Hudhayl called Banu Lihyan, who followed them with nearly a hundred archers until they saw their traces, until they halted at a stop they knew. They found in it date-stones they recognized as Madinah dates and said: These are of Yathrib dates. They followed their traces until they caught them. When Asim ibn Thabit and his companions sensed them, they took refuge on a hill, and the people surrounded them and said: You have covenant and pledge; if you come down to us we shall kill none of you. Asim ibn Thabit said: As for me, I shall not come down under a disbeliever's protection. O Allah, inform Your Messenger of us. So they fought them until they killed Asim among seven men. Khubayb ibn Adi, Zayd ibn Dathinah, and another man remained. They gave them covenant and pledge if they came down to them, so they came down. When they gained power over them, they untied their bowstrings and bound them with them. The third man with them said: This is the first treachery. He refused to accompany them; they dragged him and he refused to follow them, saying: In these is an example for me. So they struck his neck. They took Khubayb ibn Adi and Zayd ibn Dathinah and sold them in Makkah. The sons of al-Harith ibn Amir ibn Nawfal bought Khubayb, he having killed al-Harith on the day of Badr. He remained with them captive until they resolved to kill him, when he borrowed a razor from one of al-Harith's daughters to shave with. She lent him. She said: I was heedless of a child of mine, and he toddled to him. She said: He took him and placed him on his thigh. When I saw him I was frightened, and he knew it in me, with the razor in his hand. He said: Do you fear I shall kill him? I would never do so, Allah willing. She said: She used to say: I never saw a captive better than Khubayb; I saw him eating from a bunch of grapes when there was no fruit in Makkah that day, though bound in iron; it was only provision Allah provided him. Then they took him out of the Sanctuary to kill him. He said: Let me pray two rak'ahs. He prayed two rak'ahs, then said: Were it not that you think fear of death is in me, I would add. He was the first to institute the two rak'ahs at killing. Then he said: O Allah, count them in number. Then he said: I care not when I am killed a Muslim, on which side my death for Allah is; that is for Allah's sake, and if He wills He blesses the severed limbs of a torn body. Then Uqbah ibn al-Harith arose to him and killed him. He said: And Quraysh sent to Asim that they be brought part of his body by which to know him, for he had killed a great one of their great men. So Allah sent like a cloud of hornets that shielded him from their messengers, and they could take nothing of him."
"9494","From Ma'mar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam the freedman of Ibn Abbas, Ma'mar said: And al-Zuhri narrated part of it to me, he said: Ibn Abi Mu'ayt and Ubayy ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi met; Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt said to Ubayy ibn Khalaf - they being close friends in the Jahiliyyah, and Ubayy ibn Khalaf had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who offered him Islam - when Uqbah heard that, he said: I shall not be pleased with you until you come to Muhammad and spit in his face, revile and belie him. He said: Allah gave him no power over that. On the day of Badr, Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt was taken among the captives, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Ali ibn Abi Talib to kill him. Uqbah said: O Muhammad, am I to be killed apart from these? He said: Yes. He said: Why? He said: For your disbelief, wickedness, and insolence toward Allah and His Messenger. Ma'mar said: And Miqsam said: It reached us, and Allah knows best, that he said: Who for the children? He said: The Fire. He said: Ali ibn Abi Talib arose to him and struck his neck. As for Ubayy ibn Khalaf, he said: By Allah, I shall surely kill Muhammad. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Rather I shall kill him, Allah willing. He said: A man who heard that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to Ubayy ibn Khalaf, and it was said: When Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told what you said, he said: Rather I shall kill him, Allah willing. That frightened him, and he said: I adjure you by Allah, did you hear him say that? He said: Yes. It entered his soul, for they never heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say a word but it was true. On the day of Uhud, Ubayy ibn Khalaf went out with the idolaters, seeking a heedless moment of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to charge him, while a Muslim would come between him and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw that, he said to his companions: Leave him. He took the spear and struck him with it, meaning he cast it at him, landing in his collarbone beneath the helmet's skirt above the armor. Not much blood came out, and the blood congealed in his belly. He began bellowing like a bull. His companions came and carried him while he bellowed, saying: What is this? By Allah, you have only a scratch. He said: By Allah, had only his spittle struck me it would have killed me. Did he not say: I shall kill him, Allah willing? By Allah, had what is in me been in the people of Dhu al-Majaz it would have killed them. He said: He lingered only a day or so until he died to the Fire. Allah sent down concerning him: And the day the wrongdoer bites his hands, up to His saying: Satan is to man a deserter."
"9495","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, in his hadith, from Urwah: Then came the campaign of Banu al-Nadir, a party of Jews, six months after the battle of Badr. Their dwellings and palms were in a quarter of Madinah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besieged them until they came down upon exile, with what their camels could carry of goods and property except arms, meaning weapons. Allah sent down concerning them: All that is in the heavens and earth glorifies Allah, and He is the Mighty, the Wise. He it is Who expelled the disbelievers of the People of the Book from their homes at the first gathering. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought them until he made peace with them upon exile, exiling them to Syria. They were of a tribe untouched by exile before, though Allah had decreed exile upon them; but for that He would have punished them in this world with killing and captivity. As for His saying: at the first gathering, their exile was the first gathering in this world to Syria."
"9496","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, from a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The disbelievers of Quraysh wrote to Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn al-Salul and the idol-worshippers of Aws and Khazraj, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Madinah before the battle of Badr, saying: You have sheltered our man; you are the most numerous people of Madinah; we swear by Allah, you shall either kill him or expel him, or we shall seek aid against you from the Arabs, then march to you all together until we kill your fighters and violate your women. When that reached Ibn Ubayy and the idol-worshippers with him, they corresponded, gathered, dispatched, and resolved to fight the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions. When that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he met them in a company and said: The threat of Quraysh has reached its limit among you; it would not plot against you more than you plot against yourselves. Here you are, wanting to kill your sons and brothers. When they heard that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they dispersed. That reached the disbelievers of Quraysh, and the battle of Badr occurred. After Badr the disbelievers of Quraysh wrote to the Jews: You are people of arms and fortresses; you shall surely fight our man, or we shall do such-and-such, and nothing shall stand between us and your women's anklets, meaning anklets. When their letter reached the Jews, Banu al-Nadir resolved upon treachery. They sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Come out to us with thirty men of your companions, and we shall come out with thirty rabbis, meeting in such-and-such place halfway between us and you, that they may hear from you; if they affirm and believe you, we shall all believe. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with thirty of his companions, and thirty Jewish rabbis went out to him, until they emerged in open land. Some Jews said to others: How can you get at him with thirty of his companions, each loving to die before him? So they sent to him: How can we understand with sixty men? Come out with three of your companions, and three of our scholars shall come out to you, to hear from you; if they believe you, we shall all believe and affirm you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with three of his companions, concealing daggers, intending to strike the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) treacherously. A well-wishing woman of Banu al-Nadir sent to her brother, a Muslim man of the Ansar, informing him of Banu al-Nadir's intended treachery against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Her brother came swiftly until he reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him their news secretly before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned back. Next morning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon them with the troops and besieged them, saying to them: You are not safe with me except by a covenant you make with me. They refused to give him a covenant, so he and the Muslims fought them that day. Then next morning he went to Banu Qurayzah with cavalry and troops, leaving Banu al-Nadir, calling them to covenant with him, and they covenanted him. He turned from them and went to Banu al-Nadir with the troops, fighting them until they came down upon exile, with what camels could carry except arms, and arms are weapons. Banu al-Nadir came and carried what a camel could carry of their goods, their houses' doors and timber; they would ruin their houses, demolishing them and carrying what suited them of its wood. Their exile was the first gathering of people to Syria. Banu al-Nadir were of a tribe of Israel's tribes untouched by exile since Allah decreed exile upon Israel, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exiled them. But for what Allah decreed upon them of exile, He would have punished them in this world as Banu Qurayzah were punished. Allah sent down: All that is in the heavens and earth glorifies Allah, until he reached: And Allah over all things is Powerful. The palms of Banu al-Nadir belonged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exclusively; Allah gave them to him and singled him with them, saying: And what Allah restored to His Messenger from them, you urged upon it neither horse nor camel, meaning without fighting. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave most of it to the Emigrants, dividing it among them, and to two needy men of the Ansar, dividing to no other Ansari besides them. What remained was the charity of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hands of Fatimah's descendants."
"9497","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard Ikrimah told me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Makkah fifteen years, of which four or five calling to Islam secretly, in fear, until Allah sent against the men concerning whom was sent down: Indeed We suffice you against the mockers, those who made the Qur'an shreds. And al-idin in Quraysh's tongue is sorcery; the sorceress is called adihah. So he was commanded to oppose them, saying: Proclaim what you are commanded and turn from the idolaters. Then he was commanded to go out to Madinah, arriving eight nights past of Rabi' al-Awwal. Then came the battle of Badr; concerning them Allah sent down: And when Allah promised you one of the two parties, and concerning them was sent down: The host shall be routed, and concerning them was sent down: Until when We seized their affluent with punishment, and concerning them was sent down: That He might cut off a part of the disbelievers, and concerning them was sent down: You have nothing of the affair, meaning the people, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted the caravan. And concerning them was sent down: Have you not seen those who exchanged Allah's favor for disbelief, and concerning them was sent down: Have you not seen those who went out of their homes, and concerning them was sent down: There was a sign for you in the two hosts that met, concerning the caravan, the riders below you taking the valley's bottom; all this concerning Badr's people. Two months before Badr was the detachment on the day al-Hadrami was killed. Then came Uhud, then the day of the Confederates two years after Uhud, then al-Hudaybiyyah, the day of the Tree. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with them that he would perform Umrah next year in this month; concerning it was sent down: The sacred month for the sacred month, the month of the first year for the month of the second year; and retaliation for sanctities. Then came the Conquest after the Umrah; concerning it was sent down: Until when We opened upon them a gate of severe punishment, lo, they therein despair, for the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided them unprepared for fighting. Four men of Quraysh were killed, and of their allies of Banu Bakr fifty or more. And concerning them was sent down, when they entered Allah's religion: And it is He Who produced for you hearing and sight. Then he went out to Hunayn after twenty nights, then to Taif, then returned to Madinah, then appointed Abu Bakr over the Hajj. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj next year, then bade people farewell, then returned and died two nights past of Rabi'. When Abu Bakr returned from Hajj, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Tabuk."
"9498","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, in his hadith, from Urwah, he said: The battle of Uhud was in Shawwal, six months after the battle of Banu al-Nadir. Al-Zuhri said, from Urwah, concerning His saying: And you disobeyed after He showed you what you love: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Uhud when Abu Sufyan and Quraysh's disbelievers raided: I saw as though I wore a strong coat of mail, and I interpreted it as Madinah; so stay in your dwellings and fight from behind them. Madinah had been knit with buildings, like a fortress. A man who had not witnessed Badr said: O Messenger of Allah, go out with us to them to fight them. Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul said: Yes, by Allah, O Prophet of Allah, what I saw: never did an enemy alight upon us and we went out to him without him striking among us, nor did we stay in Madinah fighting from behind it without routing our enemy. Some Muslims spoke to him, saying: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, go out with us to them. So he called for his armor and wore it, then said: I think the slain shall be many among you and them; I see in sleep slaughtered ones, and I say: cattle, by Allah, with good. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you, stay with us. He said: It is not fitting for a prophet, once he wears his armor, to lay it down until he meets the people. Is there a man to guide us to the people by a near path? Guides went before him until at al-Shawt of al-Jabbanah, Abdullah ibn Ubayy withdrew with a third of the army, or near a third. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went until they met them at Uhud and drew up. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had enjoined his companions, if they routed them, not to enter their camp nor pursue them. When they met they routed them, but disobeyed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), disputed and differed. Then Allah turned them from them to try them, as Allah said. The idolaters advanced with Khalid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah over their cavalry, killing seventy Muslims and wounding them grievously. The front tooth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was broken and his face bloodied, until Satan shouted at the top of his voice: Muhammad is killed. Ka'b ibn Malik said: I was the first to recognize the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I knew his eyes behind the helmet, and called at my highest voice: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He gestured to me to be silent. Allah held back the idolaters while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions stood. After some of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) companions were mutilated, noses cut, some with bellies ripped, Abu Sufyan called: You shall find among your slain some mutilation; that was not by our counselors or chiefs. Then Abu Sufyan said: Exalted be Hubal. Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Allah is higher and greater. He said: A gladdened eye; slain for Badr's slain. Umar said: The slain are not equal; our slain in Paradise, your slain in the Fire. Abu Sufyan said: Then we have lost. Then they turned back, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called his companions to pursue them until they reached near Hamra al-Asad. Among those pursuing that day was Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. That is when Allah said: Those to whom people said: People have gathered against you, so fear them, but it increased them in faith, and they said: Allah suffices us, an excellent Trustee."
"9499","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, in his hadith: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the Mosque, he called the Muslims to pursue the disbelievers, and they responded, pursuing them most of their day. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned with them. Allah sent down: Those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after wounds befell them. And Abd al-Razzaq informed us that the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was struck that day with the sword seventy blows, and Allah shielded him from all their evil."
"9500","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departed until he reached Madinah, he raided Khaybar from al-Hudaybiyyah. Allah sent down upon him: Allah promised you many spoils you shall take, so He hastened this for you, up to: and guides you a straight path. When Khaybar was conquered he assigned it to those who had raided with him at al-Hudaybiyyah and pledged beneath the Tree, absent and present, because Allah had promised it to them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the fifth of Khaybar, dividing the rest as spoils among the Muslims who witnessed it and those absent of Hudaybiyyah's people. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions had no workers to work Khaybar or sow it. Al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Khaybar's Jews, who had gone out to depart from it, and handed Khaybar to them to work for half, delivering it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: I settle you upon that so long as Allah settles you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send Abdullah ibn Rawahah al-Ansari to them, who would estimate the palms when the first dates ripened, before any was eaten, then tell the Jews: take them at that estimate or hand them over at that estimate? Al-Zuhri said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Umrah in Dhu al-Qa'dah of the truce between him and Quraysh, and they vacated it for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leaving Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza al-Qurashi then al-Adawi, ordering that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had circumambulated thrice, he come and order him to depart. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had made peace with them to stay three days circumambulating the House. After three days the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Huwaytib, who spoke to him of departing, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departed returning to Madinah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided the Conquest, the conquest of Makkah. Al-Zuhri said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah informed me, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in Ramadan from Madinah with ten thousand Muslims, eight and a half years after his arrival in Madinah. He marched with the Muslims to Makkah, fasting with them, until he reached al-Kadid between Usfan and Qudayd, where he broke fast and the Muslims with him, fasting nothing of Ramadan's remainder. Al-Zuhri said: Breaking fast was the latter of the two matters; what is taken of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affair is the latter after the latter. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Makkah on the night of the thirteenth past of Ramadan."
"9501","From Ma'mar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam, Ma'mar said: And Uthman al-Jazari was called al-Mushahid, from Miqsam the freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: When the truce between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Quraysh in the time of al-Hudaybiyyah - it was years, he mentioned - there was war between Banu Bakr, allies of Quraysh, and Khuza'ah, allies of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Quraysh aided their allies against Khuza'ah. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I shall surely defend them as I defend myself and my household. He began preparing against them. That reached Quraysh, who said to Abu Sufyan: What will you do, with these armies preparing against us? Go and renew a document between us and Muhammad. That was his arrival from Syria. Abu Sufyan went out until he reached Madinah, and spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Come, let us renew a document between us and you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are upon our affair as it was; have you brought any new event? Abu Sufyan said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then we are upon our affair as it was between us. He came to Ali ibn Abi Talib and said: Will you, ruling the Arabs and favoring your people, grant them protection and renew a document for them? He said: I would never decide a matter over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he entered upon Fatimah and said: Will you be the best young she-camel among the Arabs, granting protection between people? Your sister has granted protection to her husband Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi' before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he did not change it. Fatimah said: I would never decide a matter over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said that to al-Hasan and al-Husayn: Grant protection between people; say: Yes. They said nothing, looking at their mother, saying: We say what our mother says. He gained nothing he sought from any of them. He went out until he reached Quraysh, who said: What have you brought? He said: I come to you from people whose hearts are upon one heart. By Allah, I left neither small nor great, neither female nor male among them without speaking to him, gaining nothing from them. They said: You have done nothing; return. He returned, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out toward Quraysh. At part of the road, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to some Ansar: Watch for Abu Sufyan; you shall find him. They watched and found him. When he entered the camp, the Muslims crowded him, hastening to him. He called: O Muhammad, I am slain. He was ordered into al-Abbas's care, al-Abbas being his close friend in the Jahiliyyah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to al-Abbas, and he spent the night with him. At dawn prayer, when the caller called, people stirred, and he thought they meant him. He said: O Abbas, what is wrong with people? He said: They stirred for the caller to prayer. He said: All these stirred only for Muhammad's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caller? He said: Yes. Al-Abbas arose for prayer and he with him. When they finished, he said: O Abbas, does Muhammad do nothing without them doing the like? He said: Yes; were he to order them to abandon food and drink until they died of hunger, they would. I see them destroying your people tomorrow. He said: O Abbas, take us in to him. He entered to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a leather tent, with Umar ibn al-Khattab behind the tent. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began offering him Islam. Abu Sufyan said: What shall I do with al-Uzza? Umar said from behind the tent: Defecate upon her. He said: By your father, you are foul-mouthed; I did not come to you, O son of al-Khattab, I came only to my cousin, speaking to him. He said: Al-Abbas said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a noble of our people and their elders, and I love that you grant him something by which that is known for him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever enters Abu Sufyan's house is safe. He said: Abu Sufyan said: My house? My house? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; whoever lays down his weapon is safe, and whoever locks his door is safe. He went with al-Abbas until at part of the road al-Abbas feared some treachery from him, seating him on a hill until the troops passed him. He said: A squadron passed, and he said: Who are these, O Abbas? He said: This is al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam on the right flank. He said: Then another squadron passed, and he said: Who are these, O Abbas? He said: They are Quda'ah under Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. He said: Then another squadron passed, and he said: Who are these, O Abbas? He said: This is Khalid ibn al-Walid on the left flank. He said: Then people passed walking in iron, and he said: Who are these, O Abbas, like a black lava-field? He said: These are the Ansar; with them is red death; among them is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the Ansar around him. Abu Sufyan said: Go, O Abbas; I never saw a morning of a people in their homes like today. He said: Then he went, and overlooking Makkah he called - Quraysh's slogan being O Al Ghalib, submit and be safe - when his wife Hind met him, seizing his beard, saying: O Al Ghalib, kill the foolish old man, for he has apostatized. He said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall surely submit, or your neck shall be struck. He said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) overlooked Makkah, he held people from entering until al-Abbas's messenger came to him. He was slow, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps they do to al-Abbas what Thaqif did to Urwah ibn Mas'ud; by Allah, then I shall spare none of them. He said: Then al-Abbas's messenger came to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, ordering his companions to hold back, saying: Hold back weapons except Khuza'ah against Bakr for a while. Then he ordered them and they held back. He granted safety to all people except Ibn Abi Sarh, Ibn Khatal, Miqyas al-Kinani, and another woman. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I did not forbid Makkah, but Allah forbade it. It was not permitted to anyone before me, nor is permitted to anyone after me until the Day of Resurrection; Allah permitted it to me only for an hour of the day. He said: Then Uthman ibn Affan brought him Ibn Abi Sarh, saying: Pledge him, O Messenger of Allah. He turned from him. Then he came from another side and he turned from him. Then he came again, saying: Pledge him, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I turned from him, thinking some of you would kill him. A man of the Ansar said: Why did you not wink to me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: A prophet does not wink, as though he saw it treachery. Al-Zuhri said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Khalid ibn al-Walid, who fought with those with him the ranks of Quraysh in lower Makkah until Allah routed them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered, and it was lifted from them; they entered the religion. Allah sent down: When Allah's help and victory come, to its end. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned with those with him of Quraysh - Kinanah and those who embraced Islam on the day of the Conquest - before Hunayn. Hunayn is a valley before Taif with waters, with that day the aged of Hawazin's idolaters, with them Thaqif. The chief of the idolaters that day was Malik ibn Awf al-Nasri. They fought at Hunayn, and Allah aided His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Muslims. It was a hard day upon people. Allah sent down: Allah aided you in many fields, and on the day of Hunayn. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to conciliate them; for that he sent Khalid ibn al-Walid that day."
"9502","From Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah on the day of the Conquest wearing the helmet."
"9503","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Kathir ibn al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib informed me, from his father al-Abbas, he said: I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the day of Hunayn. He said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with none but me and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib. We stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not parting him, while he was upon a grey mule - Ma'mar sometimes said: white - gifted to him by Farwah ibn Nu'amah al-Judhami. He said: When the Muslims met the disbelievers, the Muslims turned fleeing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began spurring his mule toward the disbelievers. Al-Abbas said: I held the bridle of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mule, checking it, while he pressed swiftly toward the idolaters, with Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith holding the stirrup of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O Abbas, call the companions of the acacia tree. He said: I was a loud-voiced man, so I called at my highest voice: Where are the companions of the acacia tree? He said: By Allah, their turning when they heard my voice was like cows' turning to their calves, saying: At your service, at your service. The Muslims advanced and fought them with the disbelievers. The Ansar called out, saying: O company of Ansar. Then the call narrowed to Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, calling: O Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked from upon his mule, craning to their fighting, and said: This is when the oven grows hot. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took pebbles and cast them at the disbelievers' faces, then said: Routed, by the Lord of the Ka'bah. He said: I went to look, and the fighting was as I saw it. He said: By Allah, no sooner had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast his pebbles than I saw their edge dulled and their affair retreating, until Allah routed them. He said: It is as though I see the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) galloping after them upon his mule. Al-Zuhri said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar used to narrate that Khalid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah that day was over the cavalry of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Azhar said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Allah routed the disbelievers and the Muslims returned to their baggage, walking among the Muslims, saying: Who will guide me to Khalid ibn al-Walid's baggage? So I walked - or he said: hastened - before him, a youth just pubescent, saying: Who will guide to Khalid's baggage? Until we were guided to him, and there was Khalid leaning against his saddle's rear. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and looked at his wound. Al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took captive that day six thousand captives of women and boys. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put Abu Sufyan ibn Harb over them. Al-Zuhri said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, he said: When Hawazin returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they said: You are the most dutiful and joining of people; our retainers, women, and property were taken captive. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I waited for you, with whom you see with me. The truest speech to me is the most truthful. Choose one of the two parties: either property or captives. They said: O Messenger of Allah, since you give us choice between property and honor, we choose honor - or he said: we would not equate anything with honor. So they chose their women and children. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arose and addressed the Muslims, praising Allah as He deserves, then said: To proceed: these your brothers have come as Muslims, or submitting. We gave them choice between offspring and property, and they equated nothing with honor. I have seen fit to return to them their children and women. Whoever of you loves to willingly grant that, let him do so; whoever loves that his share of that be recorded against us until we give him of what Allah bestows upon us, let him do so. He said: The Muslims said: We willingly grant that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I know not who of you permits that and who does not; order your headmen to raise that to us. When the headmen raised to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that people had surrendered that and permitted it, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to Hawazin their women and children. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave choice to women he had granted to Qurayshi men, between staying with whoever had them and returning to their families. Al-Zuhri said: It reached me that a woman among them under Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf chose to return to her family, leaving Abd al-Rahman, though he admired her; another with Safwan ibn Umayyah chose her family. Al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided what he divided among the Muslims, then performed Umrah from al-Ji'ranah after returning from Hunayn's campaign, then set out to Madinah. Then he appointed Abu Bakr over that Hajj. Ma'mar said, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, he said: Mula'ib al-Asinnah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gift, and he offered him Islam, but he refused to submit. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not accept a polytheist's gift. He said: Send to Najd's people whom you wish, and I am their protector. So he sent to them a party: al-Mundhir ibn Amr, called al-Mu'niq to die, among them Amir ibn Fuhayrah. Amir ibn al-Tufayl incited Banu Amir against them, but they refused to obey him or betray Mula'ib al-Asinnah. He said: So he incited Banu Sulaym against them, and they obeyed and followed them with nearly a hundred archers, catching them at Bi'r Ma'unah and killing them except Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, whom they released. Al-Zuhri said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that when he returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Were you apart from them? Al-Zuhri said: And it reached me that when they buried them, they sought Amir ibn Fuhayrah's body and could not find it; they hold the angels buried him."
"9504","From Ma'mar, he said: Thumamah ibn Abdullah ibn Anas informed us, from Anas ibn Malik, that Haram ibn Milhan, Anas's maternal uncle, was stabbed that day; he received his blood in his palm, then sprinkled it upon his head and face, saying: I have won, by the Lord of the Ka'bah. Ma'mar said: And Asim informed me that Anas ibn Malik said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grieve over anything as he grieved over the companions of Bi'r Ma'unah and the detachment of al-Mundhir ibn Amr. He spent a month praying against those who struck them in the qunut of dawn prayer, praying against Ri'l, Dhakwan, Usayyah, and Lihyan of Banu Sulaym."
"9505","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, in his hadith, from Urwah, he said: When the Muslims grew many and faith prevailed, the idolatrous disbelievers of Quraysh spoke of those of their tribes who believed, tormenting and imprisoning them, wanting to turn them from their religion. He said: When it reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to those who believed in him: Disperse in the land, they said: Where shall we go, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Here, pointing with his hand toward Abyssinia's land, the dearest land to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to emigrate toward. So people emigrated in number, some with their families, some alone, until they reached Abyssinia. Al-Zuhri said: Ja'far ibn Abi Talib went out in the emigration with his wife Asma bint Umays al-Khath'amiyyah, and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah have mercy on him, with his wife Ruqayyah daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-As went out with his wife Umaymah bint Khalaf. Abu Salamah went out with his wife Umm Salamah bint Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah. A man of Quraysh went out with their women. Abdullah ibn Ja'far was born there, and Amah daughter of Khalid ibn Sa'id, mother of Amr ibn al-Zubayr and Khalid ibn al-Zubayr, was born there, and al-Harith ibn Hatib among Quraysh born there. Al-Zuhri said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Aishah said: I never knew my parents but as professing the religion. No day passed us without the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) coming to us at both ends of the day, morning and evening. When the Muslims were tried, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, went out emigrating toward Abyssinia until at Bark al-Ghimad he met Ibn al-Daghinah, chief of al-Qarah. Ibn al-Daghinah said: Where do you intend, O Abu Bakr? Abu Bakr said: My people expelled me; I wish to wander the earth worshipping my Lord. Ibn al-Daghinah said: One like you, O Abu Bakr, is neither expelled nor goes out. You earn for the destitute, uphold kinship, bear the burdened, honor the guest, and aid in calamities of truth. I am your protector; return and worship your Lord in your land. So Ibn al-Daghinah departed, returning with Abu Bakr, and toured Quraysh's disbelievers, saying: Abu Bakr went out, and one like him does not go out. Would you expel a man who earns for the destitute, upholds kinship, bears the burdened, honors the guest, and aids in calamities of truth? Quraysh honored Ibn al-Daghinah's protection, securing Abu Bakr, telling Ibn al-Daghinah: Order Abu Bakr to worship his Lord in his house, praying therein as he wishes, neither harming us nor proclaiming prayer and recitation outside his house. He did so. Then it occurred to Abu Bakr to build a mosque in his house's courtyard, praying and reciting therein. The idolaters' women and children crowded over him, marveling and watching. Abu Bakr was a weeping man, unable to hold his tears when reciting the Qur'an. That alarmed Quraysh's nobles, who sent to Ibn al-Daghinah. He came to them, and they said: We only protected Abu Bakr to worship Allah in his house; he has exceeded that, building a mosque in his courtyard, proclaiming prayer and recitation. We feared he would seduce our women and children. Go to him; order him: if he loves to confine himself to worshipping Allah in his house, let him; if he refuses but to proclaim that, ask him to return your protection to you, for we dislike betraying you and do not approve Abu Bakr's proclamation. Aishah said: Ibn al-Daghinah came to Abu Bakr and said: O Abu Bakr, you know what I contracted for you: either confine yourself to that, or return my protection to me, for I dislike Arabs hearing I was betrayed in a man's covenant I contracted. Abu Bakr said: I return your protection to you, content with Allah and His Messenger's protection. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Makkah that day. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Muslims: I have been shown your emigration land; I was shown a salty land with palms between two lava-fields, the two Harrahs. Whoever emigrated emigrated toward Madinah when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned that. Some Muslims who had emigrated to Abyssinia returned to Madinah. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, prepared to emigrate. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wait, for I hope permission is given me. Abu Bakr said: Do you hope that, O Prophet of Allah? He said: Yes. So Abu Bakr held himself back for the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) company, feeding two riding camels he had with acacia leaves for four months. Al-Zuhri said: Urwah said: Aishah said: While we sat one day in our house at midday, one said to Abu Bakr: Here is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) coming, his head covered, at an hour he never came to us. Abu Bakr said: May my father and mother be ransom for him; he comes at this hour only for a matter. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, asked permission, and was admitted. Abu Bakr said: They are only your family, may my father be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Permission has been given me to go out. Abu Bakr said: Companionship, may my father be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. Abu Bakr said: Take, may my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah, one of these two riding camels of mine. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the price. Aishah said: We equipped them most swiftly, preparing travel food in a bag. Asma bint Abi Bakr cut part of her girdle and tied the bag with it; for that she was called Dhat al-Nitaqayn. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr reached a cave in a mountain called Thawr, staying three nights. Ma'mar said: Uthman al-Jazari informed me that Miqsam the freedman of Ibn Abbas informed him concerning His saying: And when the disbelievers plotted against you to confine you. He said: Quraysh consulted at Makkah. Some said: In the morning confine him in bonds, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Some said: Rather kill him. Some said: Expel him. Allah informed His Prophet of that. Ali slept upon the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bed that night, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out until he reached the cave. The idolaters spent the night guarding Ali, thinking him the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In the morning they rose against him; seeing Ali, Allah foiled their plot. They said: Where is this companion of yours? He said: I know not. They tracked him; reaching the mountain, the matter confused them. They climbed the mountain, passing the cave, seeing a spider's web upon its door. They said: Had he entered here, no spider's web would be upon its door. He stayed therein three nights. Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: They entered Dar al-Nadwah plotting against the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Let none enter with you not of you. Satan entered with them in the form of a Najdi elder. Some said: You have no watcher from this one; this is a man of Najd. He said: They consulted. A man among them said: I see you mount him upon a camel and expel him. Satan said: Evil is this one's view; here he corrupted between you while among you, so how when you expel him and he corrupts people, then brings them against you to fight you? They said: Excellent is this elder's view. Another said: I see you put him in a house, mudding its door, leaving him therein until he dies. Satan said: Evil is this one's view; do you think his people would ever leave him therein? They must rage for him and release him. Abu Jahl said: I see you take from each tribe a man, then take their swords and strike him one blow, none knowing who killed him, then pay blood-money. Satan said: Excellent is this one's view. Allah informed His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that. He and Abu Bakr went out to a cave in a mountain called Thawr, while Ali slept upon the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bed. They spent the night guarding him, thinking him the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In the morning Ali arose for dawn prayer, and they rushed to him; there was Ali. They said: Where is your companion? He said: I know not. They tracked him until they reached the cave, then returned. He and Abu Bakr stayed therein three nights. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said in his hadith from Urwah: They stayed therein three nights; Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, a clever perceptive youth, would spend the night with them, leaving them at dawn to be with Quraysh in Makkah as though overnight there, hearing no plot against them without retaining it until bringing them its news when darkness mingled. Amir ibn Fuhayrah, Abu Bakr's freedman, would pasture a milch ewe, bringing it to them when part of the night passed; they would spend the night in its milk until Amir ibn Fuhayrah called it away at dawn. He did that each of the three nights. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr hired a man of Banu al-Dil of Banu Abd ibn Adi, a skilled guide - al-khirrit is the expert in guidance - who had dipped his oath in Al al-As ibn Wail's alliance, upon Quraysh disbelievers' religion. They trusted him, handing him their riding camels, promising him Thawr's cave after three nights. He came to their cave with their camels on the morning of three nights. They departed with Amir ibn Fuhayrah, Abu Bakr's freedman, and the Dili guide. He took them by the Adhakhir road, the coastal road. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik al-Mudliji informed me - the nephew of Suraqah ibn Ju'shum - that his father informed him he heard Suraqah say: Messengers of Quraysh disbelievers came to us offering for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr the blood-money of each for whoever killed or captured them. He said: While I sat in a gathering of my people of Banu Mudlij, a man among them came until he stood over us and said: O Suraqah, I just saw dark shapes by the coast, I think Muhammad and his companions. Suraqah said: I knew they were they, but said: They are not they; you saw so-and-so and so-and-so gone seeking strays. He said: I stayed in the gathering only an hour until I arose, entered my house, ordered my maid to bring my horse behind a hill, keeping it for me. I took my spear, leaving by the house's back, trailing its butt upon the ground, lowering the spear, until I reached my horse and mounted. I urged it trotting until I saw their dark shapes. When I drew near where sound is heard, my horse stumbled and I fell. I arose, reaching to my quiver, drawing divining arrows to cast whether to harm them or not. Out came what I hate: not to harm them. So I mounted my horse, disobeying the arrows, urging it trotting again until I drew near, hearing the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recitation - he not turning, Abu Bakr much turning - when my horse's forelegs sank into the earth up to the knees. I fell from her, urged her, and she rose, her forelegs hardly emerging. When she stood upright, at her forelegs' trace was dust rising in the sky like smoke. Ma'mar said: I said to Abu Amr ibn al-Ala: What is al-uthan? He was silent a while, then said: It is smoke without fire. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said in his hadith: I cast the divining arrows, and out came what I hate: not to harm them. I called to them for safety, and they halted. I mounted my horse until I reached them. It had entered my soul, from what befell me of being held from them, that the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affair would prevail. I said to him: Your people offered blood-money for you. I told them of my journey's news and what people intended for them. I offered them provision and goods, but they took nothing from me, asking only to conceal us. I asked him to write me a covenant of truce for my safety. He ordered Amir ibn Fuhayrah, who wrote it for me on a leather piece. Then he went on. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that he met al-Zubayr and a party of Muslims, Madinah merchants in Syria returning to Makkah, who offered the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr white garments; it is said they clothed them, giving them. The Muslims in Madinah heard of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departure, going out each morning to the Harrah awaiting him until midday heat harmed them. One day after long waiting they turned to their houses, when a Jew atop one of their forts, looking for a matter, saw the Messenger of Allah and his companions in white, the mirage shifting with them. The Jew could not but call at his highest voice: O Arabs, this is your fortune you await. The Muslims rushed to arms, meeting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until they reached him at the Harrah's outskirts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned them rightward until he halted at Banu Amr ibn Awf, that Monday of Rabi' al-Awwal. Abu Bakr would remind people, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat silent. Ansar newcomers who had never seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mistook him for Abu Bakr, until the sun struck the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr came shading him with his cloak, and people knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed at Banu Amr ibn Awf over ten nights, building the mosque founded upon piety, praying therein. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted his riding camel, marching with people until it knelt at the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mosque in Madinah, where Muslim men prayed that day. It was a drying place for dates of Sahl and Suhayl, two orphan brother boys under Abu Umamah As'ad ibn Zurarah of Banu al-Najjar's care. When his riding camel knelt, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the dwelling, Allah willing. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the two boys, bargaining for the drying place to make it a mosque. They said: Rather we grant it you, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to accept it as gift until he bought it from them, building it a mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began carrying bricks with them in his garments, saying: This carrying is not Khaybar's carrying; this is more dutiful, our Lord, and purer. And saying: O Allah, reward is the Hereafter's reward, so mercy upon the Ansar and Emigrants. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite verses of an unnamed Muslim man; it never reached me in hadiths that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ever recited a complete verse of poetry except these lines, but he would chant them for the mosque's building. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought Quraysh's disbelievers, war barred Abyssinia's emigrants from coming to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until they met him in Madinah at the Trench's time. Asma bint Umays used to narrate that Umar ibn al-Khattab would taunt them for staying in Abyssinia. Asma mentioned that, she claimed, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: You are not so. The first verse sent down concerning fighting was: Permission is given to those fought because they were wronged, and Allah over aiding them is Powerful."
"9506","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, from his father, he said: I never stayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any campaign he raided until the campaign of Tabuk, except Badr. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) blamed no one staying behind Badr; he had only gone out seeking the caravan, and Quraysh went out to rescue their caravan, meeting without appointment, as Allah said. By my life, the noblest of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) scenes among people is Badr. I would not love to have witnessed in place of my pledge on the night of al-Aqabah, when we covenanted upon Islam. Then I never stayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any campaign he raided until the campaign of Tabuk, his last campaign. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced to people the departure, wanting them to prepare a campaign's preparation when shades were pleasant and fruits ripe. He seldom intended a campaign without hinting otherwise, saying: War is deceit. In Tabuk's campaign the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted people to prepare fully. I was at my easiest, having gathered my mounts, most able in myself for jihad and light of gear. Yet I inclined to shades and ripe fruits. I remained so until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arose departing in the morning, that Thursday - he loved to go out on Thursday - departing in the morning. I said: I shall go tomorrow to the market and buy my gear, then join them. I went to the market next day, and part of my affair proved hard for me also. I said: I shall return tomorrow, Allah willing. I remained so until sin enveloped me and I stayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I would walk in the markets, touring Madinah, grieving to see none but a man accused of hypocrisy. No one stayed behind but thought it would be hidden for him; people were many, no register gathering them. All who stayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were eighty-odd men. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mention me until he reached Tabuk. At Tabuk he said: What did Ka'b ibn Malik do? A man of my people said: His two cloaks and looking at his sides kept him, O Messenger of Allah. Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: Evil is what you said. By Allah, O Prophet of Allah, we know of him only good. He said: While they were so, a man appeared shifting in the mirage. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be Abu Khaythamah. And it was Abu Khaythamah. He said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished Tabuk's campaign and returned, nearing Madinah, I began looking by what to escape the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anger, seeking aid thereon from every judicious kinsman, until it was said the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would reach you tomorrow morning, when falsehood left me. I knew I would be saved only by truth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered in the forenoon, praying two rak'ahs in the Mosque - when coming from travel he would do that, entering the Mosque and praying two rak'ahs therein - then sitting. Those staying behind began coming, swearing to him, excusing themselves; he would seek forgiveness for them, accepting their outward, leaving their inward to Allah. I entered the Mosque, and there he sat. When he saw me he smiled an angry smile. I came and sat before him. He said: Had you not bought your mount? I said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. He said: What kept you behind? I said: By Allah, had before me any other people sat, I would have escaped his anger upon me with an excuse; I have been given argument. I know, O Prophet of Allah, that if I tell you today a word you find against me in, though true, I hope Allah's pardon; and if I narrate to you today a tale you are pleased with me in, though a lie, Allah may soon disclose you to it. By Allah, O Prophet of Allah, I was never easier or lighter of gear than when I stayed behind you. He said: As for this, he has told you truth. Arise until Allah judges concerning you. I arose, and men of my people sprang after me reproaching me, saying: By Allah, we know you committed no sin before this; would that you had excused yourself to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with an excuse he would be pleased with you in; the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking forgiveness would have followed that. You stood a stand, knowing not what is judged for you therein. They kept reproaching me until I intended to return and belie myself. I said: Did anyone else say this saying? They said: Yes; Hilal ibn Umayyah and Murarah ibn al-Rabi' said it. They named two righteous men who had witnessed Badr, in them an example for me. I said: No, by Allah, I shall never return to him in this nor belie myself. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade people speaking to us three. He said: I would go out to the market, none speaking to me. People changed to us until not those we know; walls changed to us until not the walls we know for us; earth changed to us until not the earth we know. I was the strongest of people, going out to the market, coming to the Mosque, entering, coming to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeting him, saying: Did he move his lips with greeting? When I arose praying toward a pillar, looking before my prayer, he would look at me from his eyes' corner; when I looked at him he turned from me. He said: My two companions submitted, weeping night and day, never raising their heads. While I toured the market, a Christian man came with food to sell, saying: Who will guide me to Ka'b ibn Malik? He said: People pointed him to me. He came to me with a letter from Ghassan's king, in it: To proceed: It reached me your companion has forsaken and banished you; you are not in a land of waste or humiliation, so join us and we shall console you. He said: I said: This too is trial and evil. So I heated the oven with it, burning it therein. When forty nights passed, a messenger of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me, saying: Withdraw from your wife. I said: Divorce her? He said: No, but do not approach her. He said: Hilal ibn Umayyah's wife came, saying: O Prophet of Allah, Hilal ibn Umayyah is a great weak old man; will you permit me to serve him? He said: Yes, but let him not approach you. She said: O Prophet of Allah, by Allah, he has no movement for anything; he has remained prostrate weeping night and day since his affair was what it was. Ka'b said: When trial lengthened upon me, I leapt over Abu Qatadah's wall - my cousin - greeting him, but he did not return to me. I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatadah, do you know I love Allah and His Messenger? He was silent. Then I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatadah, do you know I love Allah and His Messenger? He was silent. Then I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatadah, do you know I love Allah and His Messenger? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: I could not hold myself from weeping, then leapt the wall out. When fifty nights passed since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade speaking to us, I prayed dawn upon a roof of ours, then sat in the state of which Allah said: The earth narrowed upon them spacious though it was, and their souls narrowed upon them, when I heard a call from Sal's peak: Glad tidings, O Ka'b ibn Malik. I fell prostrate, knowing Allah had brought us joy. Then a man came galloping upon a horse to gladden me; the voice was swifter than his horse. I gave him my garment as glad tidings, wearing two other garments. He said: Our repentance was sent down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the night's last third. Umm Salamah said: O Prophet of Allah, shall we not gladden Ka'b ibn Malik? He said: Then people would crowd you and prevent you sleep all night. She said: Umm Salamah was kind in my affair, grieving at my matter. I set out to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there he sat in the Mosque with Muslims around him, shining like the moon's shining - when gladdened by a matter he would shine. I came and sat before him. He said: Glad tidings, O Ka'b ibn Malik, of the best day that came upon you since your mother bore you. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, a matter from Allah or from you? He said: Rather from Allah. Then he recited to them: Allah turned to the Prophet and the Emigrants and Ansar, until he reached: the Relenting, the Merciful. He said: And concerning us was also sent down: Fear Allah and be with the truthful. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, of my repentance is that I shall speak only truth, and strip myself of all my property as charity to Allah and His Messenger? He said: Keep part of your property; it is better for you. I said: I shall keep my share at Khaybar. He said: No blessing Allah bestowed upon me after Islam is greater in myself than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I told him truth, I and my two companions, that we did not lie to him, perishing as they perished. I hope Allah never tried anyone in truthfulness like He tried me. I never intended a lie after, and I hope Allah shall preserve me in what remains. Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached us of Ka'b ibn Malik's hadith."
"9507","From Ma'mar, he said: Qatadah and Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an informed me that they heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab say: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he went out to Tabuk, left Ali ibn Abi Talib in charge of us over Madinah. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I never loved to go out anywhere without being with you. He said: Are you not pleased to be to me as Harun to Musa, except that there is no prophet after me? Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri informed me, he said: Abu Lubabah was among those staying behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Tabuk's campaign, binding himself to a pillar, then saying: By Allah, I shall not free myself from it, nor taste food or drink until I die or Allah turns to me. He stayed seven days tasting neither food nor drink, until he would fall unconscious. He said: Then Allah turned to him. It was said to him: You have been turned to, O Abu Lubabah. He said: By Allah, I shall not free myself until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) frees me with his hand. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and freed him with his hand. Then Abu Lubabah said: O Messenger of Allah, of my repentance is to abandon my people's house where I committed the sin, and strip myself of all my property as charity to Allah and His Messenger. He said: A third suffices you, O Abu Lubabah."
"9508","From Ma'mar, he said: Al-Zuhri informed me, he said: Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, he said: The first matter blamed upon Abu Lubabah was that between him and an orphan was a cluster of dates. They disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who judged it for Abu Lubabah. The orphan wept. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave it to him. He refused. He said: Give it to him, and for you its like in Paradise. He refused. Ibn al-Dahdahah went and said to Abu Lubabah: Sell me this cluster for two gardens. He said: Yes. Then he went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, if I give this orphan this cluster, is its like in Paradise mine? He said: Yes. So he gave it to him. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: How many a hanging cluster for Ibn al-Dahdahah in Paradise. He said: And he gestured to Banu Qurayzah when they came down upon Sa'd's judgment, gesturing to his throat: slaughter. And he stayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Tabuk's campaign. Then Allah turned to him thereafter."
"9509","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, he said: Of what Allah did for His Prophet is that these two Ansar clans, Aws and Khazraj, would vie in Islam like two stallions. Aws would do nothing without Khazraj saying: By Allah, you shall never surpass us with it in Islam. When Khazraj did something, Aws said the like. When Aws struck Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf, Khazraj said: By Allah, we shall not stop until we suffice the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as they sufficed him. They discussed the weightiest Jewish man, seeking the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission to kill him, Sallam ibn Abi al-Huqayq al-A'war Abu Rafi' at Khaybar. He permitted them to kill him, saying: Do not kill a child or woman. A party went out to him, among them Abdullah ibn Atik, the people's commander, one of Banu Salimah, Abdullah ibn Unays, Mas'ud ibn Sinan, Abu Qatadah, Khuza'i ibn Aswad, a man of Aslam their ally, and another man called so-and-so ibn Salamah. They went until they reached Khaybar. Entering the town, they went to every house, locking it from outside upon its people. Then they leaned to him in a loft of his amid date-stalks, leaning therein until they knocked upon his door. His wife came out to them and said: Who are you? They said: Arabs wanting provisions. She said: This is the man; enter upon him. When they entered upon him they locked the door upon them, then rushed him with their swords. One of them said: By Allah, nothing guided me to him but his whiteness upon the bed in the night's darkness, like a Coptic cloth cast. He said: His wife shouted at us. He said: A man among us would raise his sword to strike her, then remember the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibition. He said: But for that we would have finished her that night. He said: Abdullah ibn Unays pressed with his sword in his belly until it pierced through. He was weak-sighted, falling from the loft, his leg badly sprained. He said: We descended, carrying him, going with us until we reached a spring's stream among those springs, staying therein. He said: They lit fires, kindling them in palm-fronds, seeking and pressing. Allah hid our place from them. He said: Then they returned. He said: One of our companions said: Shall we go, not knowing whether Allah's enemy died or not? He said: A man among us went out until he mingled with people, entering with them. He found his wife stooping with a lamp in her hand, Jewish men around her. One among them said: By Allah, I heard Ibn Atik's voice, then belied myself, saying: How would Ibn Atik be in this land? She said something, then raised her head, saying: He died, by the God of the Jews. He said: I never heard a word dearer to myself than it. He said: Then she went out. My companions told me he had died. We carried our companion and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informing him of that. He said: They came to him on Friday, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day upon the pulpit preaching. When he saw them he said: Successful faces."
"9510","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, Alqamah ibn Waqqas, and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud informed me of Aishah, the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wife's hadith, when the slanderers said of her what they said. He said: Allah exonerated her. Each narrated to me part of her hadith, some more retentive than others, firmer in narration. I retained from each the hadith he narrated to me, parts confirming parts. They mentioned that Aishah, the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wife, said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to go out on travel, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever one's lot came out, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go out with her. Aishah said: He cast lots among us in a campaign he raided, and my lot came out. I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after Allah had sent down the veil upon us. I was carried in my howdah, alighting therein. We marched until, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his campaign and returned, nearing Madinah, he announced departure one night. I arose when they announced departure, walking beyond the army. When I finished my need, I came to my camel, and a necklace of mine of Zafari onyx had broken. I sought my necklace, and seeking it detained me. The party who used to mount me came, carrying the howdah, mounting it upon my camel I used to ride, thinking me in it. He said: Women then were light, neither fleshy nor covered with flesh, eating only a morsel of food. The people did not notice the howdah's weight when they mounted and lifted it. I was a young girl. They sent the camel forth with it. I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their dwellings, with neither caller nor answerer therein. I headed to my place where I was, thinking the people would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my place, my eyes overcame me and I slept until morning. Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani had rested behind the army, traveling by night, reaching me in the morning. He saw a sleeping person's darkness and came to me, knowing me when he saw me - he had seen me before the veil was struck upon me. I woke only at his uttering istirja' when he knew me. I veiled my face with my cloak. By Allah, he spoke to me no word besides his istirja', until he knelt his she-camel, treading upon her forelegs, and I mounted her. He set out leading the she-camel with me until we reached the army after they had halted at midday's heat. Whoever perished perished concerning me. The one who undertook its greatest part was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. I reached Madinah, falling ill upon arrival for a month, people delving into the slanderers' saying, unaware of anything of that. What troubled me in my sickness was that I knew not from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the tenderness I used to see from him when I complained; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would only enter, greet, and say: How is she? That is what troubled me, unaware, until I went out after recovering, with Umm Mistah toward al-Manasi', our open privy - we went out only night to night - before privies were taken near our houses. Umm Mistah and I set out; she is the daughter of Abu Ruhm ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf, her mother Umm Sakhr ibn Amir, aunt of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and her son Mistah ibn Uthathah ibn Abbad ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf. I and Abu Ruhm's daughter came toward my house when we finished our need. Umm Mistah stumbled in her cloak, saying: Wretched be Mistah. I said to her: Evil is what you said; do you revile a man who witnessed Badr? She said: O dear, have you not heard what he said? She said: I said: And what did he say? She said: She told me the slanderers' saying, and I added sickness to my sickness. When I returned to my house, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me, greeted, then said: How is she? I said: Will you permit me to come to my parents? She said: I then wanted to verify the news from them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me, and I came to my parents, saying to my mother: O mother, what are people saying? She said: O daughter, be easy upon yourself. By Allah, seldom was a beautiful woman with a man loving her, with co-wives, without them multiplying against her. I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of this? She said: Yes. She said: I wept that night, my tears never ceasing, sleepless. Then I woke weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali ibn Abi Talib and Usamah ibn Zayd when revelation delayed, consulting them about parting his wife. She said: As for Usamah, he advised the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he knew of his family's innocence and what he knew in himself of love for them, saying: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they are your family, and we know only good. As for Ali, he said: Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many. If you ask the maid she will tell you truth. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Barirah, saying: O Barirah, have you seen anything of Aishah's affair troubling you? Barirah said to him: By Him Who sent you with truth, I never saw upon her a matter I fault her for more than that she is a young girl, sleeping over her family's dough, and the household goat comes eating it. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arose seeking excuse from Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn al-Salul. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said upon the pulpit: O Muslims, who will excuse me concerning a man whose harm reached my household? By Allah, I knew of my household only good. They mentioned a man I knew of only good; he would enter upon my family only with me. Sa'd ibn Mu'adh al-Ansari arose, saying: I excuse you from him, O Messenger of Allah. If of Aws, we strike his neck; if of our Khazraj brothers, command us and we do your command. She said: Sa'd ibn Ubadah arose - Khazraj's chief, a righteous man, but Jahiliyyah zeal carried him - saying to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh: By Allah's life, you shall never kill him nor can you kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr arose - Sa'd ibn Mu'adh's cousin - saying to Sa'd ibn Ubadah: You lied, by Allah's life; we shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for hypocrites. She said: The two clans, Aws and Khazraj, rose until they nearly fought, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon the pulpit. He kept calming them until they fell silent, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent. She said: I spent that day, my tears never ceasing, sleepless, my parents thinking weeping would split my liver. She said: While they sat with me weeping, a woman asked permission of me. I permitted her, and she sat weeping with me. While we were so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us, then sat. She said: He had not sat with me since what was said; a month had passed with no revelation to him. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) testified when he sat, then said: To proceed, O Aishah: such-and-such reached me of you. If you are innocent, Allah shall surely exonerate you. If you committed a sin, seek Allah's forgiveness and turn to Him, for when a servant confesses his sin then turns, Allah turns to him. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his saying, my tears dried until I felt not a drop. I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me in what he said. He said: By Allah, I know not what to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said to my mother: Answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me. She said: By Allah, I know not what to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said, a young girl reading little of the Qur'an: By Allah, I knew you heard this matter until it settled in your souls and you affirmed it. If I tell you I am innocent - and Allah knows my innocence - you will not affirm me in that. If I confess to you a sin - and Allah knows I am innocent - you will affirm me. By Allah, I find for me and you no example but as Abu Yusuf said: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is sought for aid over what you describe. She said: Then I turned, lying upon my bed. By Allah, I then knew I was innocent and Allah would exonerate me with my innocence. But by Allah, I never thought revelation to be recited would descend concerning me; my affair was meaner in myself than that Allah speak of me in a recited matter. But I hoped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would see in sleep a dream by which Allah would exonerate me. She said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not left his مجلس nor anyone of the household gone out when Allah sent down upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He was seized with what used to seize him of revelation's hardship, until pearls like jewels rolled from him on a cold day from the weight of revelation sent down upon him. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was relieved, he was relieved laughing. The first word he spoke was: Glad tidings, O Aishah; by Allah, Allah has surely exonerated you. My mother said to me: Arise to him. I said: No, by Allah, I shall not arise to him, nor praise but Allah, Who sent down my exoneration. She said: Allah, blessed and exalted, sent down: Indeed those who brought the slander are a band among you, ten verses. Allah sent down these verses in my exoneration. She said: Abu Bakr - who used to spend upon Mistah for his kinship and poverty - said: By Allah, I shall never spend upon him anything after what he said of Aishah. Allah sent down: Let not the possessors of bounty and plenty among you swear, up to His saying: Do you not love that Allah forgive you? Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I love that Allah forgive me. He restored to Mistah the spending he used to spend upon him. He said: By Allah, I shall never remove it. Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked Zaynab bint Jahsh, the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wife, about my affair: What do you know or see? She said: O Messenger of Allah, I guard my hearing and sight. By Allah, I knew only good. Aishah said: She was the one rivaling me among the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives, but Allah preserved her with piety. Her sister Hamnah bint Jahsh contended for her, perishing among those who perished. Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached us of these people's affair."
"9511","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: When Allah sent down her exoneration, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged those men who said of her what they said."
"9512","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged them."
"9513","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Asr he would murmur; murmuring in some people's saying is moving his lips as though speaking something. It was said to him: O Prophet of Allah, when you pray Asr you murmur? He said: A prophet among the prophets was impressed with his nation, saying: Who will stand to these? So it was revealed to him to give them choice between My avenging them or My empowering their enemy over them. They chose vengeance, so Allah empowered death over them, and seventy thousand of them died in a day. He said: When he narrated this hadith he would narrate this other: There was a king, and that king had a soothsayer divining for him. That soothsayer said: Look for me a clever boy - or he said: quick to learn - to teach him this knowledge of mine, for I fear to die and this knowledge cease among you, none among you knowing it. He said: They looked for a boy as he described, ordering him to attend that soothsayer and frequent him. He said: Upon the boy's road was a monk in a cell. Ma'mar said: I think the cell-dwellers then were Muslims. He said: The boy began asking that monk whenever he passed him, until he informed him, saying: I only worship Allah. The boy began staying with the monk, delaying from the soothsayer. He said: The soothsayer sent to the boy's family that he hardly attends me. The boy told the monk of that, who said to him: When the soothsayer says: Where were you?, say: I was with my family; and when your family says to you: Where were you?, say: I was with the soothsayer. He said: While the boy was so, he passed a great company of people held back by a beast; some said: That beast, meaning the lion. The boy took a stone and said: O Allah, if what the monk says is true, I ask You to kill this beast; if what the soothsayer says is true, I ask You not to kill it. He said: Then he cast it and killed the beast. People said: Who killed it? They said: The boy. People hastened to him, saying: This boy has learned knowledge none learned. A blind man heard of him and came, saying to him: If you restore my sight, for you is such-and-such. The boy said to him: I want none of this from you, but if your sight is restored to you, will you believe in Him Who restored it to you? He said: Yes. He said: He prayed to Allah, restoring his sight. He said: The blind man believed. That reached the king; their affair reached him, and he sent for them, bringing them. He said: I shall surely kill each of you a killing I kill not his companion. He said: He ordered the monk and the formerly blind man, placing the saw upon one's parting, killing him, killing the other with another killing. Then he ordered the boy, saying: Take him to such-and-such mountain and cast him from its top. When they took him to that intended place, they began falling from that mountain, tumbling from it, until none remained but the boy. He returned. The king ordered him, saying: Take him to the sea and cast him therein. He was taken to the sea, and Allah drowned those with him, saving him. The boy said: You shall never kill me until you crucify me and shoot me, saying when you shoot me: In the name of the boy's Lord - or he said: In Allah, the boy's Lord's name. He ordered him crucified, then shot him, saying: In Allah, the boy's Lord's name. He said: The boy placed his hand upon his temple, then died. People said: This boy learned knowledge none learned; we believe in this boy's Lord. He said: It was said to the king: Did you grieve that three opposed you? Now the whole world opposed you. He said: He dug the trench, then cast therein firewood and fire, then gathered people, saying: Whoever returns to his religion we leave him; whoever does not return we cast him into the Fire. He began casting them into that trench. He said: That is Allah's saying: Slain were the trench companions, the fire of fuel, until he reached: the Mighty, the Praised. He said: As for the boy, he was buried. He said: It is mentioned he was exhumed in Umar ibn al-Khattab's, may Allah have mercy on him, time, his finger upon his temple as he had placed it. Abd al-Razzaq said: The trench is at Najran."
"9514","From Ma'mar, he said: Ismail ibn Sharus informed me, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, he said: A man of Isa ibn Maryam's disciples came to the Companions of the Cave's city, wanting to enter it. It was said: Upon its gate is an idol; none enters without prostrating to it. He disliked entering it, coming to a bathhouse near that city. He worked therein, hiring himself to the bathhouse owner. The bathhouse owner saw blessing and gentleness in his bathhouse, entrusting it to him, confiding in him. Youths of the city's people attached to him; he would tell them of heaven and earth's tidings and the Hereafter's tidings until they believed and affirmed him. They were like him in goodly state. He would stipulate to the bathhouse owner: The night is mine; bar me not from prayer when due. Until the king's son came with a woman to enter the bathhouse with her. The disciple reproached him, saying: You are the king's son, entering with this such-and-such woman with you. He felt shy and left, returning another time. He said to him the like, so he reviled and scolded him, unheeding, until he entered, the woman entering with him. They spent the night in the bathhouse, dying therein. The king was told: The bathhouse owner killed your son. He was sought but not found; he fled. He said: Who accompanied him? The youths were named. They went out of the city, passing a companion of theirs in his crop, like them in affair. They told him they were sought, so he went with them, with a dog, until night sheltered them at a cave. They entered it, saying: We shall spend tonight here, then in the morning, Allah willing, you shall see your view. He said: Their ears were struck. The king went out with his companions following them until they found them, entering the cave. Whenever a man among them wanted to enter, he was terrified, none able to enter. One said to him: Did you not say: Had I power over them I would have killed them? He said: Yes. He said: Build a gate upon them at the cave's mouth, leaving them therein to die of thirst and hunger. He did so. Then long time passed. Then a sheep-herder, caught by rain at the cave, said: Would that I opened this cave sheltering my sheep from rain. He kept working it until he opened it for his sheep, entering them therein. Allah returned their spirits into their bodies next morning. They sent one of them with silver coins to buy them food. Coming to their city's gate, he showed his coins to none without him disliking them, until he came to a man, saying: Sell me food for these dirhams. He said: Whence these dirhams? He said: My companions and I went out yesterday, night sheltered us, then morning came and they sent me. He said: These dirhams were in so-and-so king's time; whence have you these dirhams? He raised him to the king, a righteous man, who said: Whence have you this silver? He said: My companions and I went out yesterday until night overtook us in such-and-such cave. Then my companions ordered me to buy them food. He said: And where are your companions? He said: In the cave. He went with him until he came to the cave's mouth, saying: Let me enter upon my companions before you. When they saw him and he drew near them, his ear and their ears were struck. They wanted to enter upon them; whenever a man entered he was terrified, unable to enter upon them. So they built a church, building a mosque wherein to pray."
"9515","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning His saying: And We cast upon his throne a body, then he turned. He said: Upon his throne was a devil forty nights until Allah restored his kingdom to him. Ma'mar said: He was not empowered over his women. Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: Sulayman said to the devils: I am commanded to build a mosque, meaning Bayt al-Maqdis, hearing therein neither pick's nor saw's sound. The devils said: In the sea is a devil; perhaps if you power over him he will tell you of that. That devil would come every seven days to a spring to drink from it. The devils went to that spring, emptied it, then filled it with wine. The devil came and said: You are sweet-scented, but you befool the clement and increase the fool's folly. Then he went, not drinking. Thirst overtook him, so he returned, saying the like thrice. Then he lapped and drank, becoming drunk. They seized him, bringing him to Sulayman. Sulayman showed him his ring - Sulayman's kingdom was in his ring - and said to him: I am commanded to build a mosque hearing therein neither pick's nor saw's sound. The devil ordered a glass made, then placed upon hoopoe eggs. The hoopoe came to brood its eggs, unable to. It went, and the devil said: See what the hoopoe brings, and take it. It brought diamond, placing it upon the glass, splitting it. They took the diamond, cutting therewith stones in pieces until he built Bayt al-Maqdis. He said: One day Sulayman went to the bath, having parted one of his wives in some offense. He entered the bath with that devil. When he entered, the devil took his ring, casting it into the sea, casting upon his throne a body: the couch, Sulayman's likeness. Sulayman went out, his kingdom gone. The devil was upon Sulayman's couch forty nights. His companions disapproved him, saying: Sulayman has been tried for neglecting prayer. That devil neglected prayer and things of religion's affair. Among Sulayman's companions was a man likened to Umar ibn al-Khattab in vigor and strength. He said: I shall ask him for you. He came to him, saying: O Prophet of Allah, what say you of one of us who touches his wife on a cold night, then sleeps until sunrise, neither washing nor praying - see you harm in that for him? He said: No harm upon him. He returned to his companions, saying: Sulayman has surely been tried. He said: While Sulayman went in the earth, he sheltered with a woman who made him a fish - or he said: brought him a fish - splitting its belly. Sulayman saw his ring in the fish's belly, raising and taking it, wearing it. Everything meeting him of beast, bird, or thing prostrated to him, and Allah restored his kingdom to him. Thereupon he said: My Lord, forgive me and grant me a kingdom befitting none after me. Qatadah said: Meaning: take it not away another time. Ma'mar said: Al-Kalbi said: Then were subjected to him the devils together, and birds."
"9516","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham informed me, from Asma bint Umays, she said: The first the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained in Maymunah's house. His sickness intensified until he fainted. He said: His wives consulted about forcing medicine into his mouth, and they forced it. When he awoke, he said: This is the deed of women come from these - pointing to Abyssinia's land; Asma bint Umays was among them. They said: We suspected pleurisy in you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: That is an ailment Allah would never afflict me with. Let none remain in the house without being forced, except the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uncle, meaning Abbas. He said: Maymunah was forced that day, fasting, by the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) resolve."
"9517","Al-Zuhri said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah informed me that Aishah informed him, she said: The first the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained in Maymunah's house. He asked his wives' permission to be nursed in my house, and they permitted him. She said: He went out with one hand upon al-Fadl ibn Abbas, another hand upon another man's hand, trailing his feet upon the ground. Ubaydullah said: I narrated it to Ibn Abbas, who said: Know you the man Aishah did not name? He is Ali ibn Abi Talib, but Aishah's soul has no good for him."
"9518","Al-Zuhri said: Urwah informed me, from others, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his sickness in which he died: Pour upon me of seven skins whose mouths were not untied, that I may rest and enjoin people. Aishah said: We seated him in a brass washtub of Hafsah's and poured water upon him, until he began gesturing to us: You have done. Then he went out."
"9519","Al-Zuhri said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me - his father one of the three turned to - from a man of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) companions: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood that day preaching, praising Allah, extolling Him, seeking forgiveness for the martyrs killed on Uhud's day. He said: You, O Emigrants, you increase while the Ansar increase not. The Ansar are my chest wherein I sheltered; honor their noble, overlook their offender."
"9520","Al-Zuhri said: I heard a man mention that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A servant whose Lord gave him choice between this world and the Hereafter chose what is with his Lord. Abu Bakr perceived he meant himself, and wept. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Gently. Then he said: Close these doors opening into the Mosque except Abu Bakr's door, may Allah have mercy on him, for I know no man of better hand to me among the companions than Abu Bakr."
"9521","Al-Zuhri said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah informed me that Aishah and Ibn al-Abbas informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when death descended upon him, began casting a garment of his upon his face; when distressed he uncovered it from his face, saying: Allah's curse upon the Jews and Christians; they took their prophets' graves as mosques. He said: Aishah says: Warning against the like of what they did."
"9522","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abdullah ibn Zam'ah: Order people to pray. Abdullah ibn Zam'ah went out, meeting Umar ibn al-Khattab, saying: Pray with people. Umar prayed with people, raising his voice, being loud-voiced. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard and said: Is this not Umar's voice? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Allah and the believers refuse that; let Abu Bakr pray with people. Umar said to Abdullah ibn Zam'ah: Evil is what you did; I thought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered you to order me. He said: No, by Allah, he ordered me not to order anyone."
"9523","Al-Zuhri said: Abdullah ibn Umar informed me, from Aishah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grew heavy, he said: Order Abu Bakr to pray with people. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr is a tender man; when he recites the Qur'an he cannot hold his tears. Would that you ordered other than Abu Bakr. She said: By Allah, only my dislike that people take augury from the first to stand in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) place. She said: I reviewed him twice or thrice. He said: Let Abu Bakr pray with people, for you are Yusuf's companions."
"9524","Al-Zuhri said: Anas ibn Malik informed me, he said: On Monday the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uncovered the chamber's curtain, seeing Abu Bakr praying with people. He said: I looked at his face as though a mushaf page, smiling. He said: We almost were tried in our prayer, rejoicing at seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr turned backward, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him: As you are. Then he let down the curtain. He was taken that very day. Umar arose, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die; his Lord sent to him as He sent to Musa forty nights from forty nights. By Allah, I hope the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shall live until he cuts hands of hypocrite men and their tongues, claiming - or he said: saying - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died."
"9525","Ma'mar said: Ayyub informed me, from Ikrimah, he said: Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib said: By Allah, I shall surely know the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remaining among us. I said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you took something to sit upon, shielding you from dust, repelling the disputant from you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall surely leave them disputing my cloak, treading my heel, dust covering me, until Allah relieves me of them. So I knew his remaining among us was little. He said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, Umar arose, saying: The Messenger did not die, but swooned as Musa swooned. By Allah, I hope the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shall live until he cuts hands of men and their tongues among hypocrites, saying the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died. Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib arose, saying: O people, has any among you a covenant or contract from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: O Allah, no. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until he joined ties, warred, joined, made peace, married women, divorced, leaving you upon clear proof and a plain path. If what Ibn al-Khattab says is true, Allah is not unable to scatter dust from him, bringing him out to us. Else leave between us and our companion, for he decays as people decay."
"9526","Al-Zuhri said: Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Al-Abbas and Ali went out from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his sickness, meeting a man who said: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wake, O Abu al-Hasan? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke convalescent. Al-Abbas said to Ali ibn Abi Talib: After three you are a servant of the staff. Then it befell him, saying: It seems to me I know Banu Abd al-Muttalib's faces at death. I fear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shall not rise from this sickness of his. Come with us to him to ask him: if this affair is ours, we shall know that; if not ours, we shall order him to enjoin good for us. Ali said to him: If we came to him and he did not grant it us, see you people granting it? By Allah, I shall never ask him for it."
"9527","Al-Zuhri said: Aishah said: When the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sickness intensified, he said: In the Highest Company, three times. Then he was taken."
"9528","Ma'mar said: And I heard Qatadah say: The last thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke was: Fear Allah concerning women and what your right hands possess."
"9529","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, he said: Ibn Abbas used to narrate that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq entered the Mosque with Umar speaking to people. He went until the house wherein the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, in Aishah's house. He uncovered from his face a striped اليمني cloak covering him, looking at the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) face, then bent over him kissing him, then saying: By Allah, Allah shall never join two deaths upon you; you have died the death after which you shall never die. Then Abu Bakr went out to the Mosque with Umar speaking to people, saying to him, Abu Bakr: Sit, O Umar. He refused to sit. He spoke to him twice or thrice, but he refused to sit. Abu Bakr arose and testified. People turned to Abu Bakr, leaving Umar. When Abu Bakr finished his testimony, he said: To proceed: whoever used to worship Muhammad, Muhammad has died; whoever among you worships Allah, Allah is Living, never dies. Then he recited this verse: Muhammad is only a Messenger; messengers passed before him, the whole verse. When Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, recited it, people were certain of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death, receiving it from Abu Bakr, until one among people said: They knew not this verse was sent down until Abu Bakr recited it."
"9530","Al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me, he said: Umar said: By Allah, no sooner had Abu Bakr recited it while I stood than I fell to the ground, certain the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had died."
"9531","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Anas ibn Malik informed me that he heard Umar's, may Allah have mercy on him, last sermon when he sat upon the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pulpit, the morrow of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death. He said: Umar testified, with Abu Bakr silent, not speaking. Then Umar said: To proceed: I said a saying, and it was not as I said. By Allah, I found not the saying I said in Allah's Book nor in a covenant the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenanted to me. But I hoped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would live until he managed us - meaning until he be the last of them. If Muhammad has died, Allah has placed among you a light to guide by: this is Allah's Book; hold to it, guided to what Allah guided Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with. Then Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) companion and second of two, is the worthiest of people for your affairs. Arise and pledge him. A party among them had pledged him before that in Banu Sa'idah's shed. The general pledge was upon the pulpit. Al-Zuhri said: Anas informed me, he said: I saw Umar urging Abu Bakr to the pulpit forcefully."
"9532","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was dying, with men in the house among them Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I write for you a document after which you shall not stray? Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is overcome by pain; with you is the Qur'an; Allah's Book suffices us. The household's people differed and disputed. Some said: Bring near, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may write for you a document after which you shall not stray. Some said what Umar said. When they multiplied idle talk and dispute near the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Arise. Abdullah said: Ibn Abbas used to say: The calamity, all calamity, is what barred between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and writing them that document, of their differing and clamor."
"9533","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I used to teach Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf in Umar's caliphate. In Umar's last Hajj, at Mina, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came to me in my dwelling in the evening, saying: Would that you had witnessed the Commander of the Believers today! A man came to him, saying: O Commander of the Believers, I heard so-and-so say: Would that the Commander of the Believers were dead, I have pledged so-and-so. Umar said: In the evening I shall surely stand among people warning them of these men wanting to seize Muslims' affair. He said: I said: O Commander of the Believers, the season gathers the rabble and mob, who overcome your مجلس. I fear if you say among them today a saying, they will fly with it everywhere, neither retaining nor placing it in its places. But wait, O Commander of the Believers, until you reach Madinah, the home of Sunnah and emigration, secluded with Emigrants and Ansar, saying what you said deliberately; they will retain your saying and place it in its places. He said: Umar said: By Allah, Allah willing, I shall surely stand with it at the first stand I stand in Madinah. He said: When we reached Madinah and Friday came, I went early for what Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf told me, finding Sa'id ibn Zayd preceding me in early going, sitting beside the pulpit. I sat beside him, my knee touching his knee. He said: When the sun declined, Umar, may Allah have mercy on him, came out to us. He said: I said as he came: By Allah, the Commander of the Believers shall surely say upon this pulpit a saying he never said before him. He said: Sa'id ibn Zayd grew angry, saying: What saying will he say never said before him? He said: When Umar ascended the pulpit, the caller began his call. When he finished his call, Umar arose, praising Allah and extolling Him as He deserves, then saying: To proceed: I wish to say a saying decreed for me to say; I know not, perhaps before my end. Allah sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with truth, sending down with him the Book. Of what Allah sent down upon him was the stoning verse. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned, and we stoned after him. I fear time lengthens with people, and one says: By Allah, what is stoning in Allah's Book? So he strays, or abandons an ordinance Allah sent down. Verily stoning is true upon whoever commits adultery when married, with proof established, pregnancy or confession. Then we used to recite: Do not turn from your fathers, for it is disbelief in you - or: for disbelief in you is turning from your fathers. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not extol me as the Christians extolled Mary's son, Allah's blessings upon him. I am only Allah's servant, so say: Allah's servant and His Messenger. Then it reached me that so-and-so among you says: Would that the Commander of the Believers were dead, I have pledged so-and-so. Let no man be deceived to say: Abu Bakr's pledge was a slip. It was so, except Allah shielded its evil. None among you is one to whom necks are cut like Abu Bakr. He was of our best when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died. Ali, al-Zubayr, and those with them stayed from him in Fatimah's house. The Ansar all stayed from us in Banu Sa'idah's shed. The Emigrants gathered to Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him. I said: O Abu Bakr, come with us to our Ansar brothers. We went heading them, meeting two righteous Ansar men who had witnessed Badr. They said: Where do you intend, O Emigrants? We said: We intend these our Ansar brothers. They said: Return and settle your affair among you. He said: I said: Settle, but we shall surely come to them. We came to them, and there they gathered in Banu Sa'idah's shed, among them a wrapped man. I said: Who is this? They said: This is Sa'd ibn Ubadah. I said: What is wrong with him? They said: He is ill. He said: The Ansar's orator arose, praising Allah and extolling Him as He deserves, then saying: To proceed: we are the Ansar, Islam's phalanx. You, O Quraysh, are a group of us. A party of you flowed to us. They want to cut us from our root and bar us from the affair. I had prepared in myself, wanting to utter it before Abu Bakr. I would spar with Abu Bakr somewhat sharply, he graver and greater than me. When I wanted to speak, he said: Gently. I disliked disobeying him. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, praised Allah and extolled Him as He deserves, then saying: By Allah, he left no word I had prepared in myself without bringing it or better in his extempore. Then he said: To proceed: what you mentioned of good in you, O Ansar, you deserve it. The Arabs shall know this affair only for this Quraysh clan, the middlemost of Arabs in abode and lineage. I have approved for you these two men; pledge whichever you wish. He said: He took my hand and Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah's hand. He said: By Allah, I disliked nothing he said but this word. To be brought forth and my neck struck, not bringing me near sin, is dearer to me than to rule a people with Abu Bakr among them. When Abu Bakr finished his saying, a man of the Ansar arose, saying: I am their rubbed palm-trunk and honored date-palm. From us an amir and from you an amir, O Quraysh; else we shall stir war between us and you anew. Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Two swords in one sheath are not sound; but from us the rulers, from you the ministers. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said in his hadith with the chain: Voices rose among us, clamor increasing, until I feared dissension. I said: O Abu Bakr, extend your hand that I may pledge you. He said: He extended his hand and I pledged him. The Emigrants pledged him, the Ansar pledged him. He said: And we sprang upon Sa'd when one said: You killed Sa'd. He said: I said: Allah killed Sa'd. By Allah, we saw in our present affair no affair stronger than pledging Abu Bakr. We feared, had we parted the people, they would make a pledge after us; either we pledge them upon what we dislike, or oppose them, becoming corruption. Let no man be deceived to say Abu Bakr's pledge was a slip. It was so, except Allah shielded its evil. None among you is one to whom necks are cut like Abu Bakr. Whoever pledges a man without Muslims' consultation is followed neither he nor the one pledging him, for fear they be killed."
"9534","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Urwah informed me that the two men they met of the Ansar were Uwaym ibn Sa'idah and Ma'n ibn Adi. The one who said: I am their rubbed palm-trunk and honored date-palm was al-Hubab ibn al-Mundhir."
"9535","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: Whoever calls to ruling himself or another without Muslims' consultation, it is lawful for you only to kill him."
"9536","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar said: Retain from me three: Rule is consultation; in ransoming Arabs, for every slave a slave; in the slave-woman's son, two slaves. Ibn Tawus concealed the third."
"9537","From Ma'mar, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Qari informed me, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab and an Ansar man sat, when Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari came and sat with them. Umar said: We love not those who carry our speech to sit with us. Abd al-Rahman said to him: I do not sit with those, O Commander of the Believers. Umar said: Yes, sit with these and these, but carry not our speech. Then Umar said to the Ansari: Whom see you people saying shall be caliph after me? He said: He numbered men of the Emigrants, not naming Ali. Umar said: What is wrong with them concerning Abu al-Hasan? By Allah, he is the worthiest of them, were he over them, to establish them upon truth's path. Ma'mar said: Abu Ishaq informed me, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: I was with Umar ibn al-Khattab when he entrusted the affair to the six. When they passed, he followed them with his sight, then said: If they entrust it to al-Ujaylih, he shall surely ride them upon the road, meaning Ali."
"9538","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: A party met, among them al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, saying: Whom see you the Commander of the Believers appointing? One said: Ali. One said: Uthman. One said: Abdullah ibn Umar, for in him is a successor. Al-Mughirah said: Shall I not learn that for you? They said: Yes. He said: Umar used to ride every Saturday to land of his. When Saturday came, al-Mughirah mentioned his son, standing upon the road. He passed him upon his she-ass, beneath him a cloak folded over it. Umar greeted, and al-Mughirah returned. Then he said: O Commander of the Believers, permit me to ride with you? He said: Yes. When Umar reached his estate, he alighted from the she-ass, taking the cloak, spreading it, leaning upon it. Al-Mughirah sat before him, narrating to him. Then al-Mughirah said: O Commander of the Believers, by Allah, you know not your term's measure. Why have you not set people a limit or taught them a mark to be guided to? He said: Umar sat upright, then said: Aha! You gathered, saying: Whom see you the Commander of the Believers appointing? One said: Ali. One said: Abdullah ibn Umar, for in him is a successor. He said: They shall not be safe questioned of it, two men of Umar's family. I said: I know not that for you. He said: I said: Then appoint. He said: Whom? I said: Uthman. He said: I fear his عقد and favoritism. He said: I said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf. He said: A weak believer. He said: I said: Al-Zubayr. He said: Obstinate. He said: I said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah. He said: His pleasure a believer's pleasure, his anger a disbeliever's anger. Were I to entrust it to him, he would place his ring in his wife's hand. He said: I said: Ali. He said: He is the worthiest of them, were he over them, to establish them upon their Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sunnah. We used to fault him for a jesting in him."
"9539","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: I entered upon Hafsah, who said: Know you your father shall not appoint? He said: I said: He would not do so. She said: He shall do so. He said: I swore to speak to him of that. I was silent until morning, not speaking to him. He said: I was as though carrying a mountain in my right hand, until I returned, entering upon him. He asked me people's state, and I told him. Then I said to him: I heard people saying a saying, swearing to tell it you. They claim you shall not appoint. Were you to have a camel-herder and sheep-herder, then he came to you leaving them, would you see he wasted? People's رعاية is weightier. He said: My saying agreed with him. He lowered his head a while, then raised it to me, saying: Allah preserves His religion. If I appoint not, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed not. If I appoint, Abu Bakr appointed. He said: No sooner had he mentioned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr than I knew he would not equate with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that he shall not appoint."
"9540","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Asma bint Umays, she said: A man of the Emigrants entered upon Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, ailing, saying: You appointed Umar? He was harsh upon us with no authority; were he to rule us he would be harsher and harsher. How will you say to Allah when you meet Him? Abu Bakr said: Seat me. They seated him. He said: Do you frighten me but with Allah? I shall say when I meet Him: I appointed over them the best of your family. Ma'mar said: I said to al-Zuhri: What is his saying: best of your family? He said: Best of Makkah's people."
"9541","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: When Abu Bakr was pledged, Ali stayed in his house. Umar met him, saying: Did you stay from Abu Bakr's pledge? He said: I swore an oath when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, not to don a cloak except to the prescribed prayer until I gather the Qur'an. Then he went out and pledged him."
"9542","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Ala ibn Ar'ar, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about Ali and Uthman. He said: As for Ali, here is his house, meaning his house near the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) house in the Mosque. I shall narrate to you of him, meaning Uthman. As for Uthman, may Allah have mercy on him, he sinned between him and Allah a great sin, and He forgave him; and sinned between him and you a small sin, and you killed him."
"9543","Ibn Mubarak informed us, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Ibn Abjar, he said: When Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was pledged, Abu Sufyan came to Ali, saying: This affair was taken from you by the lowliest house in Quraysh. By Allah, I shall surely fill it with horses and men. He said: I said: You ever remain Islam and its people's enemy; that harmed Islam and its people nothing. We saw Abu Bakr worthy of it."
"9544","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A man said to Ali: Inform me of Quraysh. He said: Weightiest of us in forbearance are our brothers Banu Umayyah; boldest of us at meeting; most generous of us with what the right hand possesses ... Banu Hashim; Quraysh's sweet basil we smell among them, Banu al-Mughirah. Away from me the rest of the day."
"9545","Ma'mar informed us, he said: A man said to Ali: Inform me of Quraysh. He said: As for we Banu Hashim, brave, glorious, guides, generous. As for our brothers Banu Umayyah, mannered, defenders. Quraysh's sweet basil we smell among them, Banu al-Mughirah."
"9546","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Then after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) emigrated and those in Abyssinia came, he sent two forces toward Syria, to Kalb, Bilqayn, Ghassan, and the Arab disbelievers in Syria's outskirts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed over one force Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, of Banu Fihr, and over the other Amr ibn al-As. Abu Bakr and Umar volunteered in Abu Ubaydah's force. When the two forces were to go out, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah and Amr ibn al-As, saying to them: Do not disobey each other. When they parted from Madinah, Abu Ubaydah came to Amr ibn al-As, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined us not to disobey each other; either obey me or I obey you. Amr ibn al-As said: Rather obey me. Abu Ubaydah obeyed him, and Amr commanded both forces. Umar ibn al-Khattab found that grievously, speaking to Abu Ubaydah, saying: Do you obey Ibn al-Nabighah, appointing him over yourself, Abu Bakr, and us?! What is this view? Abu Ubaydah said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: Son of Umm, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined me and him not to disobey each other. I feared, had I not obeyed him, disobeying the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That was complained of to him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I favor none with it over you but after you, meaning the Emigrants. That campaign was called Dhat al-Salasil. Many Arabs were captured and taken therein. Then after that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Usamah ibn Zayd, a young lad. Umar ibn al-Khattab and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam volunteered in his force. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died before that force set out. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq dispatched it after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then when Abu Bakr ruled after the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death, he sent three commanders to Syria, appointing Khalid ibn Sa'id over a troop, Amr ibn al-As over a troop, Shurahbil ibn Hasanah over a troop, sending Khalid ibn al-Walid over a troop toward Iraq. Then Umar spoke to Abu Bakr, persisting until he appointed Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan over Khalid ibn Sa'id and his troop, from a grudge Umar ibn al-Khattab held against Khalid ibn Sa'id, when he came from Yemen after the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death. Khalid ibn Sa'id met Ali ibn Abi Talib, saying: Were you overcome, O Banu Abd Manaf, in your affair? Abu Bakr did not hold it against him, but Umar did, saying: You shall surely leave his rule over foxes. When Abu Bakr employed him, he mentioned that, speaking to Abu Bakr, who employed in his place Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan. Yazid reached him as commander after he reached Syria at Dhu al-Marwah. Abu Bakr wrote to Khalid ibn al-Walid, ordering him to march to Syria with his troop. He did so. Syria was upon four commanders until Abu Bakr died. When Umar was made caliph, he removed Khalid ibn al-Walid, appointing in his place Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. Then Umar came to al-Jabiyah, removing Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, ordering his troop to disperse among the three commanders. Shurahbil ibn Hasanah said: O Commander of the Believers, was I unable or treacherous? He said: You were neither unable nor treacherous. He said: For what did you remove me? He said: I was scrupulous to appoint you while finding stronger than you. He said: Then excuse me, O Commander of the Believers. He said: I shall do so; had I known otherwise I would not do so. He said: Umar arose excusing him. Then he ordered Amr ibn al-As to march to Egypt. Syria remained upon two commanders: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah and Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan. Then Abu Ubaydah died, appointing Khalid and his cousin Iyad ibn Ghanm. Umar confirmed him. It was said to Umar: How do you confirm Iyad ibn Ghanm, a generous man withholding nothing asked? You removed Khalid ibn al-Walid for giving without you? Umar said: This is Iyad's trait with his property until it reaches his property. Moreover I would never change a matter Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah decreed. He said: Then Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan died, appointing in his place Mu'awiyah. Umar announced him to Abu Sufyan, saying: Count Yazid, O Abu Sufyan. He said: Mercy upon him; whom did you appoint in his place? He said: Mu'awiyah. He said: Your kin has joined you. He said: Then Iyad ibn Ghanm died, appointing in his place Umayr ibn Sa'd al-Ansari. Syria was upon Mu'awiyah and Umayr until Umayr was killed. Uthman ibn Affan was made caliph, removing Umayr, leaving Syria to Mu'awiyah. He removed al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah from Kufah, appointing in his place Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas. He removed Amr ibn al-As from Egypt, appointing in his place Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh. He removed Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, appointing in his place Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Kurayz. Then he removed Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas from Kufah, appointing al-Walid ibn Uqbah. Then testimony was borne against al-Walid; he flogged and removed him, appointing Sa'id ibn al-As in his place. Then people spoke, embroiling in the fitnah. Sa'id ibn al-As performed Hajj, then returned from his Hajj, meeting Iraq's cavalry, turning him back from al-Udhayb. Egypt's people expelled Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh. Basrah's people retained Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Kurayz. So it was at the fitnah's start. When Uthman, may Allah have mercy on him, was killed, people pledged Ali ibn Abi Talib. He sent to Talhah and al-Zubayr: If you wish, pledge me; if you wish, I pledge one of you. They said: Rather we pledge you. Then they fled to Makkah, with Makkah Aishah, the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wife, with what they spoke. She aided them upon their view. Many Quraysh obeyed them. They went out toward Basrah seeking Ibn Affan's blood. Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr went out with them. Abd al-Rahman ibn Attab ibn Usayd went out with them, Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, Marwan ibn al-Hakam, among Quraysh, speaking to Basrah's people, telling them Uthman was killed wronged, and they came repentant of what they had exceeded in Uthman's affair. Basrah's common folk obeyed them. Al-Ahnaf of Tamim withdrew. Abd al-Qays went out to Ali ibn Abi Talib with most who obeyed him. Aishah rode a camel of hers called Askar, in a howdah clad with cowhides, saying: I only wish my place to separate people. She said: I never thought there would be fighting between people; had I known that I would never have stood that stand. She said: People heard not my speech, nor heeded me. There was fighting. Seventy of Quraysh were killed that day, each seizing Aishah's camel's halter lest he be killed. Then they carried the howdah until they brought it into a house of those houses. Marwan was grievously wounded. Talhah ibn Ubaydullah was killed that day. Al-Zubayr was killed thereafter at Wadi al-Siba'. Aishah and Marwan returned with Quraysh's remnant, reaching Madinah. Aishah set out to Makkah. Marwan and al-Aswad ibn Abi al-Bakhtari were over Madinah and its people, dominating it. War raged between Ali and Mu'awiyah. Their dispatches would reach Madinah, reaching Makkah for Hajj; whichever preceded was the season's amir for people in Hajj days. Then Umm Habibah, the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wife, sent to Umm Salamah, one saying to the other: Come, let us write to Mu'awiyah and Ali to free from these dispatches frightening people, until the nation gathers upon one of them. Umm Habibah said: I suffice you my brother Mu'awiyah. Umm Salamah said: I suffice you Ali. Each wrote to her man, sending a delegation of Quraysh and Ansar. As for Mu'awiyah, he obeyed Umm Habibah. As for Ali, he intended to obey Umm Salamah, but al-Hasan ibn Ali forbade him that. Their dispatches and agents kept coming to Madinah and Makkah until Ali, may Allah have mercy on him, was killed. Then people gathered upon Mu'awiyah, with Marwan and Ibn al-Bakhtari dominating Madinah's people in that fitnah. Egypt was in Ali ibn Abi Talib's authority; he appointed over it Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah al-Ansari - the Ansar's banner-bearer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Badr's day and others was Sa'd ibn Ubadah. Qays was of people's judicious, except what the fitnah's affair overcame. Mu'awiyah and Amr ibn al-As strove to expel him from Egypt, overcoming Egypt. He had withheld from them with shrewdness and stratagem; they could not open Egypt until Mu'awiyah schemed against Qays ibn Sa'd from Ali's side. He said: Mu'awiyah would speak to a judicious Qurayshi man, saying: I never devised a stratagem sweeter to me than one I schemed with Qays ibn Sa'd from Ali's side, while he was in Iraq, when Qays withheld from me. I said to Syria's people: Do not revile Qays, nor call me to raid him, for Qays is our partisan; his letters and counsel reach us. See you not what he does with your brothers with him of Kharbita's people? He grants them their stipends and provisions, secures their flocks, beneficent to every seeker coming to him. We disapprove nothing in his counsel. Mu'awiyah said: I kept writing that to my partisans of Iraq's people. Ali's spies of Iraq's people heard that. When that reached Ali, conveyed to him by Abdullah ibn Ja'far and Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, he suspected Qays ibn Sa'd, writing to him ordering him to fight Kharbita's people - Kharbita's people then ten thousand. Qays refused to fight them, writing to Ali: They are Egypt's faces, nobles, and zealous among them. They are pleased with me securing their flocks, granting their stipends and provisions. You know their inclination is with Mu'awiyah. I shall not plot them with a matter easier upon me and you than doing that with them today. Were I to call them to fight me, they would be two black ... of Arabs, among them Busr ibn Artah, Maslamah ibn Mukhallad, Mu'awiyah ibn Khudayj al-Khawlani. Leave me and my view in them; I know best what I manage of them. Ali refused but their fighting. Qays refused to fight them. Qays wrote to Ali: If you suspect me, remove me from your work, sending another to him. He sent al-Ashtar as amir over Egypt, until at al-Qalzam he drank at al-Qalzam a drink of honey, wherein was his death. That reached Mu'awiyah and Amr ibn al-As. Amr ibn al-As said: Allah has soldiers of honey. When al-Ashtar's death reached Ali, he sent Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr as amir over Egypt. When Qays ibn Sa'd was told of Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr coming as amir over him, he met him, secluding and conferring with him, saying: You came from one with no view in war. My removal by you bars me not from advising you. I am insightful in your affair. I shall guide you to what I plotted with Mu'awiyah, Amr ibn al-As, and Kharbita's people; plot them therewith, for if you plot them otherwise you perish. Qays described to him the plotting he plotted them with. Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr distrusted him, opposing him in everything he ordered him. When Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr reached Egypt, Qays went out toward Madinah. Marwan and al-Aswad ibn Abi al-Bakhtari frightened him, until fearing seizure and killing, he mounted his riding camel, appearing to Ali. Mu'awiyah wrote to Marwan and al-Aswad ibn Abi al-Bakhtari, raging at them, saying: You reinforced Ali with Qays ibn Sa'd, his view and plotting. By Allah, had you reinforced him with eight thousand fighters, it would not enrage me more than your sending Qays ibn Sa'd out to Ali. Qays ibn Sa'd reached Ali. When the tidings became clear to him, and Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr's killing came to them, Ali knew Qays ibn Sa'd had managed of them great matters of plotting, which Ali's view and those aiding him upon Qays's removal fell short of. Ali obeyed Qays in the whole affair, placing him over Iraq's vanguard and those in Adharbayjan and its land, and over the fifty's police who volunteered for death. Forty thousand had pledged Ali upon death. Qays ibn Sa'd kept guarding ... that frontier until Ali was killed. Iraq's people made al-Hasan ibn Ali caliph over the caliphate. Al-Hasan wanted not fighting, but wanted to take for himself what he could from Mu'awiyah, then enter the community and pledge. Al-Hasan knew Qays ibn Sa'd would not agree with him upon that, removing him, appointing in his place Ubaydullah ibn al-Abbas. When Ubaydullah ibn al-Abbas knew what al-Hasan wanted to take for himself, Ubaydullah wrote to Mu'awiyah asking safety, stipulating for himself upon the wealth he had taken. Mu'awiyah stipulated that for him, sending him Ibn Amir in great cavalry. Ubaydullah went out to them by night until he joined them, leaving his troops he commanded with no commander, with them Qays ibn Sa'd. The fifty's police appointed Qays ibn Sa'd, covenanting to fight Mu'awiyah and Amr ibn al-As until he stipulated for Ali's partisans and those following him upon their wealth, blood, and what they took of the fitnah. Mu'awiyah was free, when finished with Ubaydullah and al-Hasan, to plot the man most important to him in plotting, with him forty thousand. Mu'awiyah, Amr, and Syria's people halted with them forty nights. Mu'awiyah would send to Qays, reminding him of Allah, saying: Upon whose obedience do you fight me? Saying: The one upon whose obedience you fight has pledged me. Qays refused to acknowledge him, until Mu'awiyah sent with a sealed scroll at its bottom, saying: Write in this scroll; what you write is yours. Amr said to Mu'awiyah: Give him not this; fight him. Mu'awiyah, the better of the two men, said: Gently, O Abu Abdullah, for we shall not reach killing these until their number of Syria's people are killed. What is life's good after that? By Allah, I shall not fight him until I find no escape from that. When Mu'awiyah sent him that scroll, Qays ibn Sa'd stipulated for himself and Ali's partisans safety upon what they took of blood and wealth, asking Mu'awiyah no wealth therein. Mu'awiyah granted him what he stipulated, and Qays and those with him entered the community. He was counted among Arabs, until the first fitnah erupted, five called Arabs' judicious plotters: of Quraysh counted Mu'awiyah and Amr; of the Ansar counted Qays ibn Sa'd; of the Emigrants counted Abdullah ibn Budayl ibn Warqa al-Khuza'i; of Thaqif counted al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah. With Ali among them were two men: Qays ibn Sa'd and Abdullah ibn Budayl. Al-Mughirah was secluded at Taif and its land. When the two arbiters were appointed, meeting at Adhruh, al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah came to them. The two arbiters sent to Abdullah ibn Umar and Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. Many Quraysh men came. Mu'awiyah came with Syria's people. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari and Amr ibn al-As came, the two arbiters. Ali and Iraq's people refused to come. Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah said to judicious Quraysh men: See you anyone able to know: will these two arbiters agree or not? They said to him: We see none knowing that. He said: By Allah, I think I shall know it of them when alone with them, reviewing them. He entered upon Amr ibn al-As, beginning with him, saying: O Abu Abdullah, inform me of what I ask you: how see you us neutrals? We doubted this affair you clarified in this fighting, seeing to wait and verify until the nation gathers upon a man, entering into the community's righteous entry? Amr said: I see you neutrals behind the righteous, with the wicked. Al-Mughirah departed, asking him of nothing else, until he entered upon Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, secluding with him, saying to him the like of what he said to Amr. Abu Musa said: I see you the firmest of people in view; I see in you Muslims' remnant. He departed, asking him of nothing else. He said: He met his companions to whom he had said what he said of Quraysh's judicious, saying: I swear to you these two shall never agree upon one man; each shall surely call to his view. When the two arbiters met, speaking alone, Amr said: O Abu Musa, see you the first we judge in truth upon us is to judge the loyal with loyalty, the treacherous with treachery? Abu Musa said: And what is that? He said: Know you not Mu'awiyah and Syria's people came to the appointment we promised them? He said: Then write it. Abu Musa wrote it. Amr said: I and you are free to name a man to rule this nation's affair. Name, O Abu Musa, for I can pledge you than you pledge me. Abu Musa said: I name Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab - Abdullah ibn Umar was of those secluded. Amr said: I name for you Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan. They left not that مجلس until they differed and reviled. Then they went out to people. Then Abu Musa said: O people, I found Amr ibn al-As's example like Him Whom Allah, blessed and exalted, said: Recite to them the tidings of him to whom We gave Our signs but he slipped from them, until he reached: that they may reflect. Amr ibn al-As said: O people, I found Abu Musa's example like Him Whom Allah, blessed and exalted, said: The example of those laden with the Torah then bearing it not is like the ass bearing books, until he reached: the wrongdoers. Then each wrote with the example he struck for his companion to the garrisons."
"9547","Al-Zuhri said: From Salim, from Ibn Umar, Ma'mar said: And Ibn Tawus informed me, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Umar, he said: Mu'awiyah arose one evening, praising Allah as He deserves, then saying: To proceed: whoever would speak in this affair, let him show me his horn. By Allah, none shows himself therein without me worthier of it than him and his father. He said: Alluding to Abdullah ibn Umar. Abdullah ibn Umar said: I loosened my cloak, wanting to arise to him, saying: Men who fought you and your father upon Islam speak therein. Then I feared to say a word splitting the community, with blood shed therein, carried therein upon other than view. What Allah, blessed and exalted, promised in the Gardens was dearer to me than that. He said: When I went to my house, Habib ibn Maslamah came to me, saying: What barred you from speaking when you heard the man speak? I said to him: I intended that, then feared to say a word splitting the community, with blood shed therein, carried therein upon other than view. What Allah, blessed and exalted, promised in the Gardens was dearer to me than all that. Habib ibn Maslamah said to Abdullah ibn Umar: May my father and mother be ransom for you; you were protected, preserved from what you feared its disgrace."
"9548","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Khaybar, al-Hajjaj ibn Ilat said: O Messenger of Allah, I have property in Makkah, and family therein. I wish to come to them. Am I free if I disparage you or say something? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him to say what he wished. He came to his wife upon arriving, saying: Gather for me what you have, for I wish to buy of Muhammad's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions' spoils; they have been violated, their wealth taken. That spread in Makkah. The Muslims were downcast, the idolaters showing joy and gladness. He said: The news reached al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. He sat, unable to stand. Ma'mar said: Uthman al-Jazari informed me, from Miqsam, he said: He took a son of his resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), called Qutham, lying him upon his chest, saying: My love Qutham, like the high-nosed one, a prophet of favors, despite the nose of whoever is spiteful. Thabit said: Anas said: Then he sent a slave of his to al-Hajjaj: What have you brought? What do you stand upon? What Allah promised is better than what you brought. He said: Al-Hajjaj ibn Ilat said: Convey peace to Abu al-Fadl, telling him: Let him be alone in one of his houses that I may come to him, for the news is what gladdens him. He said: A slave came to him; reaching the house's door, he said: Glad tidings, O Abu al-Fadl. He said: Al-Abbas sprang joyfully until he kissed between his eyes. He told him what al-Hajjaj said, and he freed him. He said: Then al-Hajjaj came to him, telling him the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had conquered Khaybar, spoiled their wealth, Allah's, blessed and exalted, arrows ran in their wealth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose Safiyyah bint Huyayy, taking her for himself, giving her choice between freeing her to be his wife or joining her family. She chose to be freed and be his wife. But I came for property of mine here, wanting to gather and go with it. I asked the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission, and he permitted me to say what I wished. Conceal me three days, then mention what seems you. He said: His wife gathered what she had of jewelry and goods, handing it to him. Then he departed with it. After three days, al-Abbas came to al-Hajjaj's wife, saying: What did your husband do? She told him he had gone on such-and-such day, saying: May Allah not disgrace you, O Abu al-Fadl; what reached you grieved us. He said: Yes, may Allah not disgrace me. By Allah's praise, it was only what we loved. Allah, blessed and exalted, opened Khaybar upon His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah's, exalted, arrows ran in their wealth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose Safiyyah for himself. If you have need of your husband, join him. She said: I think, by Allah, you truthful. He said: By Allah, I am truthful; the affair is as I told you. He said: Then he went until he reached Quraysh's gatherings, saying when he passed them: Naught befalls you but good, O Abu al-Fadl. He said: Naught befell me but good, Allah's praise. Al-Hajjaj ibn Ilat told me: Khaybar, Allah opened it upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah's arrows ran therein. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose Safiyyah for himself. He asked me to conceal him three days; he only came to take his property and what belongs to him here, then go. He said: Allah, blessed and exalted, returned the gloom in the Muslims upon the idolaters. Muslims who had entered his house gloomy went out until they came to al-Abbas, who told them the news. The Muslims rejoiced. Allah, blessed and exalted, returned what was of gloom, rage, or grief upon the idolaters."
"9549","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan al-Nasri, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab sent to me that some households of your people reached Madinah, and we ordered them a grant; divide it among them. I said: O Commander of the Believers, order another with that. He said: Take it, O man. He said: While I was so, his freedman came to him, saying: Here are Uthman, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam. He said: I know not whether he mentioned Talhah or not. They seek permission upon you. He said: Permit them. He said: Then he waited a while, then came, saying: Here are al-Abbas and Ali seeking permission upon you. He said: Permit them. He said: Then he waited a while. He said: When al-Abbas entered, he said: O Commander of the Believers, judge between me and this one - that day disputing in what Allah restored to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Banu al-Nadir's wealth. The people said: Judge between them, O Commander of the Believers, relieving each of his companion; their dispute lengthened. Umar said: I adjure you by Allah by Whose permission heavens and earth stand: know you the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity? He said: They said: He said that. Then he said to them the like, and they said: Yes. He said to them: I shall tell you of this fay': Allah, blessed and exalted, singled His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereof with something He gave none else, saying: And what Allah restored to His Messenger from them, you urged upon it neither horse nor camel, but Allah empowers His messengers over whom He wills. This was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exclusively. Then by Allah, he neither took it apart from you nor favored himself with it over you. By Allah, he divided among you, spreading it in you until this property remained thereof. He would spend upon his family thereof a year. He said: Perhaps he said: He would withhold his family's sustenance thereof a year, then make its remainder as Allah's property. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, Abu Bakr said: I am the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guardian after him, working therein as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) worked. Then he turned to Ali and al-Abbas, saying: You two claim he therein was unjust, wicked; Allah knows he therein was truthful, dutiful, following truth. Then I was entrusted it after Abu Bakr two years of my rule, working therein as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr worked. You two claim I therein am unjust, wicked; Allah knows I therein am truthful, dutiful, following truth. Then you two came to me - this one came to me, meaning al-Abbas, asking me his inheritance from his brother's son; this one came to me, meaning Ali, asking me his wife's inheritance from her father. I said to you: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity. Then it occurred to me to hand it to you. I took upon you Allah's covenant and pledge to work therein as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and I worked while I ruled it. You said: Hand it to us upon that. Want you of us a judgment other than this? By Him by Whose permission heaven and earth stand, I shall never judge between you with other than this. If you are unable for it, hand it to me. He said: Ali overcame him for it. It was in Ali's hand, then Hasan's hand, then Husayn's hand, then Ali ibn Husayn's hand, then Hasan ibn Hasan's hand, then Zayd ibn Hasan's hand. Ma'mar said: Then in Abdullah ibn Hasan's hand. Then these took it, meaning Banu al-Abbas."
"9550","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah and Amrah, they said: The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives sent to Abu Bakr asking their inheritance from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Aishah sent to them: Do you not fear Allah? Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity? He said: They were pleased with her saying, abandoning that."
"9551","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that Fatimah and al-Abbas came to Abu Bakr seeking their inheritance from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then seeking his land of Fadak and his share of Khaybar. Abu Bakr said to them: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity. Muhammad's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) family only eats of this property. By Allah, I shall never abandon a matter I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do without doing it. He said: Fatimah forsook him, not speaking to him therein until she died. Ali buried her by night, not informing Abu Bakr. Aishah said: Ali had of people in Fatimah's life standing. When Fatimah died, people's faces turned from him. Fatimah stayed six months after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then died. Ma'mar said: A man said to al-Zuhri: Did Ali not pledge him six months? He said: No, nor anyone of Banu Hashim, until Ali pledged him. When Ali saw people's faces turning from him, he hastened to Abu Bakr's reconciliation, sending to Abu Bakr: Come to us, coming with none with you, disliking Umar's coming for what he knew of his harshness. Umar said: Come them not alone. Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I shall surely come them alone; what might they do to me? He said: Abu Bakr went, entering upon Ali, who had gathered Banu Hashim with him. Ali arose, praising Allah and extolling Him as He deserves, then saying: To proceed, O Abu Bakr: what barred us from pledging you is neither denial of your merit nor envy of you for good Allah drove to you. But we see we have in this affair a right, and you decided it apart from us. He said: Then he mentioned his kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their right. He kept mentioning that until Abu Bakr wept. When Ali fell silent, Abu Bakr testified, praising Allah and extolling Him as He deserves, then saying: To proceed: by Allah, the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kinship is dearer to me to join than my kinship. By Allah, I spared no good in this wealth between me and you. But I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity. Muhammad's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) family only eats in this property. By Allah, I mention no matter the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did therein without doing it, Allah willing. Then Ali said: Your appointment is the evening for the pledge. When Abu Bakr prayed Zuhr, he turned to people, then excused Ali with part of what he excused with. Then Ali arose, magnifying Abu Bakr's, may Allah be pleased with him, right, merit, and precedence. Then he went to Abu Bakr pledging him. People turned to Ali, saying: You did right and well. She said: They were near to Ali when he neared the affair and the good."
"9552","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab would leave none of the non-Arabs entering Madinah. Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah wrote to Umar that with me is a carpenter, engraver, blacksmith lad, beneficial to Madinah's people. If you see fit to permit me to send him, I shall do so. He permitted him. He had set upon him every day two dirhams. He was called Abu Lu'luah, Magian in origin. He stayed as Allah willed. Then he came to Umar complaining of his much tax. Umar said to him: What crafts do you know? He said: Carpenter, engraver, blacksmith. Umar said: Your tax is not great for what crafts you know. He said: He went muttering. Then he passed Umar sitting, saying: Was I not told you say: Had I wished to make a mill grinding with wind, I would do so? Abu Lu'luah said: I shall surely make a mill people talk of. He said: Abu Lu'luah went. Umar said: The slave just threatened me. When he resolved what he resolved, he took a dagger, wrapping himself in it. Then he sat for Umar in a corner of the Mosque's corners. Umar would go out at dawn waking people for prayer. He passed him, and he sprang to him, stabbing him thrice: one beneath his navel, which killed him. He stabbed twelve men of the Mosque's people; six among them died, six remaining. Then he cut his own throat with his dagger, dying. Ma'mar said: I heard other than al-Zuhri say: A man of Iraq's people cast upon him a cloak; when he grew distressed therein, he cut his throat. Ma'mar said: He said: When Umar feared bleeding, he said: Let Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf pray with people. Al-Zuhri said: Abdullah ibn Abbas informed me, he said: We carried Umar, I and men of the Ansar, until we brought him into his house. He remained in one evening until dawn brightened. A man said: You shall startle him with nothing but prayer. He said: We said: Prayer, O Commander of the Believers. He said: He opened his eyes, then saying: Did people pray? We said: Yes. He said: Verily no share in Islam for one abandoning prayer. He said: Perhaps, Ma'mar said: wasting prayer. Then he prayed, his wound gushing blood. Ibn Abbas said: Then Umar said to me: Go out and ask people who stabbed me? I went, and people gathered. I said: Who stabbed the Commander of the Believers? They said: Allah's enemy Abu Lu'luah stabbed him, al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah's slave. I returned to Umar, he awaiting my coming with the news. I said: O Commander of the Believers, Allah's enemy Abu Lu'luah stabbed you. Umar said: Allah is Greatest. Praise to Allah Who made not my killer dispute me on the Day of Resurrection in a prostration he prostrated to Allah. I thought the Arabs would never kill me. Then a physician came, giving him date-wine to drink; it came out of him. People said: This is blood's redness. Then another came, giving him milk to drink; the milk came out curdling. The one who gave him milk said to him: Make your covenant, O Commander of the Believers. Umar said: The brother of Banu Mu'awiyah told me truth."
"9553","Al-Zuhri said: From Salim, from Ibn Umar: Then he called the six men: Ali, Uthman, Sa'd, Abd al-Rahman, al-Zubayr - I know not whether he mentioned Talhah or not - saying: I looked among people, seeing no dissension. If dissension be, it is among you. Arise, consult, then appoint one of you."
"9554","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came to me on the third night of the consultation days, after what Allah willed of the night passed, finding me asleep. He said: Wake him. They woke me. He said: Do I not see you asleep? By Allah, I hardly closed eyes with much sleep these three nights. Go, call me so-and-so and so-and-so, men of the Ansar's precedence. I called them, and he secluded with them in the Mosque long. Then they arose. Then he said: Go, call me al-Zubayr, Talhah, and Sa'd. I called them, conferring with them long. Then they arose from him. Then he said: Call me Ali. I called him, conferring with him long. Then he arose from him. Then he said: Call me Uthman. I called him, conferring with him; only dawn's call separated them. Then Suhayb prayed with people. When finished, people gathered to Abd al-Rahman. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then saying: To proceed: I looked among people, seeing them equating none with Uthman. Make not, O Ali, a way upon yourself. Then he said: Upon you, O Uthman, Allah's covenant, pledge, protection, and His Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) protection, to work with Allah's Book, His Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sunnah, and what the two caliphs after him worked with. He said: Yes. He wiped upon his hand, pledging him. Then people pledged him. Then Ali pledged him. Then he went out, meeting Ibn Abbas, who said: Were you deceived? Ali said: Is it a deceit? He said: He worked with his two companions' work six years, omitting nothing, until six years. Then the old man softened, weakening, overcome in his affair."
"9555","Al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr - we never tried a lie upon him - said: When Umar was killed, I reached al-Hurmuzan, Jufaynah, and Abu Lu'luah in secret counsel. I surprised them; they sprang, and from among them fell a dagger with two heads, its hilt in its middle. Abd al-Rahman said: See with what Umar was killed? They looked, finding a dagger upon Abd al-Rahman's described likeness. He said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar went out wrapped in his sword until he came to al-Hurmuzan, saying: Accompany me to look at a horse of mine - al-Hurmuzan was horse-wise. He went out walking before him, and Ubaydullah smote him with the sword. Feeling the sword's cut, he said: There is no god but Allah. He killed him. Then he came to Jufaynah, a Christian, calling him. When he appeared to him, he smote him with the sword, crucifying between his eyes. Then he came to Abu Lu'luah's daughter, a small girl claiming Islam, killing her. Madinah darkened that day upon its people. Then he came with the sword unsheathed in his hand, saying: By Allah, I shall leave no captive in Madinah but I kill him, and others - as though alluding to Emigrant men. They began saying to him: Cast the sword, but he refused, fearing to near him, until Amr ibn al-As came to him, saying: Give me the sword, O brother's son. He gave it him. Then Uthman sprang to him, seizing his head; they contended until people separated them. When Uthman ruled, he said: Advise me concerning this man who rent in Islam what he rent, meaning Ubaydullah ibn Umar. The Emigrants advised him to kill him. A company of people said: Was Umar killed yesterday, and you want to follow him with his son today? Allah banish al-Hurmuzan and Jufaynah. He said: Amr ibn al-As arose, saying: O Commander of the Believers, Allah excused you that this affair be while you have authority over people. This affair was while you had no authority. Pardon him, O Commander of the Believers. He said: People dispersed upon Amr's sermon. Uthman paid blood-money for the two men and the girl. Al-Zuhri said: Hamzah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar informed me, he said: Mercy upon Hafsah; she was of those encouraging Ubaydullah to kill al-Hurmuzan and Jufaynah. Al-Zuhri said: Abdullah ibn Tha'labah - or he said: Ibn Khalifah al-Khuza'i - informed me, he said: I saw al-Hurmuzan raising his hand praying behind Umar. Ma'mar said: Other than al-Zuhri said: Uthman said: I am al-Hurmuzan, Jufaynah, and the girl's guardian; I made them blood-money."
"9556","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: When Umar was stabbed he called Ali, Uthman, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and al-Zubayr. He said: I think he said: And Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas. He said: I looked into people's affair, seeing no dissension with them. If dissension be, it is among you. Then your people only appoint one of you, O three. If you are over something of people's affair, O Ali, fear Allah, and impose not Banu Hashim upon people's necks. Ma'mar said: Other than al-Zuhri said: Impose not Banu Abi Rukanah upon people's necks."
"9557","Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said in his hadith, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: If you, O Uthman, are over something, fear Allah, and impose not Banu Abi Mu'ayt upon people's necks. If you are over something of people's affairs, O Abd al-Rahman, fear Allah, and impose not your kin upon people's necks. Consult, then appoint one of you. He said: They arose to consult. Abdullah ibn Umar said: Uthman called me to consult me, though Umar had not put me in the consultation. When he much called me, I said: Fear you not Allah? Do you appoint while the Commander of the Believers is still alive? He said: It is as though I awakened Umar. He called them, saying: Wait; let Suhayb pray with people. Then consult, then gather your affair in three days. Gather the garrison commanders. Whoever of you takes rule without Muslims' consultation, kill him. Ibn Umar said: By Allah, I loved not to be with them, for seldom saw I Umar move his lips without part of what he said being so. Al-Zuhri said: When Umar died they gathered. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to them: If you wish, I shall choose for you among you. They entrusted him that. Al-Miswar said: I never saw like Abd al-Rahman. By Allah, he left none of Emigrants, Ansar, or others of judicious without consulting them that night."
"9558","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Usamah ibn Zayd over an army with Umar ibn al-Khattab and al-Zubayr therein. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken before that army set out. Usamah said to Abu Bakr when he was pledged - Usamah had not departed until Abu Bakr was pledged - arising, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directed me to what he directed me. I fear the Arabs apostatize. If you wish, I shall stay near you until you see. Abu Bakr said: I would never turn back a matter the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered. But if you wish to permit Umar, do so. He permitted him. Usamah ibn Zayd went until he reached the place the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him. He said: Mist seized them until a man among them could hardly see his companion. He said: They found a man of those lands. He said: They took him guiding them the road where they wanted, raiding the place ordered. He said: People heard of that, some saying to others: You claim the Arabs differed, with their cavalry in such-and-such place?! He said: Allah, blessed and exalted, thereby repelled from the Muslims. He was called by the command until he died, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched him, never removing him until he died."
"9559","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When Umar was made caliph he removed Khalid ibn al-Walid, appointing Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, sending him his appointment while he was in Syria on Yarmuk's day. The appointment stayed with Abu Ubaydah two months, not informing Khalid of it from shyness of him. Khalid said: Bring forth, O man, your appointment; we hear and obey you. By my life, the dearest of people to us died, and the most hateful of people to us ruled. Abu Ubaydah was over the cavalry."
"9560","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Tawus informed me, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Umar, he said: I entered upon Hafsah with her braids dripping. I said: Of people's affair has been what you see, and nothing of the affair was made for me. She said: Join them, for they await you. By Him I fear your withholding from them be dissension. She left him not until he went. When the two arbiters parted, Mu'awiyah preached, saying: Whoever would speak, let him show his horn."
"9561","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Humayd ibn Hilal, he said: On Qadisiyyah's day, over the cavalry was Qays ibn Makshuh al-Absi, over the infantry al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah al-Thaqafi, over people Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas. Qays said: I witnessed Yarmuk's day, Ajnadayn's day, Abs's day, Fahl's day. I never saw like today in numbers, iron, or fighting craft. By Allah, their ends are not seen. Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah said: This is foam of Satan's foam. Had we charged them, Allah would set some upon others. When I charge them with my infantry, be sure not to charge them with your cavalry in their rear; but hold your cavalry from us, charging whoever faces you. He said: A man arose, saying: Allah is Greatest; I see the earth behind them. Al-Mughirah said: Sit, for standing and speaking at fighting is failure. When one of you wants to pray, let him pray at his spear's station. Then he said: I shall shake my mount thrice. When I shake it the first time, prepare. Then when I shake it the third, prepare for the charge - or he said: charge, for I am charging. He said: He shook it the third, then charged, wearing two coats of mail. He said: We reached not for himself until he pierced them with two thrusts, slipping between them. There was victory. He said: Allah set some upon others until they were heaps. We wished to take two men, one of them, killing him, but we did."
"9562","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah - or one of them, Abu Bakr doubted - that Asma bint Umays said: When Fatimah was presented to Ali, we found in his house only spread sand, a cushion stuffed with fiber, a jar, and a mug. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Ali: Do no deed - or he said: approach not your wife - until I come to you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, saying: Is my brother here? Umm Ayman - Usamah ibn Zayd's mother, an Abyssinian, a righteous woman - said: O Prophet of Allah, he is your brother, yet you married him your daughter?! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had fraternized his companions, fraternizing Ali with himself. He said: That shall be, O Umm Ayman. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a vessel with water, saying therein what Allah willed him to say. Then he sprinkled upon Ali's chest and face. Then he called Fatimah, who arose to him stumbling in her cloak from shyness. He sprinkled upon her of that water, saying to her what Allah willed him to say. Then he said to her: I spared you not; I married you my dearest family to me. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw darkness behind the curtain or behind the door, saying: Who is this? She said: Asma. He said: Asma bint Umays? She said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Did you come honoring the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his daughter? She said: Yes; a girl on the night she is wed needs a woman near her; if a need عرضت she confides that to her. She said: He prayed for me a prayer; it is my surest deed with me. Then he said to Ali: Take your wife. Then he went out, turning. She said: He kept praying for them until he vanished in his chambers."
"9563","From Yahya ibn al-Ala al-Bajali, from his uncle Shu'ayb ibn Khalid, from Hanzalah ibn Sabrah ibn al-Musayyab, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Fatimah would be mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but none mentioned her without him turning from him, until they despaired of her. Sa'd ibn Mu'adh met Ali, saying: By Allah, I see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withholds her only for you. He said: Ali said to him: Why see you that? He said: By Allah, I am not one of two men: I am neither a worldly man seeking what is with me - he knew my property, neither yellow nor white - nor a disbeliever he conciliates with her from his religion, meaning he conciliates him with her. I am the first to embrace Islam. Sa'd said: I adjure you to relieve it from me, for in that is relief. He said: What shall I say? He said: Say: I come proposing to Allah and to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Fatimah bint Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Ali went, عرضing to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he prayed, confined in supererogatory prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is as though you have a need, O Ali? He said: Yes; I come proposing to Allah and His Messenger Fatimah daughter of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Welcome, a weak word. Then Ali returned to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, who said to him: What did you do? He said: I did what you ordered me; he added only to welcome me with a weak word. Sa'd said: He married you, by Him Who sent him with truth. There is no refusal now, no lie with him. I adjure you to come to him tomorrow, saying: O Prophet of Allah, when will you wed me? Ali said: This is harder than the first. Shall I not say: O Messenger of Allah, my need? He said: Say as I ordered you. Ali went, saying: O Messenger of Allah, when will you wed me? He said: The third, Allah willing. Then he called Bilal, saying: O Bilal, I married my daughter to my cousin. I love it be of my nation's sunnah to feed food at marriage. Go to the sheep, taking a ewe and four or five mudds, making me a bowl, that I may gather thereon Emigrants and Ansar. When you finish it, inform me of it. He went, doing what he ordered, then bringing him a bowl, placing it before him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pierced its top, then saying: Bring people in groups, leaving no group to another - meaning when a group finished, another would not return. People began coming, whenever a group finished another came, until people finished. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to its remainder, spitting therein, blessing. He said: O Bilal, carry it to your mothers, telling them: Eat and feed whoever visits you. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arose, entering upon the women, saying: I married my daughter to my cousin. You knew her station with me. I am handing her to him now, Allah willing. Take your daughter. The women arose, perfuming her of their perfume and jewelry. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered; when the women saw him they went, between them and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a curtain. Asma bint Umays stayed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Gently, who are you? She said: I am who guarded your daughter, for a girl on the night she is wed needs a woman near her; if a need عرضت her, or she wants something, she confides that to her. He said: I ask my God to guard you before you, behind you, right of you, left of you, from accursed Satan. Then he called Fatimah, who came. When she saw Ali sitting beside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she was shy and wept. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared her weeping was because Ali had no property. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What makes you weep? I spared you not in myself; I sought for you my best family. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I married you him happy in this world; in the Hereafter he is of the righteous. He made her adhere. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring me the washtub, filling it water. Asma brought the washtub, filling it water. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rinsed therein, washing therein his feet and face. Then he called Fatimah, taking a handful of water, striking therewith upon her head, a handful between her breasts. Then he sprinkled his skin and her skin. Then he embraced her, saying: O Allah, she is of me and I of her. O Allah, You removed impurity from me, purifying me; purify her. Then he called for another washtub. Then he called Ali, doing with him as he did with her, praying for him as he prayed for her. Then he said: Now arise to your house. Allah join you, bless your secret, set right your state. Then he arose, closing upon them his door with his hand. Ibn Abbas said: Asma bint Umays told me she watched the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he kept praying for them exclusively, associating none in his prayer for them, until he vanished in his chambers."
"9564","From Waki' ibn al-Jarrah, he said: Sharik informed me, from Abu Ishaq, that when Ali married Fatimah, she said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): You married me to a bleary-eyed one, great-bellied?! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I married you him, and he is the foremost of my companions in submission, most knowledgeable, greatest in forbearance."
"9565","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode an ass upon a packsaddle, beneath him a Fadak cloak, mounting behind him Usamah ibn Zayd, visiting Sa'd ibn Ubadah among Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, before Badr's battle, until he passed a mixed مجلس of Muslims, idolatrous idolaters, and Jews, among them Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, in the مجلس Abdullah ibn Rawahah. When the mount's dust covered the مجلس, Abdullah ibn Ubayy veiled his nose with his cloak, then saying: Soil us not. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted them, then halted, alighting, calling them to Allah, reciting them the Qur'an. Abdullah ibn Ubayy said to him: O man, nothing is better than this, if what you say is true; so harm us not in our مجلس, returning to your رحل. Whoever of us comes to you, narrate to him. Ibn Rawahah said: Come to us in our gatherings, for we love that. The Muslims, idolaters, and Jews reviled one another until they nearly leapt at one another. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept calming them. Then he mounted his mount until he entered upon Sa'd ibn Ubadah, saying: O Sa'd, heard you not what Abu Hubab says, meaning Abdullah ibn Ubayy? He said such-and-such. Sa'd said: Pardon him, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and overlook. By Allah, Allah gave you what He gave you. This lake's people had agreed to crown him, meaning make him king, binding him with the fillet. When Allah, blessed and exalted, repelled that with the truth He gave you, he choked therewith. For that he did with you what you saw. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pardoned him."
"9566","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: No harm to drink while circumambulating the House. And al-Thawri mentioned it from him."
"9567","From a companion of his, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, he said: An elder of Al Wada'ah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank while circumambulating the House."
"9568","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: It was said to Muhammad ibn Ali: What is best you say in these days, Hajj's days or days? He said: There is no god but Allah, Allah is Greatest. He said: It is it."
"9569","From Ibn Uyaynah, a Meccan caller elder informed me, from Ali al-Azdi, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: There is no god but Allah, Allah is Greatest. He said: It is it. I said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what is it is it? He said: And He bound them to piety's word; they were worthier of it and its people."
"9570","Abdullah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar liked to circumambulate by night seven, by day five."
"9571","Ubaydullah ibn Umar reported from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar liked to end his tawaf on an odd number and used to say: Allah is Witr and loves the witr."
"9572","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayra saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Witr and loves the witr. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same; Ayyub said: Ibn Sirin used to like odd in everything, even eating an odd number."
"9573","From Ibn Jurayj, Ata said: three circuits are dearer to me than four. He then reported from Abu Hurayra that he heard him saying: Allah is Witr and loves the witr. Abu Hurayra counted: the heavens are odd among much odd. He said: whoever cleans his teeth should do so odd times, whoever cleans with stones should use odd, and when one rinses his mouth he should do so odd times. Ibn Jurayj said: Mujahid used to say about the saying of Allah, Blessed and Exalted, by the even and the odd: Allah is the Odd, and the even is every pair."
"9574","From Ibn Jurayj, Abu al-Zubayr reported that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: when one of you cleans with stones, let him do so an odd number of times."
"9575","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata being asked: are three circuits dearer to you or four? He would say: three. When asked: what about six? He said: if you wish you may do more, but three are dearer to me than four."
"9576","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: Aisha said: two sevens are better than one seven."
"9577","From Ibn Jurayj, that Amr ibn Dinar said: two are dearer to me than three."
"9578","Ma'mar said: one who heard Mujahid reported to me that he liked to finish on the odd of tawaf."
"9579","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Yunus, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: every seven is odd, and four are dearer to me than three."
"9580","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Sharik, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: whoever performs fifty circuits around the House will be like the day his mother gave birth to him."
"9581","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I doubted in tawaf, two or three? He said: complete upon me, secure that. I said: I and a man performed tawaf and differed. He said: you and him. I said: he refused. He said: do so, secure that in yourselves. I said: I performed tawaf and said: all that was with me. He said: begin a new seven afresh."
"9582","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I performed seven, then it occurred to me that I performed eight circuits. He said: perform another seven and make them six circuits."
"9583","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I performed seven and prayed, then it occurred to me that I performed six circuits. He said: perform another seven and make them eight circuits. Ata said: if you performed six circuits, perform one and pray two rak'as. Amr said the same."
"9584","From Ibn Jurayj, Abu Khalaf said: I was in the guard of Ibn al-Zubayr and he performed eight circuits until he reached amid the people the middle of the Stone, and he was told about that, so he completed with seven circuits. He said: tawaf is only odd."
"9585","From al-Aslami, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that Ali used to say about a man who performs tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa as three circuits: he should perform fourteen."
"9586","From al-Thawri, from Abu Bakr, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr being asked about a man who performs a hundred circuits between al-Safa and al-Marwa. He said: nothing is due on him."
"9587","From Ibn Jurayj, Ata reported that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr performed tawaf when Amr ibn Sa'id was governor over Mecca, and Amr went out for prayer, so Abd al-Rahman said to him: wait for me until I finish on an odd number. He finished on three circuits, then did not repeat that seven."
"9588","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mina reported to me that two men of Muzayna were evil men who had cut off the road and killed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by them, so they performed ablution and prayed, then pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we intended to come to you but Allah shortened our steps. He said: what are your names? They said: al-Muhanan (the humiliated). He said: rather you are al-Mukraman (the honored)."
"9589","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Ubada ibn al-Samit, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took allegiance from a group, and I was among them, and he recited to them the verse of women: that they associate nothing with Allah. Then he said: whoever fulfills, his reward is with Allah; whoever commits something of that and is punished for it in this world, it is purification and expiation for him; and whoever commits something of that and Allah conceals it for him, his affair is with Allah, if He wills He forgives him, and if He wills He punishes him."
"9590","Al-Thawri and Ibn Uyayna reported from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa, he said: I heard Jarir ibn Abdillah saying: I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my hand, and he stipulated upon me sincerity to every Muslim. So I am sincere to you."
"9591","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abdullah ibn Uthman reported to me that Muhammad ibn al-Aswad ibn Khalaf told him that his father al-Aswad saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledging allegiance to the people on the day of the Conquest. He said: he sat at Qarn Musqala, and Qarn Musqala is where the houses of Ibn Abi Umama drain toward, and it is the house of Ibn Samura and what is around it; what flows behind it is toward the house of Ibn Amir, and what faces from it is toward the house of Ibn Samura and around it. Al-Aswad said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sit there, and people came to him, young and old and women, and pledged allegiance to him upon Islam and the testimony. I said: what is the testimony? He said: Muhammad ibn al-Aswad reported to me that he pledged them upon faith in Allah and testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger."
"9592","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Jarir that when he pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he took upon him that he associate nothing with Allah, establish prayer, give zakat, be sincere to the Muslim, and separate from the polytheist."
"9593","Al-Thawri reported from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pledge allegiance from us upon hearing and obeying, then he would dictate to us: as much as you are able."
"9594","Al-Thawri reported from Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: when the people pledged allegiance to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, Ibn Umar wrote to him: to proceed, I affirm hearing and obedience to Abdullah Abd al-Malik, Commander of the Faithful, upon the sunna of Allah and the sunna of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as much as I am able, and my sons have affirmed the like of that. Peace."
"9595","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take from whoever entered Islam, saying: you establish prayer, give zakat, perform hajj of the House, fast Ramadan, and that you do not see the fire of a polytheist except while you are at war with him."
"9596","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pledge allegiance to people by speech with this verse: that they associate nothing with Allah. And the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never touched the hand of a woman except the hand of a woman he owned."
"9597","Al-Thawri reported from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Umayma bint Ruqayqa, she said: I came with women to pledge allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he stipulated upon us that we not commit adultery nor steal, and this verse. She said: so we pledged allegiance to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stipulated upon us, he said: as much as you are able and can bear. She said: so we said: Allah and His Messenger are more merciful to us than ourselves. She said: so we said: will you not shake hands with us, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I do not shake hands with women; my word to one woman is like my word to a hundred women."
"9598","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: Fatima bint Utba ibn Rabi'a came to pledge allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he took upon her that she associate nothing with Allah, the verse. She said: so she placed her hand on her head out of shyness, and what he saw of her pleased the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Aisha said: affirm, O woman, for by Allah he pledged allegiance from us only upon this. She said: yes then. So he pledged allegiance from her upon the verse."
"9599","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make them swear that they only went out out of desire for Islam and love for Allah and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"9600","From Ma'mar, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took upon women when he pledged them that they not wail. They said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), women helped us in wailing in the pre-Islamic time, shall we help them in Islam? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: there is no helping in wailing in Islam."
"9601","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took upon women when he pledged them that they not wail, nor be alone in speech with men."
"9602","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take upon them, saying: I do not shake hands with women."
"9603","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to shake hands with women with a garment on his hand."
"9604","Al-Thawri reported from al-Agharr, from Khalifa ibn Husayn, from his grandfather Qays ibn Asim, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intending Islam, and I became Muslim, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to wash with water and lote leaves, so I washed with water and lote leaves."
"9605","Ubaydullah and Abdullah sons of Umar reported from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayra, that Thumama al-Hanafi was captured, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to him in the morning saying: what do you have, O Thumama? He would say: if you kill, you kill one with blood, and if you bestow favor you bestow upon one grateful, and if you want wealth we will give of it what you wish. The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked ransom and said: what shall we do by killing this one? One day the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him and he became Muslim, so he freed him and sent him to the garden of Abu Talha and ordered him to wash, so he washed and prayed two rak'as. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the Islam of your brother has become good."
"9606","Ibn Jurayj reported: I was told from Uthaym ibn Kalb, from his father, from his grandfather that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have become Muslim. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: cast off from you the hair of disbelief, and circumcise, meaning: shave."
"9607","Another reported to me from him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to another: cast off from you the hair of disbelief and circumcise."
"9608","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard him saying about one who becomes Muslim: he is ordered to wash."
"9609","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: when you meet the polytheists on a road, do not initiate greeting with them, and force them to its narrowest part."
"9610","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from Humayd al-Azraq, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: we were ordered not to add for the People of the Book beyond: and upon you."
"9611","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: a group of Jews entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: death upon you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: upon you. Aisha said: I understood it and said: upon you death and curse. She said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: gently, O Aisha, Allah loves gentleness in all matters. She said: O Messenger of Allah, did you not hear what they said? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I already said: upon you."
"9612","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: when the Jews greet you, they say: death upon you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: say: and upon you."
"9613","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, he said: greeting the People of the Book when you enter their houses is: peace upon whoever follows guidance."
"9614","Ma'mar reported from one who heard al-Hasan saying: when you pass a gathering in which there are Muslims and disbelievers, greet them."
"9615","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, that he was with Abdullah on a journey, and some People of the Book accompanied him, and when they parted, he said: where are you going? They said: here. So he followed them and greeted them."
"9616","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, that Usama ibn Zayd told him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed a gathering with a mix of Muslims, Jews and polytheists, and greeted them."
"9617","Al-Thawri reported from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab, from Abu Burda, he said: a polytheist man wrote to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and wrote at the bottom of the letter greeting him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that greeting be returned to him."
"9618","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Heraclius: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah to Heraclius the chief of Rome, peace upon whoever follows guidance."
"9619","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, he said: I asked Ibrahim and Mujahid, he said: how do I write to the dihqan? Ibrahim said: write: peace upon you. Mujahid said: write: peace upon whoever follows guidance."
"9620","Al-Thawri reported from Ammar al-Duhni, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, that he wrote to a man of the dihqans greeting him, and he said to him: you lied in that; Allah is Peace."
"9621","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, that when he sought permission from polytheists he used to say: indarayim, meaning: may I enter?"
"9622","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sinan, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: polytheists are not admitted to me except by permission."
"9623","Ma'mar and al-Awza'i reported from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usama ibn Zayd, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, where will you stay tomorrow? That was in the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: has Aqil ibn Abi Talib left us any house? Then he said: a Muslim does not inherit a disbeliever, nor a disbeliever a Muslim. Then he said: tomorrow we shall stay at Khayf Bani Kinana, where Quraysh covenanted upon disbelief, meaning al-Abtah. Al-Zuhri said: al-khayf is the valley. He said: that is because Quraysh allied with Banu Bakr against Banu Hashim that they would not sit with them, nor marry them, nor trade with them, nor shelter them."
"9624","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usama ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a Muslim does not inherit a disbeliever, nor does a disbeliever inherit a Muslim."
"9625","Malik reported from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn Husayn, that Abu Talib was inherited by Aqil and Talib, and Ali inherited nothing of him. He said: for that reason we left our share of the quarter."
"9626","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn that Abu Talib was inherited by Aqil and Talib, and Ali and Ja'far did not inherit him, because they were Muslims. Amr said the same."
"9627","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: a Muslim does not inherit a disbeliever, nor a disbeliever a Muslim."
"9628","Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Umar, he said: the people of shirk, we do not inherit them, nor do they inherit us."
"9629","Ibn Jurayj reported: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: people of two different religions do not inherit from one another. He said: and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that Muslims and Christians do not inherit from one another, and Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman likewise."
"9630","Ibn Jurayj reported: Maymun ibn Mihran reported to me, from a man of Kinda called al-Irs, an old shaykh who was employed over al-Jazira, he told me that al-Ash'ath ibn Qays told him: a Jewish paternal aunt of his died, so he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab seeking her inheritance, but Umar refused to give it to him, and the Jews inherited her."
"9631","Ibn Jurayj reported: Yahya ibn Sa'id reported to us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mentioning that Muhammad ibn al-Ash'ath told him that a Jewish paternal aunt of his died in Yemen, and that al-Ash'ath ibn Qays mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so Umar said: none inherits her except people of her religion. Al-Thawri reported from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab the same."
"9632","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: a Jew does not inherit a Christian, nor a Christian a Jew. Another used to say: Islam is one religion, and shirk is one religion."
"9633","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, that al-Ash'ath ibn Qays said: O Commander of the Faithful, my sister was under a Miqwal of the Miqwals and he made her Jewish, and she died, so who inherits her? Umar said: people of her religion."
"9634","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: people of two different religions do not inherit from one another."
"9635","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba or another, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: people of religions do not inherit from one another, nor do they inherit us."
"9636","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr reported to me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah saying: a Muslim does not inherit a Jew nor a Christian, nor do they inherit him, except if he is a slave of a man or his slave woman."
"9637","Al-Thawri and Malik reported from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz freed a Christian slave boy of his, then he died, so he ordered me to place his inheritance in the public treasury. From Ma'mar, from a man, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz the same."
"9638","Ma'mar reported: one who heard Ikrima reported to me, and he was asked about a man who freed a Christian slave of his, then the slave died leaving wealth. He said: his inheritance is for people of his religion."
"9639","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: it was narrated from Makhul, he said: if a Christian slave of yours dies and you find for him gold in coin as price of wine, take it; but if you find wine and pigs, do not. He said: another said that."
"9640","Ibn Jurayj reported from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): a Muslim does not inherit a disbeliever while he has a near relative of his religion; if he has no inheriting relative, then one of the Muslims inherits him through Islam. Al-Thawri said about a Christian who frees a Muslim slave of his: his inheritance goes to the public treasury."
"9641","From al-Thawri, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Sha'bi, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: people of two different religions do not inherit from one another."
"9642","Ibn al-Mubarak reported from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, he said: Abdullah ibn Mawhab narrated to me, from Tamim al-Dari, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: whoever becomes Muslim at the hands of a man, he is his client. Ibn al-Mubarak said: and he inherits him if he has no heir. I mentioned it to al-Thawri, and he said: he inherits him, he is more deserving than another."
"9643","Al-Thawri and Ma'mar reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, about a man who allies with a man and becomes Muslim at his hands. He said: he pays blood money for him and inherits him. Ibn Uyayna reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim the same and added: and he may transfer his clientage wherever he wishes so long as he has not paid blood money for him."
"9644","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, and from Yunus, from al-Hasan, they said: his inheritance is for the Muslims."
"9645","Ibrahim ibn Umar reported: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq told me about a man who came from the people of shirk and became Muslim and allied with a man. He said: his clientage and inheritance are his, and he may not ally with another."
"9646","Ma'mar reported from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib that he used to like that the Nabateans be sent out for jizya."
"9647","Ma'mar reported from Qatada about His saying: and if you fear poverty, Allah will enrich you from His bounty. He said: Allah enriched them with the ongoing jizya month by month and year by year."
"9648","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: for them in this world is disgrace. He said: they give jizya willingly while humbled."
"9649","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: and when your Lord announced He would send upon them till the Day of Resurrection one who inflicts evil torment. He said: He sends upon them the living of the Arabs, and they are in torment from them till the Day of Resurrection."
"9650","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, he said: no Jew nor Christian is forced to Islam if they give jizya."
"9651","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: and if you return, We return. They returned, so Allah sent upon them Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they give jizya willingly while humbled."
"9652","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: so pardon them and overlook. He said: it was abrogated by: fight those who do not believe in Allah nor the Last Day nor forbid what Allah and His Messenger forbade nor practice the true religion from those given the Book until they give jizya willingly while humbled."
"9653","Al-Thawri reported that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz took jizya from the freedmen of the Muslims among Jews and Christians."
"9654","Al-Thawri reported from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: no jizya is upon them; their covenant is the covenant of the Muslims."
"9655","Al-Thawri reported from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghafla, he said: it reached Umar ibn al-Khattab that his workers were taking jizya from wine, so he adjured them three times. Bilal said: they do that. He said: do not do so, but entrust them with selling it; for fat was forbidden for the Jews, so they sold it and ate its price."
"9656","Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: when dhimmis pass with wine, the tithe collector takes a tenth from it; he values it then takes a tenth of its value."
"9657","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata said to me: if a Muslim dies leaving a Christian son, and his inheritance was not divided until his Christian son became Muslim, he has no right, for the inheritance fell before he became Muslim. The like of that is for a slave whose free father dies and his inheritance is not divided until he is freed."
"9658","Al-Thawri reported from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: whoever becomes Muslim over an inheritance and had not become Muslim has no right to it, because inheritances fell before he became Muslim, and the slave is in that position."
"9659","Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj reported from al-Zuhri, he said: when inheritances have fallen, whoever becomes Muslim over an inheritance has nothing of it."
"9660","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said to me in the like of that the saying of Ata. He said: and so he says. He said: and Muhammad also said to me: about a Jewish household whose father died and his inheritance was not divided until they became Muslim, it is not upon the division of Islam; inheritances fell before they became Muslim."
"9661","Ibn Jurayj reported: Amr ibn Dinar reported to me, he said: I heard Abu al-Mundhir saying: if a Muslim dies leaving a Muslim son and a disbelieving son, and his inheritance was not divided until the disbeliever became Muslim, he inherits with the believers, and they both inherit. It did not please me what he said, and one said to me: that is the inheritance of pre-Islamic time; whatever Islam reaches and was not divided is upon the division of Islam. Ibn Jurayj said: and I say: no, inheritances fell in Islam, and another said that."
"9662","Ma'mar reported: Ibn Tawus reported to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, and Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, they said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: whatever was upon the division of pre-Islamic time is upon the division of pre-Islamic time, and whatever Islam reached undivided is upon the division of Islam."
"9663","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: he wrote to him: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, to proceed, you wrote to me to ask Yazid ibn Qatada about what you ordered me, and I asked him, and he said: my Christian mother died while I was Muslim, and she left thirty slaves and slave girls and two hundred palms. So we rode about that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar judged that her inheritance is for her husband and her nephew, both Christians, and he did not give me anything. Yazid ibn Qatada said: then my grandfather died while Muslim; he had pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and attended Hunayn with him, and left his daughter. So we rode about that to Uthman, I and her nephew, and her daughter was Christian, so Uthman gave me all his wealth and did not give his daughter anything, so I held it a year or two, then his daughter became Muslim. So we rode to Uthman, and he asked Abdullah ibn al-Arqam, and he said to him: Umar used to judge that whoever becomes Muslim over an inheritance before it is divided, his inheritance is due by his Islam. So Uthman gave her all that, and I am witness."
"9664","Ibn Uyayna reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, he said: when a man dies leaving his son a slave or a Christian, and he is freed, if the inheritance was not divided it is his, meaning: he inherits."
"9665","From Ibn Uyayna, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: when a man dies leaving his son a slave, and he is freed before the inheritance is divided, he has nothing."
"9666","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: when the seeker of inheritance becomes Muslim after the death of the owner of inheritance, he has nothing of it."
"9667","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me, and I asked him, and he said: if there are two Christians and their father became Muslim, and they have small children, then their children die leaving wealth, their Muslim father does not inherit them, but their mother inherits them, and what remains is for people of their religion. I said: they are small, they have no religion. He said: but they were born in Christianity upon Christianity. He once said to me: the Muslim inherits his inheritance from their father. I know only that he used to say: their small child inherits them and they inherit him until one religion joins them or separates. I mentioned it to Amr ibn Dinar. I said: his parents are Christians. He said: I would give what is theirs to their child. I said to Amr: how, while the child is upon fitra? He said: why then are the children of polytheists taken captive? Though upon fitra, yet they are Muslims."
"9668","From Ma'mar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, and Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: about two Christians with a small child between them, and one of them became Muslim. He said: the Muslim is more entitled to him; they inherit him and he inherits them."
"9669","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: they both inherit him and he inherits them."
"9670","Ibn Jurayj reported: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa informing Ata, he said: the matter in what passed among our predecessors, which is acted upon and not doubted, and we are upon it now, is that two Christians with their small child between them inherit him and he inherits them until religion separates them or joins. If his mother becomes Muslim, she inherits him by the Book of Allah, and what remains is for the Muslims. If his father is Christian, and he is small, and he has a Muslim brother from his mother or a Muslim sister, his brother or sister inherits him by the Book of Allah, then what remains is for the Muslims. He said: funeral prayer is not offered over children of Christians, nor do we console him for them, nor follow them to their graves, but they bury them in their graveyard. He said: if a Muslim kills one of their children deliberately, he is not killed for him, and his blood money is the blood money of a Christian. I said to Sulayman: a small child between two polytheists, and one of them became Muslim, and their child is small, then his father dies. He said: their Muslim child inherits from his parents, and the disbeliever of them does not inherit; inheritance then is between the Muslim and the child, and the child then does not inherit the disbelieving of his parents."
"9671","Al-Thawri reported from Ismail, from al-Hasan, from Umar ibn al-Khattab about two Christians with a small child between them, and one of them became Muslim. He said: the Muslim is more entitled to him. Al-Thawri reported from Yunus, from al-Hasan the same."
"9672","Ibn Jurayj reported: I and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said: if a Magian marries his daughter and she bears him two daughters, then he dies, then they become Muslim, then one of his daughters dies, her full sister gets half, and her mother gets a sixth; she veiled herself because she is the sister of her daughter, and her remaining daughter, sister of her daughter, veiled her. Then the mother also gets what the paternal sister gets. Al-Thawri said the like of their saying: her full sister gets half, and the sister from father and mother gets a sixth completing two-thirds, and she also gets a sixth because she is a mother; she veiled herself, and because she is a sister, so she gets a third. Al-Thawri said: this is the saying of Ibrahim: they inherit from two places."
"9673","From al-Thawri, about a Christian who dies leaving his wife pregnant, then she becomes Muslim before giving birth, then gives birth and dies. He said: their child inherits them both, because the inheritance of his father fell to him when his father died, then his mother died and he followed her upon her religion, so he inherited her."
"9674","Al-Thawri reported from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Sha'bi, that Ali ibn Abi Talib and Ibn Mas'ud said: about the Magian, he inherits from two places."
"9675","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ibrahim, he said: he inherits from two places."
"9676","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: about the Magian, we give them inheritance by the nearest kinship to him."
"9677","From al-Thawri, about a Magian who marries his sister and she bears him a daughter, then they become Muslim, then he dies. He said: his daughter inherits half, and half is for his sister because she is agnate. He said: about a Magian who marries his mother and she bears him two daughters, then they become Muslim, then the man dies: his two daughters get two-thirds, and his mother gets a sixth. Then one of the daughters dies: her sister inherits half, and the mother became mother and grandmother, so she veiled herself, and we gave her the inheritance of a mother, and did not give her the inheritance of a grandmother. He says: when they became Muslim, marriage was annulled for him, so he should not remain after Islam upon his mother nor his sister, and we gave them inheritance by kinship."
"9678","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Sadiq or another, that Ali used to give the Magian inheritance from two places, meaning: when he marries his sister or his mother."
"9679","Al-Thawri and Ma'mar reported from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: whoever steals wine from the People of the Book has his hand cut."
"9680","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: whoever steals wine from the People of the Book has his hand cut. Al-Thawri said: there is no cutting upon one who steals wine from the People of the Book, but he is fined its price."
